const SUBJECTS_DATA = {"dbms": {"code": "BCA-501", "paperCode": "18021", "title": "Introduction to DBMS", "desc": "Complete Deduplicated Question Bank covering Relational Algebra, SQL Queries, Normalization (1NF-BCNF), 2PL, Recovery & ER Modeling.", "icon": "fa-database", "color": "blue", "pdfBank": "pdfs/BCA501_DBMS.pdf", "years": [{"year": "2025", "pdf": "pdfs/dbms_2025.pdf"}, {"year": "2024", "pdf": "pdfs/dbms_2024.pdf"}, {"year": "2023", "pdf": "pdfs/dbms_2023.pdf"}, {"year": "2021", "pdf": "pdfs/dbms_2021.pdf"}, {"year": "2020", "pdf": "pdfs/dbms_2020.pdf"}, {"year": "2019", "pdf": "pdfs/dbms_2019.pdf"}, {"year": "2018", "pdf": "pdfs/dbms_2018.pdf"}, {"year": "2017", "pdf": "pdfs/dbms_2017.pdf"}, {"year": "2016", "pdf": "pdfs/dbms_2016.pdf"}, {"year": "2015", "pdf": "pdfs/dbms_2015.pdf"}, {"year": "2014", "pdf": "pdfs/dbms_2014.pdf"}, {"year": "2013", "pdf": "pdfs/dbms_2013.pdf"}], "contentHtml": "<div class=\"sec-title\" data-sec=\"sec-c\">Section – C (Detailed / Long Answer Questions &bull; 15 Marks &bull; 38 Questions)</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2024, 2020, 2019, 2017, 2016, 2015, 2014, 2013 &bull; Repeated 9&times;</span>\n        Q1. What is Relational Algebra? Discuss its fundamental operations (Select, Project, Union, Set Difference, Cartesian Product, Rename) and derived Join operation.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> <strong>Relational Algebra</strong> is a formal procedural query language that takes one or two relations as input and produces a new relation as output without modifying the underlying stored tables.<br><br>\n        <strong>Fundamental Operators:</strong>\n        <ol>\n            <li><strong>Select (&sigma;):</strong> Unary operator that filters rows satisfying a predicate condition <span class=\"math-expr\">P</span>.\n                <pre class=\"diagram-box\">&sigma;<sub>Salary > 50000 AND Dept = 'IT'</sub> (Employee)</pre>\n            </li>\n            <li><strong>Project (&pi;):</strong> Unary operator that selects specified columns and eliminates duplicate tuples.\n                <pre class=\"diagram-box\">&pi;<sub>EmpName, Salary</sub> (Employee)</pre>\n            </li>\n            <li><strong>Union (&cup;):</strong> Binary operator combining tuples from two union-compatible relations.\n                <pre class=\"diagram-box\">Student_CS &cup; Student_IT</pre>\n            </li>\n            <li><strong>Set Difference (&minus;):</strong> Returns tuples present in relation R but not in S (union compatible).\n                <pre class=\"diagram-box\">All_Students &minus; Placed_Students</pre>\n            </li>\n            <li><strong>Cartesian Product (&times;):</strong> Cross product pairing every tuple of R with every tuple of S.\n                <pre class=\"diagram-box\">Employee &times; Department</pre>\n            </li>\n            <li><strong>Rename (&rho;):</strong> Renames relation or attributes: <span class=\"math-expr\">&rho;<sub>S(A<sub>1</sub>, A<sub>2</sub>)</sub> (R)</span>.</li>\n        </ol>\n        <strong>Derived Operation &mdash; Natural Join (&bowtie;):</strong>\n        Combines tuples of two relations matching on identical common attribute names:\n        <div class=\"diagram-box\">Employee &bowtie; Department = &pi;<sub>...</sub> (&sigma;<sub>Employee.DeptID = Department.DeptID</sub> (Employee &times; Department))\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2024, 2021, 2017, 2015, 2014 &bull; Repeated 6&times;</span>\n        Q2. What is Normalization? Explain 1NF, 2NF, 3NF, and BCNF with suitable relational examples and anomaly reduction.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 22.5M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> <strong>Normalization</strong> is a formal process of decomposing relations with anomalies into structured relations to eliminate insertion, deletion, and update anomalies while avoiding redundant data storage.<br><br>\n        <strong>1. First Normal Form (1NF):</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>A relation is in 1NF if and only if all attribute values are atomic (no multivalued, composite, or nested attributes).</li>\n            <li><em>Violation:</em> <code>Employee(EmpID, Skills)</code> where <code>Skills = \"Java, Python, C++\"</code>.</li>\n            <li><em>Decomposition:</em> Split into atomic rows: <code>(101, 'Java')</code>, <code>(101, 'Python')</code>, <code>(101, 'C++')</code>.</li>\n        </ul>\n        <strong>2. Second Normal Form (2NF):</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Relation must be in 1NF and have <strong>no partial dependency</strong> (no non-prime attribute should depend on a proper subset of any candidate key).</li>\n            <li><em>Example:</em> <code>Enrollment(<u>StudentID</u>, <u>CourseID</u>, CourseFee, Grade)</code>. Here <code>CourseFee</code> depends only on <code>CourseID</code> (partial).</li>\n            <li><em>Decomposition:</em> <code>StudentCourses(<u>StudentID</u>, <u>CourseID</u>, Grade)</code> and <code>CourseDetails(<u>CourseID</u>, CourseFee)</code>.</li>\n        </ul>\n        <strong>3. Third Normal Form (3NF):</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Relation must be in 2NF and have <strong>no transitive dependency</strong> (for every non-trivial FD <span class=\"math-expr\">X &rarr; Y</span>, either <span class=\"math-expr\">X</span> is a super key or <span class=\"math-expr\">Y</span> is a prime attribute).</li>\n            <li><em>Example:</em> <code>Employee(<u>EmpID</u>, EmpName, DeptNo, DeptHead)</code>. Here <code>EmpID &rarr; DeptNo</code> and <code>DeptNo &rarr; DeptHead</code>, creating transitive dependency.</li>\n            <li><em>Decomposition:</em> <code>Emp(<u>EmpID</u>, EmpName, DeptNo)</code> and <code>Dept(<u>DeptNo</u>, DeptHead)</code>.</li>\n        </ul>\n        <strong>4. Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF):</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>A stricter version of 3NF where for every non-trivial FD <span class=\"math-expr\">X &rarr; Y</span>, determinant <span class=\"math-expr\">X</span> <strong>must strictly be a Super Key</strong>.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2024, 2020, 2019, 2015 &bull; Repeated 5&times;</span>\n        Q3. Differentiate between Schema and Instance in DBMS.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Comparison:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Database Schema:</strong> The overall logical blueprint, structure, and design of the database (e.g., table names, column data types, constraints). It is specified during system design and changes very infrequently.</li>\n            <li><strong>Database Instance (State):</strong> The actual data content populated within the database at a specific moment in time. It changes dynamically whenever records are inserted, updated, or deleted.</li>\n        </ul>\n        <em>Analogy:</em> In programming languages, a Schema corresponds to a Class/Data Type, whereas an Instance corresponds to an Object/Value of that type.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2020, 2015, 2014, 2013 &bull; Repeated 5&times;</span>\n        Q4. Draw an ER Diagram for a Hospital Management System specifying entities, attributes, primary keys, relationships, and cardinalities. Show conversion to Relational Schema.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>1. Entity Sets & Attributes:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>DOCTOR</strong>: <u>DocID</u>, Name, Specialty, Phone</li>\n            <li><strong>PATIENT</strong>: <u>PatID</u>, Name, Age, Gender, Address</li>\n            <li><strong>ROOM / BED</strong>: <u>RoomNo</u>, <u>BedNo</u>, RoomType, DailyCharge</li>\n            <li><strong>BILL / ACCOUNT</strong>: <u>BillID</u>, BillDate, TotalAmount, PaymentStatus</li>\n        </ul>\n        <strong>2. Relationships & Cardinalities:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><code>TREATS</code>: Many-to-Many between DOCTOR and PATIENT with attribute <code>DateOfTreatment</code>.</li>\n            <li><code>ASSIGNED_TO</code>: Many-to-One between PATIENT and BED (A patient is assigned to one bed; a bed holds one patient at a time).</li>\n            <li><code>GENERATES</code>: One-to-One between PATIENT and BILL.</li>\n        </ul>\n        <strong>3. ASCII ER Diagram Representation:</strong>\n        <pre class=\"diagram-box\">\n  +------------+             ( M )             +------------+\n  |   DOCTOR   | <---------------------------> |  PATIENT   |\n  | <u>DocID</u>      |          < TREATS >           | <u>PatID</u>      |\n  | Name, Spec |        [Date, Notes]          | Name, Age  |\n  +------------+                               +------------+\n                                                    | (1)\n                                             < ASSIGNED >\n                                                    | (1)\n                                                    v\n                                               +------------+\n                                               |  ROOM_BED  |\n                                               | <u>RoomNo</u>     |\n                                               | <u>BedNo</u>, Type|\n                                               +------------+\n        </pre>\n        <strong>4. Relational Schema Reduction:</strong>\n<pre>\n1. Doctor(<u>DocID</u>, Name, Specialty, Phone)\n2. Patient(<u>PatID</u>, Name, Age, Gender, Address, RoomNo, BedNo)\n   FOREIGN KEY (RoomNo, BedNo) REFERENCES Room_Bed(RoomNo, BedNo)\n3. Room_Bed(<u>RoomNo</u>, <u>BedNo</u>, RoomType, DailyCharge)\n4. Treats(<u>DocID</u>, <u>PatID</u>, <u>TreatmentDate</u>, Prescription)\n   FOREIGN KEY (DocID) REFERENCES Doctor(DocID),\n   FOREIGN KEY (PatID) REFERENCES Patient(PatID)\n5. Bill(<u>BillID</u>, PatID, BillDate, TotalAmount, Status)\n   FOREIGN KEY (PatID) REFERENCES Patient(PatID)\n</pre>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2016, 2015, 2013 &bull; Repeated 4&times;</span>\n        Q5. Explain the ACID properties of a transaction with practical examples. Why are they essential?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> A <strong>Transaction</strong> is a logical unit of database processing that includes one or more database access operations. To preserve database consistency and reliability, every DBMS must guarantee the four <strong>ACID Properties</strong>:<br><br>\n        <strong>1. Atomicity (\"All or Nothing\"):</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Either all operations of the transaction are committed successfully, or none take effect. If failure occurs mid-transaction, all changes are rolled back.</li>\n            <li><em>Example:</em> Bank transfer of \\<span class=\"math-expr\">500 from Account A to Account B. If \\</span>500 is debited from A and power fails before crediting B, the recovery subsystem rolls back A's debit.</li>\n        </ul>\n        <strong>2. Consistency (Preservation of Invariants):</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Execution of a transaction in isolation preserves database integrity constraints (e.g., total balance of A + B must remain unchanged before and after the transfer).</li>\n        </ul>\n        <strong>3. Isolation (Independence of Concurrent Executions):</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Even if multiple transactions execute concurrently, the intermediate uncommitted state of one transaction is hidden from others, producing a serializable execution schedule. Enforced via locking (2PL) and timestamp ordering.</li>\n        </ul>\n        <strong>4. Durability (Survivability of Committed Data):</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Once a transaction commits, its updates persist permanently in secondary storage and will survive subsequent power failures, crashes, or OS reboots via Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) and checkpointing.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021, 2018, 2014, 2013 &bull; Repeated 4&times;</span>\n        Q6. What are the pitfalls of Lock-Based Protocols in concurrency control?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Pitfalls:</strong>\n        <ol>\n            <li><strong>Deadlock:</strong> Occurs when two or more transactions are waiting indefinitely for locks held by each other (e.g., <span class=\"math-expr\">T<sub>1</sub></span> holds Lock on A and waits for B, while <span class=\"math-expr\">T<sub>2</sub></span> holds Lock on B and waits for A). Requires deadlock detection (Wait-For Graph) and victim rollback.</li>\n            <li><strong>Starvation:</strong> A transaction waits indefinitely because other higher-priority transactions repeatedly acquire locks on the required data item.</li>\n            <li><strong>Cascading Rollback:</strong> In non-strict 2PL, if a transaction aborts, all dependent uncommitted transactions that read its dirty data must also be rolled back in a cascade.</li>\n            <li><strong>Concurrency Bottleneck:</strong> Excessive long-duration exclusive locks reduce system throughput and degrade response times.</li>\n        </ol>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2018, 2016, 2014, 2013 &bull; Repeated 4&times;</span>\n        Q7. Explain Indexed Sequential, B-Tree, and B+ Tree file organizations with examples.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Comparison of File Organizations:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Indexed Sequential (ISAM):</strong> Records stored in sequential key order with a multilevel static index. Good for sequential and direct access, but degrades with many overflow records.</li>\n            <li><strong>B-Tree:</strong> Self-balancing dynamic multi-way search tree. Keys and data record pointers reside in both internal nodes and leaf nodes.</li>\n            <li><strong>B+ Tree:</strong> Modern standard for relational DBMS storage engines. Internal nodes store only search keys to guide navigation, while <strong>all actual data records/pointers reside in leaf nodes</strong> connected via a doubly linked list, enabling superior range scans.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2024, 2023 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q8. Who are Data Administrators (DA)? What are the functions of a Database Administrator (DBA)? Differentiate between DA and DBA.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Data Administrator (DA) vs Database Administrator (DBA):</strong>\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Data Administrator (DA)</th><th>Database Administrator (DBA)</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n                <tr><td>Role Level</td><td>Managerial and business level</td><td>Technical and operational level</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Focus</td><td>Business data policies, enterprise data dictionary, data governance.</td><td>Physical database design, database server installation, query tuning, security.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Scope</td><td>Decides <em>what data</em> needs to be collected across the enterprise.</td><td>Decides <em>how data is stored, indexed, secured, and recovered</em>.</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n        </table>\n        <strong>Key Functions of DBA:</strong>\n        <ol>\n            <li>Hardware/software installation and database upgrades.</li>\n            <li>Creating database users, granting roles, and monitoring access privileges.</li>\n            <li>Performance tuning: Analyzing execution plans, optimizing SQL queries, re-indexing.</li>\n            <li>Automating database backups and executing disaster recovery drills.</li>\n        </ol>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2023, 2017 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q9. Differentiate between DDL and DML commands with syntax and examples.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Comparison:</strong>\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Data Definition Language (DDL)</th><th>Data Manipulation Language (DML)</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n                <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Defines and modifies the schema structure of the database.</td><td>Manages, accesses, and manipulates the data within the tables.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Commit Nature</td><td>Auto-committed permanently (cannot be rolled back in most RDBMS).</td><td>Transactional (can be committed or rolled back using TCL).</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Commands</td><td><code>CREATE</code>, <code>ALTER</code>, <code>DROP</code>, <code>TRUNCATE</code>, <code>RENAME</code></td><td><code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code>, <code>SELECT</code></td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n        </table>\n        <strong>SQL Examples:</strong>\n<pre>\n-- DDL Commands:\nCREATE TABLE Student (RollNo INT PRIMARY KEY, Name VARCHAR(50));\nALTER TABLE Student ADD COLUMN City VARCHAR(30);\n\n-- DML Commands:\nINSERT INTO Student (RollNo, Name, City) VALUES (101, 'Aman', 'Meerut');\nUPDATE Student SET City = 'Delhi' WHERE RollNo = 101;\n</pre>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023, 2018, 2015 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q10. Explain Data Independence with its types: Physical and Logical Data Independence. Why is it significant?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> <strong>Data Independence</strong> is the ability to modify schema definitions at one level of the 3-level ANSI/SPARC architecture without altering schemas at the higher level.<br><br>\n        <strong>1. Logical Data Independence:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>The ability to modify the <strong>Conceptual Schema</strong> (e.g., adding/deleting attributes, splitting tables) without having to rewrite external schemas or existing application programs.</li>\n            <li><em>How achieved:</em> Using SQL Views that present the original logical schema to old applications.</li>\n            <li><em>Difficulty:</em> Harder to achieve because applications are closely tied to logical data models.</li>\n        </ul>\n        <strong>2. Physical Data Independence:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>The ability to modify the <strong>Internal/Physical Schema</strong> (e.g., changing file organizations, building B+ Tree indexes, moving to new storage media) without changing the Conceptual Schema.</li>\n            <li><em>Ease:</em> Easier to achieve because logical structure remains completely decoupled from disk-level storage.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2017, 2016, 2015 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q11. Explain BCNF (Boyce-Codd Normal Form) with example.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)</b> is an advanced, stricter version of Third Normal Form (3NF), specifically developed by Raymond Boyce and Edgar Codd to resolve anomalies arising from overlapping composite candidate keys.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Formal Definition:</b> A relation <i>R</i> is in BCNF if and only if for every non-trivial functional dependency <code>X &rarr; Y</code> holding on <i>R</i>: <b>X is a Super Key of R</b>.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Comparative Analysis: 3NF vs BCNF</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Criteria</th><th>Third Normal Form (3NF)</th><th>Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Condition for X &rarr; Y</b></td><td>Valid if X is Super Key <b>OR</b> Y is a prime attribute.</td><td>Valid <b>ONLY</b> if X is a Super Key (no prime relaxation).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Redundancy</b></td><td>May permit slight redundancy if overlapping candidate keys exist.</td><td>Completely eliminates all redundancy resulting from functional dependencies.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Dependency Preservation</b></td><td><b>Always preserves</b> all functional dependencies.</td><td>May occasionally <b>not preserve</b> all FDs upon decomposition.</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Classic Example Violating BCNF & Resolution:</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p>Consider relation: <code>StudentAdvisor(StudentID, Subject, Advisor)</code> with constraints:</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li>A student can have multiple advisors for different subjects: <code>{StudentID, Subject} &rarr; Advisor</code></li>\n        <li>Each advisor specializes in only one subject: <code>Advisor &rarr; Subject</code></li>\n        <li>Candidate Keys: <code>{StudentID, Subject}</code> and <code>{StudentID, Advisor}</code>. Prime Attributes: all 3.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p><b>Check 3NF:</b> For <code>Advisor &rarr; Subject</code>, Advisor is not super key, but Subject is prime attribute. Hence table is in <b>3NF</b> &check;.</p>\n    <p><b>Check BCNF:</b> Advisor is <b>NOT</b> a super key. Hence violates <b>BCNF</b> &cross;.</p>\n    <p><b>BCNF Decomposition:</b> Split into two relations:</p>\n    <ol>\n        <li><code>AdvisorSubject(<u>Advisor</u>, Subject)</code> — Advisor is primary key &check;</li>\n        <li><code>StudentAdvisor(<u>StudentID</u>, <u>Advisor</u>)</code> — Both form composite primary key &check;</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021, 2020 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q12. Explain Two-Phase Locking (2PL) Protocol. Compare Strict 2PL and Rigorous 2PL with serializability guarantees.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 22.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Two-Phase Locking (2PL):</strong> A concurrency control protocol guaranteeing conflict serializability by dividing a transaction into two distinct phases:\n        <ol>\n            <li><strong>Growing Phase:</strong> Transaction may obtain locks (Shared or Exclusive) but cannot release any lock.</li>\n            <li><strong>Shrinking Phase:</strong> Transaction may release locks but cannot acquire any new lock.</li>\n        </ol>\n        <strong>Variants of 2PL:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Basic 2PL:</strong> Guaranteed conflict serializability, but vulnerable to cascading aborts and deadlocks.</li>\n            <li><strong>Strict 2PL:</strong> All Exclusive (X) locks held by a transaction must be retained until the transaction commits or aborts. <em>Guarantees strict schedules and eliminates cascading aborts!</em></li>\n            <li><strong>Rigorous 2PL:</strong> Both Shared (S) and Exclusive (X) locks must be retained until transaction commit/abort. Ensures serializability in commit order.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023, 2013 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q13. What is Normalization? Explain the importance of Normalization in database design.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> <strong>Normalization</strong> is the systematic process of organizing relation schemas based on functional dependencies to eliminate anomalies and avoid redundant storage.<br>\n        <strong>Importance:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Eliminates Insertion, Deletion, and Modification anomalies.</li>\n            <li>Reduces storage space by ensuring each fact is recorded in only one place.</li>\n            <li>Prevents data corruption by enforcing referential integrity constraints across smaller, well-structured tables.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020, 2017 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q14. Explain Views in SQL. What are the advantages of Views? When is a View updatable?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Views in SQL:</strong> A <strong>View</strong> is a virtual table defined by an underlying stored SQL query.<br>\n        <strong>Advantages:</strong> Data security (restricts column access), simplified queries, and logical data independence.<br>\n        <strong>Updatable View Criteria:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Query contains only one base table in <code>FROM</code> clause.</li>\n            <li>No aggregate functions (<code>SUM</code>, <code>AVG</code>, <code>COUNT</code>).</li>\n            <li>No <code>GROUP BY</code>, <code>HAVING</code>, <code>DISTINCT</code>, or <code>UNION</code>.</li>\n            <li>Primary key of base table must be included in the view columns.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2017, 2015 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q15. Explain Log-Based Recovery with WAL (Write-Ahead Logging) and Checkpoints.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Log-Based Recovery</b> guarantees the Atomicity and Durability of transactions by recording every database modification into an append-only log file residing on stable storage before writing changes to disk.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) Protocol:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li>The log record corresponding to an update must be written to stable storage <b>before</b> the actual database disk buffer is flushed.</li>\n        <li>A transaction enters the <i>Committed</i> state only after its <code>&lt;commit T&gt;</code> log record is securely written to stable disk.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p><b>Checkpoint Mechanism:</b> Periodic checkpoints flush all dirty in-memory buffers to disk and append <code>&lt;checkpoint L&gt;</code> to the log. During system crash recovery, the recovery manager only needs to scan back to the most recent checkpoint rather than reading from the start of time.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Recovery Algorithms (Undo / Redo):</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Transaction State at Crash</th><th>Action Taken</th><th>Recovery Phase</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Both <code>&lt;start T&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;commit T&gt;</code> present in log.</td><td><b>REDO(T):</b> Reapplies all new values to ensure permanence (Durability).</td><td>Redo Pass (Roll-forward)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>&lt;start T&gt;</code> present, but <b>NO</b> <code>&lt;commit T&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;abort T&gt;</code>.</td><td><b>UNDO(T):</b> Restores all updated attributes back to their old values (Atomicity).</td><td>Undo Pass (Roll-back)</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2017, 2013 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q16. Explain the Three-Level ANSI/SPARC Database Architecture with detailed diagram.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>The <b>ANSI/SPARC Three-Schema Architecture</b> separates user views from physical database storage to achieve complete Data Independence.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>1. External Level (View Level):</b> The highest level closest to users. Consists of multiple customized external schemas (views) displaying only relevant data subsets and hiding complex business structures and unauthorized fields.</li>\n        <li><b>2. Conceptual Level (Logical Level):</b> The global logical blueprint of the entire enterprise database. Describes entities, attributes, relationships, constraints, and business rules without exposing physical disk details.</li>\n        <li><b>3. Internal Level (Physical Level):</b> The lowest level describing physical disk storage, block sizes, file organization (B+ trees, hashing), compression, and encryption.</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Data Independence Across Levels:</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Level of Independence</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example Scenario</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Physical Data Independence</b></td><td>Capacity to alter the Internal Schema (re-indexing, RAID striping, hashing) without altering the Conceptual Schema.</td><td>Adding a B+ Tree index on <code>Student(Email)</code> without altering table definitions.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Logical Data Independence</b></td><td>Capacity to alter the Conceptual Schema (adding tables or attributes) without modifying existing External Views or application queries.</td><td>Adding an <code>AadharNo</code> column to the Student table without breaking existing report generation scripts.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2016, 2015 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q17. Compare Dense Index and Sparse Index in database indexing.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>An <b>Index</b> is an auxiliary data structure that speeds up tuple retrieval in database tables without full table scans.</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Dense Index</th><th>Sparse Index</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Index Entries</b></td><td>Contains an index record for <b>every single search key value</b> in the data file.</td><td>Contains index records for <b>only a subset</b> of search key values (usually one per disk block).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>File Ordering</b></td><td>Can be constructed on either sorted (primary) or unsorted (secondary) data files.</td><td>Requires data file to be <b>strictly sorted</b> on the search key.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Storage Overhead</b></td><td>High storage space requirement (index size scales linearly with number of rows).</td><td>Very low storage space requirement; index easily fits into main memory RAM.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Search Speed</b></td><td>Faster: Locates record pointer directly in index without inspecting adjacent disk rows.</td><td>Slightly slower: Finds matching block pointer, then scans within the block to find exact record.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Maintenance (Insert/Delete)</b></td><td>High maintenance overhead on inserts and deletes.</td><td>Lower maintenance; changes only when block boundaries shift.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025</span>\n        Q18. Explain SQL Aggregate Functions (COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX) with GROUP BY and HAVING clauses. Provide full query demonstrations.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Aggregate Functions:</strong> Functions that perform calculations on a set of column values and return a single scalar summary value.<br>\n        <ul>\n            <li><code>COUNT(column)</code>: Returns total count of non-null values. <code>COUNT(*)</code> counts all rows.</li>\n            <li><code>SUM(column)</code>: Calculates total numeric sum of values.</li>\n            <li><code>AVG(column)</code>: Calculates arithmetic mean of non-null values.</li>\n            <li><code>MIN(column)</code> / <code>MAX(column)</code>: Returns the minimum / maximum value.</li>\n        </ul>\n        <strong>Role of GROUP BY and HAVING:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><code>GROUP BY</code> divides records into distinct groups based on identical column values.</li>\n            <li><code>HAVING</code> filters grouped rows after aggregation (whereas <code>WHERE</code> filters individual tuples prior to grouping).</li>\n        </ul>\n        <strong>Comprehensive SQL Example:</strong>\n<pre>\n-- Schema: Employee (EmpID, EmpName, Salary, DeptID, City)\n-- Query: Find DeptID, total employees, average salary, and max salary \n-- for departments having more than 3 employees and average salary > 45000:\n\nSELECT \n    DeptID,\n    COUNT(EmpID) AS TotalEmployees,\n    AVG(Salary) AS AverageSalary,\n    MAX(Salary) AS HighestSalary,\n    MIN(Salary) AS LowestSalary\nFROM Employee\nWHERE City IN ('Delhi', 'Noida', 'Gurugram')\nGROUP BY DeptID\nHAVING COUNT(EmpID) > 3 AND AVG(Salary) > 45000\nORDER BY AverageSalary DESC;\n</pre>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q19. Explain Normalization with its advantages. What anomalies arise in unnormalized databases?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Anomalies in Unnormalized Tables:</strong>\n        <ol>\n            <li><strong>Insertion Anomaly:</strong> Cannot record information about an entity without having to insert another unrelated entity (e.g., cannot register a new department until at least one employee is hired).</li>\n            <li><strong>Deletion Anomaly:</strong> Deleting a record of one entity unintentionally erases information about another entity (e.g., deleting the last employee in a department deletes all department details).</li>\n            <li><strong>Update / Modification Anomaly:</strong> Changing an entity's attribute requires updating hundreds of duplicate rows; if one row is missed, data becomes contradictory.</li>\n        </ol>\n        <strong>Advantages of Normalization:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Eliminates data redundancy and conserves disk storage.</li>\n            <li>Maintains referential integrity automatically across tables.</li>\n            <li>Speeds up row updates, inserts, and index lookups.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q20. Explain SQL with its various characteristics and features.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Structured Query Language (SQL):</strong> The ANSI/ISO standard declarative language for relational databases.<br>\n        <strong>Key Characteristics:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Declarative / Non-Procedural:</strong> Users specify <em>what data</em> they want, leaving the query optimizer to determine <em>how</em> to retrieve it.</li>\n            <li><strong>Comprehensive Capabilities:</strong> Encompasses DDL (structure), DML (manipulation), DCL (security/privileges), and TCL (transactions).</li>\n            <li><strong>Portability & Standards:</strong> Works uniformly across major vendors (Oracle, MySQL, SQL Server, PostgreSQL).</li>\n            <li><strong>Integrity Constraints Support:</strong> Native enforcement of <code>PRIMARY KEY</code>, <code>FOREIGN KEY</code>, <code>CHECK</code>, <code>UNIQUE</code>, and <code>DEFAULT</code>.</li>\n            <li><strong>View & Subquery Support:</strong> Enables nested queries, common table expressions (CTEs), and dynamic virtual tables (Views).</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q21. Explain the reduction of ER Schema to Relational Tables with a complete case study.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Case Study: Company Database:</strong><br>\n        Entities: <code>EMPLOYEE</code>, <code>DEPARTMENT</code>, <code>PROJECT</code>.<br>\n        Relationships:\n        <ul>\n            <li><code>WORKS_FOR</code> (N:1 from Employee to Department)</li>\n            <li><code>MANAGES</code> (1:1 from Employee to Department with <code>StartDate</code>)</li>\n            <li><code>WORKS_ON</code> (M:N between Employee and Project with <code>Hours</code>)</li>\n        </ul>\n        <strong>Relational Reduction:</strong>\n<pre>\n1. DEPARTMENT(<u>DeptNo</u>, DeptName, MgrEmpID, MgrStartDate)\n   -- 1:1 MANAGES folded into DEPARTMENT with MgrEmpID as FK\n2. EMPLOYEE(<u>EmpID</u>, EmpName, Salary, DeptNo)\n   -- N:1 WORKS_FOR represented via DeptNo as FK referencing DEPARTMENT\n3. PROJECT(<u>ProjNo</u>, ProjName, DeptNo)\n   -- N:1 CONTROLS represented via DeptNo as FK\n4. WORKS_ON(<u>EmpID</u>, <u>ProjNo</u>, Hours)\n   -- M:N relationship becomes a separate junction table with composite PK\n   -- FK EmpID -> EMPLOYEE, FK ProjNo -> PROJECT\n</pre>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023</span>\n        Q22. Draw an ER Diagram for a small Marketing Company database, assuming your own data requirements.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Enterprise Requirements:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>CAMPAIGN:</strong> <u>CampaignID</u>, Title, Budget, StartDate, EndDate.</li>\n            <li><strong>CLIENT:</strong> <u>ClientID</u>, ClientName, ContactPerson, Email, Phone.</li>\n            <li><strong>PRODUCT:</strong> <u>ProductID</u>, ProductName, Category.</li>\n            <li><strong>MARKETING_AGENT:</strong> <u>AgentID</u>, AgentName, CommissionRate.</li>\n        </ul>\n        <strong>Relationships:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li>A Client commissions one or more Campaigns (1:N).</li>\n            <li>Each Campaign promotes one or more Products (M:N).</li>\n            <li>Agents are assigned to Campaigns (M:N).</li>\n        </ul>\n        <div class=\"diagram-box\">\n +------------+        (1)                 (M)        +--------------+\n |   CLIENT   | ------------------------------------ |   CAMPAIGN   |\n | <u>ClientID</u>   |          < COMMISSIONS >             | <u>CampaignID</u>   |\n | ClientName |                                      | Title, Budget|\n +------------+                                      +--------------+\n                                                            | (M)\n                                                      < PROMOTES >\n                                                            | (N)\n                                                            v\n                                                     +--------------+\n                                                     |   PRODUCT    |\n                                                     | <u>ProductID</u>    |\n                                                     | ProductName  |\n                                                     +--------------+\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023</span>\n        Q23. Describe the classification of Database Languages. Which type of language is SQL?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Classification of Database Languages:</strong>\n        <ol>\n            <li><strong>Data Definition Language (DDL):</strong> Specifies the database schema, storage structure, and constraints (<code>CREATE</code>, <code>ALTER</code>, <code>DROP</code>, <code>TRUNCATE</code>).</li>\n            <li><strong>Data Manipulation Language (DML):</strong> Retrieves, inserts, updates, and deletes stored data. Can be procedural (relational algebra) or non-procedural/declarative (relational calculus, SQL).</li>\n            <li><strong>Data Control Language (DCL):</strong> Controls permissions and access authorization (<code>GRANT</code>, <code>REVOKE</code>).</li>\n            <li><strong>Transaction Control Language (TCL):</strong> Manages the logical unit of work (<code>COMMIT</code>, <code>ROLLBACK</code>, <code>SAVEPOINT</code>).</li>\n        </ol>\n        <strong>Classification of SQL:</strong>\n        SQL is a <strong>comprehensive declarative (non-procedural) language</strong> that integrates DDL, DML, DCL, and TCL in a single standardized unified language.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021</span>\n        Q24. Given the Relational Schema:<br>\n    EMPLOYEE(<u>EmpID</u>, EmpName, Age, Gender, Salary, City, DeptID, Designation, HireDate)<br>\n    DEPARTMENT(<u>DeptID</u>, DeptName, Location)<br>\n    Write SQL Queries for:<br>\n    1. Display the age of 'Male' employees.<br>\n    2. Display the name of the highest salary employee.<br>\n    3. Display the name of department of employee 'Aman'.<br>\n    4. Display all employees belonging to the same address (city).<br>\n    5. Which employee is the oldest manager in the company?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>SQL Queries:</strong>\n<pre>\n-- Query 1: Age of Male employees\nSELECT EmpName, Age \nFROM EMPLOYEE \nWHERE Gender = 'Male';\n\n-- Query 2: Name of the highest salary employee\nSELECT EmpName, Salary \nFROM EMPLOYEE \nWHERE Salary = (SELECT MAX(Salary) FROM EMPLOYEE);\n\n-- Query 3: Department name of employee 'Aman'\nSELECT D.DeptName \nFROM DEPARTMENT D \nJOIN EMPLOYEE E ON D.DeptID = E.DeptID \nWHERE E.EmpName = 'Aman';\n\n-- Query 4: Display all employees grouped by identical city\nSELECT City, COUNT(EmpID) AS TotalEmps, \n       GROUP_CONCAT(EmpName) AS EmployeeNames \nFROM EMPLOYEE \nGROUP BY City \nHAVING COUNT(EmpID) > 1;\n\n-- Query 5: Oldest manager in the company\nSELECT EmpName, Age, Designation, HireDate \nFROM EMPLOYEE \nWHERE Designation = 'Manager' \nORDER BY Age DESC \nLIMIT 1;\n</pre>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q25. Explain File Organization Techniques: Sequential, Indexed Sequential (ISAM), and Hashed File Organization.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>File Organization</b> refers to how records are mapped onto physical storage blocks on disk drives:</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>1. Sequential File Organization:</b>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Records are stored in continuous sequential order sorted by a designated search key.</li>\n                <li><i>Pros:</i> Highly efficient for batch processing applications (e.g., end-of-month payroll processing).</li>\n                <li><i>Cons:</i> Extremely slow for single-record random access (<i>O(N)</i> scan); costly overflow blocks for new inserts.</li>\n            </ul>\n        </li>\n        <li><b>2. Indexed Sequential Access Method (ISAM):</b>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Combines sequential file storage with a hierarchical multi-level index tree (cylinder and track indexes).</li>\n                <li><i>Pros:</i> Supports both fast sequential scans and fast random access via index lookup (<i>O(log N)</i>).</li>\n                <li><i>Cons:</i> Periodic reorganization required when overflow chains become deep.</li>\n            </ul>\n        </li>\n        <li><b>3. Hashed (Direct) File Organization:</b>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Uses a mathematical hash function <code>h(Key) &rarr; Block Address</code> to directly compute disk block addresses.</li>\n                <li><i>Pros:</i> Near-instantaneous constant time access (<i>O(1)</i>) for equality lookups.</li>\n                <li><i>Cons:</i> Ineffective for range queries (<code>WHERE Age BETWEEN 20 AND 30</code>); prone to hash collisions.</li>\n            </ul>\n        </li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q26. Explain Database Security and Authorization. Discuss GRANT and REVOKE commands in SQL.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Database Security</b> protects the system against unauthorized access, malicious data corruption, and privacy breaches using authentication and role-based authorization.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>SQL Authorization Commands (DCL):</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><code>GRANT</code>: Bestows specific privileges (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) to database users or roles.</li>\n        <li><code>REVOKE</code>: Withdraws previously granted privileges.</li>\n        <li><code>WITH GRANT OPTION</code>: Allows the recipient user to delegate the granted privilege to other third-party users.</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">SQL Query Demonstrations:</p>\n<div class=\"code-box\">-- 1. Grant SELECT and UPDATE permissions to user 'finance_officer'\nGRANT SELECT, UPDATE(Salary) ON Employee TO finance_officer;\n\n-- 2. Grant all privileges on Student table with permission to delegate\nGRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON Student TO registrar WITH GRANT OPTION;\n\n-- 3. Revoke UPDATE privilege from user 'finance_officer'\nREVOKE UPDATE ON Employee FROM finance_officer;\n\n-- 4. Cascade revocation (removes delegated permissions as well)\nREVOKE SELECT ON Student FROM registrar CASCADE;</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019</span>\n        Q27. Explain the Three-Tier Client/Server Architecture of DBMS with Data Independence.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Three-Tier Architecture:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Presentation Tier (User Interface):</strong> Web browser or desktop client GUI (React, HTML).</li>\n            <li><strong>Application Tier (Business Logic):</strong> Web/Application server executing business validation and query processing (Java, Node.js, Python).</li>\n            <li><strong>Database Tier (Data Storage):</strong> Enterprise RDBMS managing database engine, query processing, transactions, and tables.</li>\n        </ul>\n        Provides enhanced security, scalability, and clean modular separation.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019</span>\n        Q28. Discuss SQL Characteristics and explain five Aggregate Functions with complete query examples.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>SQL Queries for Aggregate Functions:</strong>\n<pre>\n-- 1. COUNT: Total employees in IT\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM Employee WHERE Dept = 'IT';\n\n-- 2. SUM: Total payroll for Sales\nSELECT SUM(Salary) FROM Employee WHERE Dept = 'Sales';\n\n-- 3. AVG: Average salary in company\nSELECT AVG(Salary) FROM Employee;\n\n-- 4. MIN and MAX: Salary bounds\nSELECT MIN(Salary) AS MinSalary, MAX(Salary) AS MaxSalary FROM Employee;\n</pre>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019</span>\n        Q29. Explain Database Recovery Techniques: Log-Based Recovery, Immediate Update, Deferred Update, and Checkpoints.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Log-Based Recovery:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Deferred Update (NO-UNDO / REDO):</strong> Updates are recorded only in the log until commit. During recovery, only committed transactions are REDONE; uncommitted transactions require NO UNDO.</li>\n            <li><strong>Immediate Update (UNDO / REDO):</strong> Updates can be written to disk before commit. Active uncommitted transactions at crash must be UNDONE; committed ones are REDONE.</li>\n            <li><strong>Checkpoints:</strong> Periodically flushes dirty log buffers to disk. Recovery scans backward only to the most recent checkpoint, dramatically shortening crash recovery time.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2018</span>\n        Q30. Explain Super Class, Sub Class, Inheritance, and Generalization with examples.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Enhanced ER (EER) Concepts:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Superclass:</strong> A generic entity set that includes distinct subgroups (e.g., <code>PERSON</code>).</li>\n            <li><strong>Subclass:</strong> A specialized sub-entity set that inherits all attributes and relationships from its superclass, while adding specialized attributes (e.g., <code>STUDENT</code> with GPA, <code>TEACHER</code> with Rank).</li>\n            <li><strong>Attribute Inheritance:</strong> Subclass entities automatically inherit the primary key and all general attributes of their superclass.</li>\n            <li><strong>Generalization:</strong> Bottom-up synthesis where lower-level entity sets are combined into a generalized superclass entity.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2018</span>\n        Q31. Write short notes on: (i) Strong and Weak Entity, (ii) Different Levels of Data Abstraction, (iii) Database Recovery Techniques.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>(i) Strong vs Weak Entity:</strong> A Strong entity has a primary key of its own. A Weak entity does not have a primary key and depends on the existence of an identifying strong entity set (identified by discriminator + strong entity PK).<br>\n        <strong>(ii) Data Abstraction Levels:</strong> Physical level (low-level block storage), Logical level (community tables and relations), and View level (user-specific abstractions).<br>\n        <strong>(iii) Database Recovery Techniques:</strong> Log-based recovery (Write-Ahead Logging), Deferred/Immediate update schemes, and Checkpoints to restore consistency following system failures.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2018</span>\n        Q32. A hospital maintains data about entities: PATIENTS, DOCTORS, BEDS, ACCOUNTS. Construct an E-R diagram specifying keys, mapping cardinalities, and participation constraints. Show table mapping.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Entities & Attributes:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><code>PATIENTS(<u>SSNo</u>, LastName, FirstName, HomePhone, Sex, DOB, Age, Street, City, State, Zip)</code></li>\n            <li><code>DOCTORS(<u>SSNo</u>, LastName, FirstName, OfficePhone, Pager, Specialty)</code></li>\n            <li><code>BEDS(<u>RoomNumber</u>, <u>BedNumber</u>, Type, Status, PricePerHour)</code></li>\n            <li><code>ACCOUNTS(<u>AccountNo</u>, DateIn, DateOut, TotalAmount, Status)</code></li>\n        </ul>\n        <strong>Relationships:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><code>TREATS</code> (M:N between DOCTORS and PATIENTS)</li>\n            <li><code>ASSIGNED_BED</code> (1:1 between PATIENTS and BEDS)</li>\n            <li><code>HAS_ACCOUNT</code> (1:1 between PATIENTS and ACCOUNTS)</li>\n        </ul>\n        <div class=\"diagram-box\">\n  +------------+             ( M )             +------------+\n  |  DOCTORS   | <---------------------------> |  PATIENTS  |\n  | <u>SSNo</u>, Spec |          < TREATS >           | <u>SSNo</u>, Name |\n  +------------+                               +------------+\n                                                    | (1)\n                                            < ASSIGNED_BED >\n                                                    | (1)\n                                                    v\n                                               +------------+\n                                               |    BEDS    |\n                                               | <u>Room</u>, <u>Bed</u> |\n                                               +------------+\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2017</span>\n        Q33. Given tables VENDOR(<u>VCode</u>, Name, Address, Phone) and PRODUCT(<u>PCode</u>, PName, Price, VCode). Write SQL queries for:<br>\n    (i) Display all vendors from 'Delhi'.<br>\n    (ii) Display products with price greater than 500.<br>\n    (iii) Display vendor name who supplies product 'Laptop'.<br>\n    (iv) Count total products supplied by each vendor.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>SQL Solutions:</strong>\n<pre>\n-- (i) Vendors from Delhi\nSELECT * FROM VENDOR WHERE Address LIKE '%Delhi%';\n\n-- (ii) Products with price > 500\nSELECT PCode, PName, Price FROM PRODUCT WHERE Price > 500;\n\n-- (iii) Vendor supplying 'Laptop'\nSELECT V.Name \nFROM VENDOR V \nJOIN PRODUCT P ON V.VCode = P.VCode \nWHERE P.PName = 'Laptop';\n\n-- (iv) Count total products per vendor\nSELECT V.VCode, V.Name, COUNT(P.PCode) AS TotalProducts \nFROM VENDOR V \nLEFT JOIN PRODUCT P ON V.VCode = P.VCode \nGROUP BY V.VCode, V.Name;\n</pre>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2017</span>\n        Q34. Given tables: PLAY(<u>PlayCode</u>, PlayName, DirNum), DIRECTOR(<u>DirNum</u>, DirName, DirDOB). Write SQL queries to find director names and plays directed.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>SQL Solutions:</strong>\n<pre>\n-- Director of play 'Hamlet'\nSELECT D.DirName \nFROM DIRECTOR D \nJOIN PLAY P ON D.DirNum = P.DirNum \nWHERE P.PlayName = 'Hamlet';\n\n-- All plays directed by each director\nSELECT D.DirName, P.PlayName \nFROM DIRECTOR D \nJOIN PLAY P ON D.DirNum = P.DirNum \nORDER BY D.DirName;\n</pre>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2016</span>\n        Q35. Draw a detailed ER Diagram for a Banking Enterprise.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>An <b>Entity-Relationship (ER) Diagram</b> visually models the conceptual data architecture of an enterprise.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Core Entities & Attributes for Banking System:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>CUSTOMER:</b> <u>CustID</u>, Name (First, Last), Address, Phone, PAN.</li>\n        <li><b>BRANCH:</b> <u>BranchID</u>, BranchName, City, Assets.</li>\n        <li><b>ACCOUNT:</b> <u>AccountNo</u>, AccountType (Savings, Current), Balance.</li>\n        <li><b>LOAN:</b> <u>LoanNo</u>, Amount, InterestRate, LoanType.</li>\n        <li><b>TRANSACTION:</b> <u>TxnID</u>, Date, Amount, TxnType (Deposit, Withdrawal).</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p><b>Relationships & Cardinalities:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>Cust_Account (Many-to-Many M:N):</b> A Customer can hold multiple accounts; a Joint account can have multiple customers.</li>\n        <li><b>Branch_Account (One-to-Many 1:N):</b> Each Account is maintained at exactly one Branch; a Branch maintains many accounts.</li>\n        <li><b>Cust_Loan (Many-to-Many M:N):</b> A Customer can take multiple loans; a joint loan belongs to multiple customers.</li>\n        <li><b>Account_Txn (One-to-Many 1:N):</b> An Account records many Transactions; each Transaction belongs to one Account.</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2016</span>\n        Q36. Explain Nested Subqueries and Correlated Subqueries in SQL with examples.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Nested (Non-Correlated) Subquery</th><th>Correlated Subquery</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Execution Mechanism</b></td><td>The inner query executes <b>once independently</b>, and its result set is passed to the outer query.</td><td>The inner query executes <b>repeatedly, once for each candidate row</b> processed by the outer query.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Dependency</b></td><td>Inner query has no reference to outer query columns.</td><td>Inner query explicitly references columns from the outer query table (correlation).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Performance</b></td><td>Fast; executed a single time in memory.</td><td>Slower on large tables; <i>O(N &times; M)</i> complexity unless optimized by engine.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Code Example</b></td><td>\n        <div class=\"code-box\">SELECT Name, Salary FROM Employee \nWHERE Salary &gt; (SELECT AVG(Salary) FROM Employee);</div>\n    </td><td>\n        <div class=\"code-box\">SELECT E1.Name, E1.Salary FROM Employee E1 \nWHERE E1.Salary &gt; (SELECT AVG(E2.Salary) \n                     FROM Employee E2 \n                     WHERE E2.DeptID = E1.DeptID);</div>\n    </td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2014</span>\n        Q37. Given tables: DEPT(<u>Deptno</u>, Dname, Loc), PROJECT(<u>Projno</u>, Deptno, Projname). Write SQL queries for project allocations and department summaries.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>SQL Solutions:</strong> Complete SQL join queries, aggregate counts per department, and filter criteria.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2014</span>\n        Q38. Explain Log-Based Recovery and Checkpoint Mechanisms in DBMS.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Database Recovery:</strong> Deferred update, immediate update, write-ahead logging (WAL), and checkpoint-based fast recovery.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"sec-title\" data-sec=\"sec-b\">Section – B (Short Answer Questions &bull; 7.5 Marks &bull; 19 Questions)</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2023, 2019, 2018, 2016, 2014, 2013 &bull; Repeated 7&times;</span>\n        Q1. Explain Primary Key, Foreign Key, and Candidate Key with suitable SQL examples.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>1. Candidate Key:</strong> A minimal super key &mdash; an attribute or minimal set of attributes that uniquely identifies each tuple in a relation without any redundant attributes.<br>\n        <strong>2. Primary Key:</strong> The specific candidate key chosen by the database designer to uniquely identify tuples in a table. It cannot accept duplicate values and cannot contain <code>NULL</code>.<br>\n        <strong>3. Foreign Key:</strong> An attribute in a child table whose values must match the primary key of a referenced parent table (or be <code>NULL</code>), enforcing referential integrity.<br>\n        <strong>SQL Implementation Example:</strong>\n<pre>\n-- Parent Table: DEPARTMENT\nCREATE TABLE Department (\n    DeptID INT PRIMARY KEY,              -- Primary Key\n    DeptName VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL UNIQUE -- Candidate Key\n);\n\n-- Child Table: EMPLOYEE\nCREATE TABLE Employee (\n    EmpID INT PRIMARY KEY,               -- Primary Key\n    EmpName VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    Email VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE,           -- Candidate Key\n    DeptID INT,                          -- Foreign Key\n    CONSTRAINT fk_dept FOREIGN KEY (DeptID) REFERENCES Department(DeptID)\n        ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n);\n</pre>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2023, 2019, 2018 &bull; Repeated 4&times;</span>\n        Q2. State the key advantages of DBMS over File Processing Systems.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Advantages:</strong>\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Traditional File System</th><th>Database Management System (DBMS)</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n                <tr><td>Data Redundancy</td><td>High redundancy across duplicate files</td><td>Controlled and minimized via normalization</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Data Inconsistency</td><td>High risk due to isolated updates</td><td>Eliminated via centralized repository</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Data Independence</td><td>None; changes in files break code</td><td>Physical and Logical Data Independence achieved</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Concurrent Access</td><td>Prone to race conditions and lost updates</td><td>ACID compliant concurrency control (2PL, timestamp)</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Security & Integrity</td><td>OS-level passwords, hard to enforce</td><td>Fine-grained role-based access & constraints</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n        </table>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021, 2015, 2014 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q3. Explain the Three-Level Architecture of DBMS with a neat block diagram.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Three-Level ANSI/SPARC Architecture:</strong>\n        <pre class=\"diagram-box\">\n+---------------------+   +---------------------+   +---------------------+\n|  External View 1    |   |  External View 2    |   |  External View n    |\n+---------------------+   +---------------------+   +---------------------+\n           \\                         |                         /\n            ================== External / Conceptual Mapping =============\n                                     v\n                        +-------------------------+\n                        |    Conceptual Schema    | (Logical community view)\n                        +-------------------------+\n                                     |\n            ================== Conceptual / Internal Mapping =============\n                                     v\n                        +-------------------------+\n                        |     Internal Schema     | (Physical disk storage, indexes)\n                        +-------------------------+\n                                     |\n                                     v\n                        +=========================+\n                        |    Physical Database    | (Blocks, sectors on disk)\n                        +=========================+\n        </pre>\n        <strong>1. External Level (View Level):</strong> User-specific customized views presenting only relevant subsets of data while hiding confidential fields.<br>\n        <strong>2. Conceptual Level (Logical Level):</strong> Describes <em>what data</em> is stored in the entire database and the relationships among data without physical storage details.<br>\n        <strong>3. Internal Level (Physical Level):</strong> Describes <em>how data</em> is physically stored on storage media (record formats, B+ trees, hashing).\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021, 2018, 2013 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q4. Explain Hashing in brief with its collision handling techniques.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> <strong>Hashing</strong> is an efficient direct search technique where a mathematical hash function <span class=\"math-expr\">h(k)</span> maps a search key <span class=\"math-expr\">k</span> directly to the disk address/bucket number where the record resides, providing average <span class=\"math-expr\">O(1)</span> time complexity.<br>\n        <strong>Collision:</strong> Occurs when two distinct search keys hash to the same bucket address: <span class=\"math-expr\">h(k<sub>1</sub>) = h(k<sub>2</sub>)</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Collision Handling Techniques:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Open Addressing (Linear Probing):</strong> Searches the next contiguous sequential bucket until an empty slot is located.</li>\n            <li><strong>Chaining / Separate Overflow Buckets:</strong> Maintains a linked list of overflow buckets chained to the primary bucket.</li>\n            <li><strong>Extendible Hashing (Dynamic Hashing):</strong> Uses a directory of pointers with local and global depths to double directories and split buckets dynamically without rehashing all records.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020, 2019, 2018 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q5. What is a Referential Integrity Constraint? Why do we need it? Explain with a suitable example.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Explanation:</strong> Referential integrity requires that a foreign key value in a referencing table must match an existing primary key value in the referenced table, or be <code>NULL</code>.<br>\n        <strong>Need:</strong> Prevents orphan records and inconsistent states.<br>\n        <strong>Example:</strong>\n<pre>\n-- Parent Table\nCREATE TABLE Department (DeptNo INT PRIMARY KEY, DName VARCHAR(30));\n\n-- Child Table with Foreign Key\nCREATE TABLE Employee (\n    EmpID INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    EmpName VARCHAR(50),\n    DeptNo INT,\n    CONSTRAINT fk_emp_dept FOREIGN KEY (DeptNo) REFERENCES Department(DeptNo)\n        ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n);\n</pre>\n        If department 10 is deleted, <code>ON DELETE CASCADE</code> automatically removes all employees assigned to department 10, preventing dangling references.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2023 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q6. Define the following terms: (a) Data Model, (b) Database Schema.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>(a) Data Model:</strong> A conceptual collection of tools, constructs, and notations used to describe data, data relationships, data semantics, and consistency constraints. Major models include:\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Relational Model:</strong> Tables of tuples and attributes (SQL).</li>\n            <li><strong>Entity-Relationship (ER) Model:</strong> High-level conceptual modeling.</li>\n            <li><strong>Object-Oriented & Document (NoSQL) Models:</strong> JSON, key-value, graph.</li>\n        </ul>\n        <strong>(b) Database Schema:</strong> The overall structural skeleton of the database defined during the design phase, distinct from the dynamic instances/states stored inside it.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019, 2018 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q7. Explain Third Normal Form (3NF) with example.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> A relation is in <strong>3NF</strong> if it is in 2NF and has no transitive functional dependencies for non-prime attributes. Formally, for every non-trivial FD <span class=\"math-expr\">X &rarr; Y</span>, either <span class=\"math-expr\">X</span> is a super key or <span class=\"math-expr\">Y</span> is a prime attribute.<br>\n        <strong>Example:</strong> <code>Student(<u>RollNo</u>, Name, DeptID, DeptHead)</code>.<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">RollNo &rarr; DeptID</span> and <span class=\"math-expr\">DeptID &rarr; DeptHead</span> (Transitive!).<br>\n        Decompose into: <code>Student(<u>RollNo</u>, Name, DeptID)</code> and <code>Department(<u>DeptID</u>, DeptHead)</code>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025</span>\n        Q8. Explain ER Diagram. Describe all standard ER notations and symbols with diagrams.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> An <strong>Entity-Relationship (ER) Diagram</strong> is a graphical semantic modeling tool that represents the logical structure of a database in terms of entities, their attributes, and relationships.<br>\n        <strong>Standard Symbols & Notations:</strong>\n        <div class=\"diagram-box\">\n[ Rectangle ]           -> Entity Set (e.g., STUDENT, COURSE)\n[[ Double Rectangle ]]  -> Weak Entity Set (e.g., DEPENDENT, INSTALLMENT)\n( Ellipse / Oval )      -> Attribute (e.g., Age, Name)\n( Underlined Text )     -> Primary Key Attribute (e.g., <u>RollNo</u>)\n(( Double Ellipse ))    -> Multivalued Attribute (e.g., PhoneNumbers, Skills)\n( Dashed Ellipse )      -> Derived Attribute (e.g., Age calculated from DOB)\n{ Diamond }             -> Relationship Set (e.g., ENROLLED_IN)\n{{ Double Diamond }}    -> Identifying Relationship for Weak Entity Set\n======== Double Line    -> Total Participation (Every entity must participate)\n-------- Single Line    -> Partial Participation\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q9. Find the minimum number of tables required for the given ER diagram in the relational model. Explain table conversion rules.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Rules for ER to Relational Reduction:</strong>\n        <ol>\n            <li><strong>Strong Entity Sets:</strong> Each strong entity converts into a separate independent table.</li>\n            <li><strong>Weak Entity Sets:</strong> Converts into a table containing its discriminator (partial key) combined with the primary key of the identifying strong entity as composite primary key.</li>\n            <li><strong>1:1 Relationships:</strong> Combine both entities into 1 table, or place the primary key of one table as a foreign key in the other table.</li>\n            <li><strong>1:N Relationships:</strong> Place the primary key of the '1' side entity into the table of the 'N' side entity as a foreign key (no new table needed).</li>\n            <li><strong>M:N Relationships:</strong> Requires a new separate relationship table containing primary keys of both participating entities as foreign keys.</li>\n        </ol>\n        <strong>Result:</strong> For an ER model with two strong entities A and B in 1:N relationship, exactly <strong>2 tables</strong> are needed. If the relationship is M:N, exactly <strong>3 tables</strong> are required.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q10. What are UNION, MINUS, and INTERSECT commands in DBMS? Explain with syntax and examples.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Conditions:</strong> Both queries must be <em>Union-Compatible</em> (same number of columns with compatible data types).<br>\n        <ul>\n            <li><code>UNION</code>: Combines results of two SELECT queries and eliminates duplicate tuples.</li>\n            <li><code>INTERSECT</code>: Returns only the common tuples present in both result sets.</li>\n            <li><code>MINUS / EXCEPT</code>: Returns tuples from the first query that are not present in the second query.</li>\n        </ul>\n        <strong>SQL Examples:</strong>\n<pre>\n-- UNION: All students from CS or IT\nSELECT RollNo, Name FROM Student_CS\nUNION\nSELECT RollNo, Name FROM Student_IT;\n\n-- INTERSECT: Students enrolled in both Math and Physics clubs\nSELECT StudentID FROM MathClub\nINTERSECT\nSELECT StudentID FROM PhysicsClub;\n\n-- MINUS: Registered students who have NOT paid exam fees\nSELECT StudentID FROM RegisteredStudents\nEXCEPT\nSELECT StudentID FROM PaidStudents;\n</pre>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023</span>\n        Q11. Compare Generalization and Specialization with examples.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Comparison:</strong>\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Criteria</th><th>Generalization</th><th>Specialization</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n                <tr><td>Approach</td><td><strong>Bottom-up</strong> design process</td><td><strong>Top-down</strong> design process</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Mechanism</td><td>Combines common features of multiple lower-level entities into a single higher-level entity.</td><td>Splits a higher-level entity into specialized sub-entities based on distinct features.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Goal</td><td>Minimizes redundancy by factoring out common attributes.</td><td>Identifies distinguishing characteristics of specific sub-categories.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Example</td><td>Entities <code>CAR</code> and <code>TRUCK</code> generalized into higher entity <code>VEHICLE</code>.</td><td>Entity <code>EMPLOYEE</code> specialized into <code>SALARIED_EMP</code> and <code>HOURLY_EMP</code>.</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n        </table>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021</span>\n        Q12. Define Primary Key, NOT NULL Key, and UNIQUE Key with differences.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definitions & Differences:</strong>\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>PRIMARY KEY</th><th>UNIQUE KEY</th><th>NOT NULL</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n                <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Uniquely identifies each record.</td><td>Ensures distinct non-duplicate values.</td><td>Prevents missing or null values.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>NULL Allowed</td><td><strong>Never</strong> allowed (strictly 0 nulls).</td><td>Allowed (usually 1 or more nulls per column).</td><td>Does not enforce uniqueness, only disallows null.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Count per table</td><td>Only <strong>1</strong> per table.</td><td>Multiple unique constraints allowed.</td><td>Can be applied to every column.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Index created</td><td>Automatically creates Primary Clustered Index.</td><td>Creates Secondary Unique Non-Clustered Index.</td><td>No index created by default.</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n        </table>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q13. Explain Lossless-Join Decomposition and Dependency Preservation in relational design.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>1. Lossless-Join Decomposition:</strong><br>\n        A decomposition of relation <span class=\"math-expr\">R</span> into <span class=\"math-expr\">R<sub>1</sub></span> and <span class=\"math-expr\">R<sub>2</sub></span> is lossless if and only if natural join of the projections equals the original relation without generating spurious tuples:\n        <pre class=\"diagram-box\">R<sub>1</sub> &bowtie; R<sub>2</sub> = R &hArr; (R<sub>1</sub> &cap; R<sub>2</sub> &rarr; R<sub>1</sub>) OR (R<sub>1</sub> &cap; R<sub>2</sub> &rarr; R<sub>2</sub>)</pre>\n        <strong>2. Dependency Preservation:</strong><br>\n        A decomposition preserves dependencies if the union of functional dependencies enforceable within individual decomposed relations covers all functional dependencies of the original relation: <span class=\"math-expr\">(F<sub>1</sub> &cup; F<sub>2</sub>)^+ = F^+</span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q14. Compare 3NF and BCNF with an illustrative example.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Key Differences:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>3NF:</strong> For every non-trivial FD <span class=\"math-expr\">X &rarr; Y</span>, either <span class=\"math-expr\">X</span> is a super key <em>OR</em> <span class=\"math-expr\">Y</span> is a prime attribute (part of any candidate key). Allows dependency preservation always.</li>\n            <li><strong>BCNF:</strong> For every non-trivial FD <span class=\"math-expr\">X &rarr; Y</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">X</span> <strong>must strictly be a super key</strong>. Eliminates all FD redundancy, but may not always preserve all functional dependencies.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019</span>\n        Q15. Compare B-Tree and B+ Tree Index Structures.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Comparison:</strong>\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>B-Tree</th><th>B+ Tree</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n                <tr><td>Data Storage</td><td>Keys and data records/pointers stored in both internal and leaf nodes.</td><td>Data pointers stored <strong>only in leaf nodes</strong>; internal nodes store only routing keys.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Leaf Node Linking</td><td>Leaf nodes are not linked.</td><td>Leaf nodes are connected via a <strong>doubly linked list</strong>.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Range Queries</td><td>Slow (requires in-order tree traversal).</td><td>Extremely fast (scan linked list across leaf nodes).</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Node Capacity</td><td>Fewer keys per node due to data pointers.</td><td>High fan-out, fewer levels, reduced disk I/O.</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n        </table>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019</span>\n        Q16. Explain Conflict Serializability and Testing for Serializability using Precedence Graph.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>A schedule <i>S</i> is <b>Conflict Serializable</b> if it is conflict equivalent to some serial schedule of the same transactions by swapping non-conflicting operations.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Conflict Operations:</b> Two operations conflict if: (1) they belong to different transactions, (2) they access the same data item, and (3) at least one of them is a <code>write()</code> operation.</p>\n    <p><b>Precedence Graph (Serialization Graph) Testing:</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li>Construct a directed graph <code>G = (V, E)</code> where each vertex represents an active transaction <i>T<sub>i</sub></i>.</li>\n        <li>Draw a directed edge <i>T<sub>i</sub> &rarr; T<sub>j</sub></i> if <i>T<sub>i</sub></i> executes an operation that conflicts with an operation executed later by <i>T<sub>j</sub></i>.</li>\n        <li><b>Theorem:</b> A schedule <i>S</i> is Conflict Serializable <b>if and only if its Precedence Graph contains NO directed cycles</b>.</li>\n        <li>If acyclic, Topological Sorting yields the equivalent serial execution order.</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2017</span>\n        Q17. What is Query Optimization? Explain heuristic optimization.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Query Optimization</b> is the process of selecting the most efficient physical execution plan (evaluating lowest disk I/O, CPU, and memory cost) among equivalent relational algebra strategies.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Heuristic Optimization Rules (Pushing Down Operations):</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>Perform Selections (&sigma;) as early as possible:</b> Pushing selections down reduces the number of tuples before costly joins.</li>\n        <li><b>Perform Projections (&pi;) as early as possible:</b> Discarding unneeded columns reduces tuple byte width and memory consumption.</li>\n        <li><b>Combine Selection with Cartesian Product into Join:</b> Replace <code>&sigma;<sub>R.A = S.A</sub> (R &times; S)</code> with Equi-Join <code>R &bowtie; S</code> to eliminate full cross products.</li>\n        <li><b>Evaluate Restrictive Joins First:</b> Order joins so intermediate relation sizes are minimized.</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2016</span>\n        Q18. Explain Deadlock Detection and Prevention methods in DBMS.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>A <b>Deadlock</b> is a condition where two or more transactions are in a simultaneous circular wait state, each holding locks that the other requires to proceed.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p class=\"step-label\">1. Deadlock Prevention Strategies (Timestamp-Based):</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>Wait-Die Scheme (Non-Preemptive):</b> If older transaction <i>T<sub>i</sub></i> requests a lock held by younger <i>T<sub>j</sub></i>, <i>T<sub>i</sub></i> is allowed to <b>wait</b>. If younger requests from older, younger <b>dies</b> (aborts and restarts with original timestamp).</li>\n        <li><b>Wound-Wait Scheme (Preemptive):</b> If older requests from younger, older <b>wounds</b> (preempts/aborts) younger. If younger requests from older, younger is allowed to <b>wait</b>.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p class=\"step-label\">2. Deadlock Detection & Recovery:</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>Wait-For Graph (WFG):</b> Directed graph where nodes represent active transactions. Directed edge <i>T<sub>1</sub> &rarr; T<sub>2</sub></i> indicates <i>T<sub>1</sub></i> is waiting for <i>T<sub>2</sub></i>. A <b>cycle in the WFG signals a deadlock</b>.</li>\n        <li><b>Deadlock Recovery:</b> Pick a victim transaction based on lowest rollback cost, abort it, release its locks, and restart it.</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2014</span>\n        Q19. Differentiate between Association and Aggregation with suitable examples.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Association</th><th>Aggregation</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Concept</b></td><td>A generic structural relationship between two independent classes (\"knows-a\" relationship).</td><td>A specialized form of association representing a whole-part relationship (\"has-a\" relationship).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Ownership & Lifetime</b></td><td>Both objects have independent lifecycles; neither owns the other.</td><td>The parent (\"whole\") contains the child (\"part\"), but the child can exist independently of the parent.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Tightness of Coupling</b></td><td>Loose coupling.</td><td>Moderate coupling (weaker than Composition where parent death destroys children).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Real-World Example</b></td><td><b>Teacher and Student:</b> A Teacher associates with a Student, but both exist independently.</td><td><b>Department and Teacher:</b> A Department aggregates Teachers. If Department closes, Teachers still exist.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"sec-title\" data-sec=\"sec-a\">Section – A (Very Short Answer Questions &bull; 3 Marks &bull; 23 Questions)</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023, 2016, 2015, 2013 &bull; Repeated 4&times;</span>\n        Q1. Define Database Management System (DBMS).\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> A <strong>Database Management System (DBMS)</strong> is a computerized system software suite that enables users to define, create, maintain, query, and control access to a shared database. It serves as an intelligent interface between end-user applications and physical data storage.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2014 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q2. What do you mean by Data Inconsistency in File Management Systems? Explain.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Explanation:</strong> <strong>Data Inconsistency</strong> occurs when multiple copies of the exact same data across different files contain conflicting, contradictory values.<br>\n        <strong>Example:</strong> In a university file system, a student's address is recorded in the <em>Library File</em> and also in the <em>Fee Accounting File</em>. If the student changes their residential address and only the Library office updates their local file while the Fee office does not, the university now holds two contradictory addresses for the same student. This occurs due to uncoordinated duplicate storage without centralized integrity control.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020, 2017 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q3. What is a Data Dictionary (System Catalog)?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> A <strong>Data Dictionary</strong> is a reserved set of read-only system tables maintained by the DBMS that stores metadata (data about data), including schemas, table descriptions, column domains, integrity constraints, indexes, user privileges, and storage allocations.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q4. What are the advantages of using DBMS?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Key Advantages:</strong>\n        <ol>\n            <li><strong>Minimal Redundancy:</strong> Normalization ensures data is stored in one primary location.</li>\n            <li><strong>Data Integrity:</strong> Automatic enforcement of domain and referential integrity constraints.</li>\n            <li><strong>Concurrency Control:</strong> Multi-user simultaneous access without collisions via locking protocols.</li>\n            <li><strong>Crash Recovery:</strong> WAL (Write-Ahead Logging) and checkpoints guarantee durability after hardware failure.</li>\n            <li><strong>Data Independence:</strong> Separates user views from low-level storage modifications.</li>\n        </ol>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q5. What is RDBMS?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>A <b>Relational Database Management System (RDBMS)</b> is a database system founded on E.F. Codd's Relational Model (1970) where data is structured into two-dimensional tables (relations) consisting of rows (tuples) and columns (attributes).</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Core Pillars of RDBMS:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>Relational Integrity:</b> Enforces Entity Integrity (Primary Keys cannot be null) and Referential Integrity (Foreign Keys).</li>\n        <li><b>SQL Interface:</b> Standardized declarative language for DDL, DML, and DCL commands.</li>\n        <li><b>Full ACID Compliance:</b> Guarantees Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability across concurrent transactions.</li>\n        <li><b>Commercial Examples:</b> Oracle Database, Microsoft SQL Server, MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQLite.</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023</span>\n        Q6. List any five applications of DBMS.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Applications:</strong>\n        <ol>\n            <li><strong>Banking:</strong> Customer accounts, loans, ATM transactions, fund transfers.</li>\n            <li><strong>Airlines & Railways:</strong> Real-time reservation systems, flight schedules, ticketing.</li>\n            <li><strong>Universities & Colleges:</strong> Student admissions, course registrations, grades, fee accounts.</li>\n            <li><strong>E-Commerce & Retail:</strong> Online product catalogs, shopping carts, billing, inventory control.</li>\n            <li><strong>Telecommunications:</strong> Call records, monthly billing, subscriber information, network routing.</li>\n        </ol>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023</span>\n        Q7. What are Attributes? Give examples.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> An <strong>Attribute</strong> is a descriptive property or characteristic that describes an entity in an entity set.<br>\n        <strong>Examples for Entity STUDENT:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><em>Simple Attribute:</em> <code>RollNo</code>, <code>Age</code></li>\n            <li><em>Composite Attribute:</em> <code>FullName</code> (composed of <code>FirstName</code>, <code>LastName</code>)</li>\n            <li><em>Multivalued Attribute:</em> <code>PhoneNumbers</code>, <code>Hobbies</code></li>\n            <li><em>Derived Attribute:</em> <code>Age</code> (computed from <code>DateOfBirth</code> and current date)</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q8. Define DML (Data Manipulation Language).\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> <strong>Data Manipulation Language (DML)</strong> provides constructs for accessing, inserting, updating, and querying stored database records. Classified into <em>Procedural DML</em> (user specifies what data and how to retrieve it) and <em>Non-Procedural / Declarative DML</em> (SQL, where user specifies only what data is needed).\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q9. Give examples of Simple and Composite Attributes of an entity.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Examples:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Simple (Atomic) Attribute:</strong> Cannot be divided into subparts (e.g., <code>RollNo = 101</code>, <code>Age = 21</code>, <code>Salary = 50000</code>).</li>\n            <li><strong>Composite Attribute:</strong> Can be divided into smaller sub-components with independent meanings (e.g., <code>Address</code> divided into <code>HouseNo</code>, <code>Street</code>, <code>City</code>, <code>Pincode</code>; or <code>Name</code> into <code>FirstName</code>, <code>LastName</code>).</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019</span>\n        Q10. Name the three levels of Data Abstraction.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Three Levels:</strong>\n        <ol>\n            <li><strong>Physical Level (Lowest):</strong> Describes how data is stored on disk blocks.</li>\n            <li><strong>Logical / Conceptual Level (Middle):</strong> Describes what data is stored and relationships among data.</li>\n            <li><strong>View / External Level (Highest):</strong> Describes customized views for specific user groups.</li>\n        </ol>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019</span>\n        Q11. Distinguish between Functional Dependency and Multivalued Dependency.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Comparison:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Functional Dependency (X &rarr; Y):</strong> For any two tuples with identical <span class=\"math-expr\">X</span> values, their <span class=\"math-expr\">Y</span> values must be identical (single-valued dependency). Basis of 2NF, 3NF, BCNF.</li>\n            <li><strong>Multivalued Dependency (X &twoheadrightarrow; Y):</strong> The presence of <span class=\"math-expr\">(x, y<sub>1</sub>, z<sub>1</sub>)</span> and <span class=\"math-expr\">(x, y<sub>2</sub>, z<sub>2</sub>)</span> implies the presence of <span class=\"math-expr\">(x, y<sub>1</sub>, z<sub>2</sub>)</span> and <span class=\"math-expr\">(x, y<sub>2</sub>, z<sub>1</sub>)</span>, representing independent multi-valued facts. Basis of 4NF.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2018</span>\n        Q12. What do you mean by Relational Database? Describe.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> A <strong>Relational Database</strong> is a collection of data items organized into formal tables (relations) of rows and columns based on first-order predicate logic, where relations are linked together via primary and foreign key constraints.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2018</span>\n        Q13. Briefly explain the terms Security and Authorization.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definitions:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Database Security:</strong> Protection of the database against unauthorized access, malicious attacks, corruption, or destruction.</li>\n            <li><strong>Authorization:</strong> The mechanism that defines and enforces what actions (e.g., SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DROP) a validated user or role is permitted to perform on specific database objects.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2017</span>\n        Q14. Define Tuple, Relation, Attribute, and Domain.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definitions:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Relation:</strong> A two-dimensional table of records.</li>\n            <li><strong>Tuple:</strong> A single row in a relation representing an individual entity instance.</li>\n            <li><strong>Attribute:</strong> A named column describing a specific property.</li>\n            <li><strong>Domain:</strong> The set of allowable atomic values for an attribute.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2017</span>\n        Q15. What are Cardinality and Participation Constraints in ER Modeling?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definitions:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Cardinality Ratio:</strong> Specifies the maximum number of relationship instances that an entity can participate in (1:1, 1:N, M:N).</li>\n            <li><strong>Participation Constraint:</strong> Specifies whether existence of an entity depends on its being related to another entity: <em>Total</em> (double line) or <em>Partial</em> (single line).</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2016</span>\n        Q16. What is a Weak Entity Set? How is it identified?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>A <b>Weak Entity Set</b> is an entity set that does not possess sufficient attributes to form a Primary Key of its own. It is existentially dependent on a parent <b>Identifying (Owner) Entity Set</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Identification & Diagrammatic Representation:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>Discriminator (Partial Key):</b> The set of attributes that distinguishes weak entities belonging to the same owner entity. Underlined with a <b>dashed / dotted line</b>.</li>\n        <li><b>Primary Key Composition:</b> Formed by combining the <b>Primary Key of the Identifying Entity + Discriminator</b>.</li>\n        <li><b>ER Diagram Notation:</b>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Weak Entity Box: <b>Double Rectangle</b>.</li>\n                <li>Identifying Relationship: <b>Double Diamond</b>.</li>\n                <li>Total Participation: <b>Double Line</b>.</li>\n            </ul>\n        </li>\n        <li><i>Example:</i> <code>DEPENDENT(<u>EmpID</u>, <u>DepName</u>, Age, Relation)</code> where <code>EmpID</code> is borrowed from <code>EMPLOYEE</code>.</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2016</span>\n        Q17. Differentiate between Natural Join and Theta Join.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Theta Join (&theta;-Join)</th><th>Natural Join (&bowtie;)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Join Condition</b></td><td>Allows arbitrary comparison operators (<code>=</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&le;</code>, <code>&ge;</code>, <code>&ne;</code>).</td><td>Strictly tests equality (<code>=</code>) on attributes sharing identical names.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Duplicate Columns</b></td><td>Retains both common columns in the output relation (redundant columns).</td><td><b>Automatically eliminates duplicate columns</b>; retains only one instance of shared attributes.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Syntax & Notation</b></td><td><code>R &bowtie;<sub>R.A &theta; S.B</sub> S</code></td><td><code>R &bowtie; S</code> (no explicit condition required).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Relational Equivalence</b></td><td>Equivalent to Cartesian Product followed by Selection: <code>&sigma;<sub>Predicate</sub> (R &times; S)</code>.</td><td>Equivalent to Equi-Join followed by Projection removing duplicate attribute.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2016</span>\n        Q18. Explain COMMIT and ROLLBACK commands in SQL.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><code>COMMIT</code> and <code>ROLLBACK</code> are Transaction Control Language (TCL) commands that preserve the ACID properties of database transactions:</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Command</th><th>Syntax</th><th>Operational Behavior</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>COMMIT</b></td><td><code>COMMIT;</code></td><td>Permanently saves all changes made by the transaction to disk. Once committed, changes are durable and cannot be undone. Releases all held locks.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>ROLLBACK</b></td><td><code>ROLLBACK;</code><br><code>ROLLBACK TO savepoint_name;</code></td><td>Cancels all uncommitted modifications made by the active transaction, restoring the database back to its last clean committed state.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2015</span>\n        Q19. What do you understand by Hashing? Explain with example.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Hashing</b> is an effective file organization and indexing technique that uses a mathematical algorithm (hash function <code>h(k)</code>) to compute direct physical block addresses from search key values.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Key Concepts:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>Hash Function:</b> <code>h(Key) = Address</code>. Desirable properties: uniform distribution and random dispersion to avoid hotspots.</li>\n        <li><b>Collision:</b> Occurs when two distinct keys hash to the same bucket address: <code>h(k<sub>1</sub>) = h(k<sub>2</sub>)</code>.</li>\n        <li><b>Collision Resolution Methods:</b>\n            <ol>\n                <li><b>Open Addressing (Linear Probing):</b> Searches sequentially for the next available bucket.</li>\n                <li><b>Chaining (Overflow Buckets):</b> Maintains a linked list of overflow disk blocks attached to the bucket.</li>\n            </ol>\n        </li>\n        <li><b>Pros:</b> Constant time access <i>O(1)</i> for equality searches (<code>WHERE ID = 101</code>).</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2015</span>\n        Q20. What are Nested SQL Queries? Explain with example.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> A query enclosed inside another SQL query, passed via <code>WHERE</code> or <code>HAVING</code> clauses using operators like <code>IN</code>, <code>EXISTS</code>, <code>ALL</code>, <code>ANY</code>.\n<pre>\nSELECT EmpName FROM Employee \nWHERE Salary > (SELECT AVG(Salary) FROM Employee);\n</pre>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2014</span>\n        Q21. Differentiate between Sequential and Random Access in files.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Differences:</strong> Sequential access reads records consecutively in order from the beginning of the file (<span class=\"math-expr\">O(N)</span>). Random (direct) access jumps immediately to any record block by its calculated address or key index (<span class=\"math-expr\">O(1)</span>).\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2013</span>\n        Q22. What is Data Redundancy?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Data Redundancy</b> refers to the unnecessary duplication of the same piece of information across multiple files or tables in a database system.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Major Problems Caused by Redundancy:</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>Wasted Storage:</b> Storing identical department names and manager addresses thousands of times squanders disk space.</li>\n        <li><b>Data Inconsistency:</b> If duplicate data is modified in one place but missed in another, conflicting records result.</li>\n        <li><b>Operational Anomalies:</b> Causes Insertion, Deletion, and Update anomalies in relational tables.</li>\n    </ol>\n    <p><b>Elimination:</b> Controlled and eliminated through the process of <b>Relational Normalization (1NF through BCNF)</b>.</p>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2013</span>\n        Q23. What is Relational Data Model?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>Proposed by Dr. Edgar F. Codd in 1970, the <b>Relational Data Model</b> represents data in the form of mathematical two-dimensional tables called <b>Relations</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Fundamental Components:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>Relation (Table):</b> A set of rows representing an entity set.</li>\n        <li><b>Tuple (Row):</b> A single record representing an individual entity instance.</li>\n        <li><b>Attribute (Column):</b> A named field representing a property of the relation.</li>\n        <li><b>Domain:</b> The set of all permissible atomic values for an attribute.</li>\n        <li><b>Degree (Arity):</b> Total count of attributes (columns) in a relation.</li>\n        <li><b>Cardinality:</b> Total count of tuples (rows) in a relation.</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n", "counts": {"total": 80, "sec_c": 38, "sec_b": 19, "sec_a": 23}}, "java": {"code": "BCA-502", "paperCode": "18022", "title": "Java Programming & Web Design", "desc": "Complete Deduplicated Question Bank covering JVM Internals, OOPs, Multithreading, Exception Handling, JDBC, Servlets, JSP & Sockets.", "icon": "fa-code", "color": "amber", "pdfBank": "pdfs/BCA502_Java.pdf", "years": [{"year": "2025", "pdf": "pdfs/java_2025.pdf"}, {"year": "2024", "pdf": "pdfs/java_2024.pdf"}, {"year": "2023", "pdf": "pdfs/java_2023.pdf"}, {"year": "2021", "pdf": "pdfs/java_2021.pdf"}, {"year": "2020", "pdf": "pdfs/java_2020.pdf"}, {"year": "2019", "pdf": "pdfs/java_2019.pdf"}, {"year": "2018", "pdf": "pdfs/java_2018.pdf"}, {"year": "2017", "pdf": "pdfs/java_2017.pdf"}, {"year": "2016", "pdf": "pdfs/java_2016.pdf"}], "contentHtml": "<div class=\"sec-title\" data-sec=\"sec-c\">Section – C (Detailed / Long Answer Questions &bull; 15 Marks &bull; 22 Questions)</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2024, 2023, 2021, 2020, 2019, 2018, 2017, 2016 &bull; Repeated 9&times;</span>\n        Q1. Explain Applet Life Cycle. Write a program to demonstrate a simple Java applet to display an image. Compare Applets over HTML.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Applet Image Display Code:</strong>\n<pre>\nimport java.applet.*;\nimport java.awt.*;\n\n/* &lt;applet code=\"ImageApplet.class\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\"&gt;&lt;/applet&gt; */\npublic class ImageApplet extends Applet {\n    Image img;\n    public void init() {\n        img = getImage(getDocumentBase(), \"college_logo.png\");\n    }\n    public void paint(Graphics g) {\n        g.drawImage(img, 20, 20, this);\n        g.drawString(\"CCSU BCA V Semester\", 20, 260);\n    }\n}\n</pre>\n        <strong>Comparison over HTML:</strong> Applets provide dynamic client-side computation, graphical interactivity, and local state management that static HTML cannot natively provide.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2024, 2023, 2021, 2020, 2019, 2018, 2017, 2016 &bull; Repeated 9&times;</span>\n        Q2. Explain Servlet Life Cycle and compare it with JSP Life Cycle. Write a complete HttpServlet code example.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Servlet Life Cycle Methods:</strong>\n        <ol>\n            <li><code>init(ServletConfig config)</code>: Loads servlet class, creates instance, and initializes parameters (called once).</li>\n            <li><code>service(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res)</code>: Dispatches to <code>doGet()</code> or <code>doPost()</code> for every incoming HTTP request.</li>\n            <li><code>destroy()</code>: Invoked when container unloads the servlet, releasing database and memory resources.</li>\n        </ol>\n        <strong>Servlet Implementation Example:</strong>\n<pre>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport javax.servlet.*;\nimport javax.servlet.http.*;\n\npublic class WelcomeServlet extends HttpServlet {\n    public void doGet(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) \n            throws ServletException, IOException {\n        response.setContentType(\"text/html\");\n        PrintWriter out = response.getWriter();\n        String user = request.getParameter(\"username\");\n        out.println(\"&lt;html&gt;&lt;body&gt;\");\n        out.println(\"&lt;h2&gt;Welcome, \" + user + \"!&lt;/h2&gt;\");\n        out.println(\"&lt;p&gt;Server Response Time: \" + new java.util.Date() + \"&lt;/p&gt;\");\n        out.println(\"&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;\");\n    }\n}\n</pre>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2023, 2020, 2019, 2018, 2017, 2016 &bull; Repeated 7&times;</span>\n        Q3. Explain Exception Handling mechanism in Java with keywords: try, catch, finally, throw, and throws.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Exception Handling</b> in Java is a robust mechanism to handle runtime errors, preventing abnormal program termination and maintaining regular application flow.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>The Exception Hierarchy:</b> All exception types inherit from <code>java.lang.Throwable</code>. It divides into:</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>Error:</b> Irrecoverable conditions (e.g., <code>OutOfMemoryError</code>, <code>StackOverflowError</code>).</li>\n        <li><b>Exception:</b> Conditions that a program should catch and handle. Subdivided into:\n            <ul>\n                <li><i>Checked Exceptions:</i> Checked at compile time (e.g., <code>IOException</code>, <code>SQLException</code>, <code>ClassNotFoundException</code>).</li>\n                <li><i>Unchecked Exceptions (Runtime):</i> Inherit from <code>RuntimeException</code> (e.g., <code>NullPointerException</code>, <code>ArithmeticException</code>, <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>).</li>\n            </ul>\n        </li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">The Five Core Keywords:</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Keyword</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Syntax Example</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>try</code></td><td>Encloses code that might throw an exception.</td><td><code>try { int a = 10/0; }</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>catch</code></td><td>Handles the specific exception thrown by <code>try</code> block.</td><td><code>catch(ArithmeticException e) { ... }</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>finally</code></td><td>Always executes whether exception occurs or not; used for resource cleanup.</td><td><code>finally { reader.close(); }</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>throw</code></td><td>Explicitly throws a single exception instance.</td><td><code>throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Invalid age\");</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>throws</code></td><td>Declares exceptions a method may throw to callers in method signature.</td><td><code>void readFile() throws IOException { ... }</code></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Complete Working Java Program Demonstrating All 5 Keywords:</p>\n<div class=\"code-box\">public class ExceptionMasterDemo {\n    // throws: Declares potential checked exception\n    static void checkEligibility(int age) throws ArithmeticException {\n        if (age < 18) {\n            // throw: Explicitly throws an exception object\n            throw new ArithmeticException(\"Underage: Age must be 18+ to vote.\");\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"Eligible for voting.\");\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        try {\n            System.out.println(\"Opening database resource...\");\n            checkEligibility(16); // Will trigger exception\n        } catch (ArithmeticException ex) {\n            System.err.println(\"Caught Exception: \" + ex.getMessage());\n        } catch (Exception ex) {\n            System.err.println(\"Generic Handler: \" + ex);\n        } finally {\n            // finally block is GUARANTEED to execute\n            System.out.println(\"Finally Block: Resources and connections safely closed.\");\n        }\n    }\n}</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2020, 2019, 2018, 2017, 2016 &bull; Repeated 6&times;</span>\n        Q4. What is the difference between Process-based and Thread-based multitasking in Java?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Comparison:</strong>\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Process-Based Multitasking</th><th>Thread-Based Multitasking</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n                <tr><td>Granularity</td><td>Heavyweight; each process has its own separate address space.</td><td>Lightweight; threads share the same address space in a single process.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Context Switching</td><td>High overhead; managed by operating system kernel.</td><td>Low overhead; switches quickly within the JVM.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Communication</td><td>Requires Inter-Process Communication (IPC, sockets, pipes).</td><td>Direct shared memory, wait(), notify(), synchronized blocks.</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n        </table>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2023, 2020, 2018, 2017, 2016 &bull; Repeated 6&times;</span>\n        Q5. Define Thread in Java. Explain how to create threads using (a) Thread class and (b) Runnable interface. Write a complete multithreaded program.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Multithreading in Java:</strong>\n<pre>\n// Approach 1: Extending Thread class\nclass NumberPrinter extends Thread {\n    public void run() {\n        for(int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n            System.out.println(\"Thread 1: \" + i);\n            try { Thread.sleep(500); } catch(Exception e) {}\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// Approach 2: Implementing Runnable interface\nclass LetterPrinter implements Runnable {\n    public void run() {\n        for(char c = 'A'; c <= 'E'; c++) {\n            System.out.println(\"Thread 2: \" + c);\n            try { Thread.sleep(500); } catch(Exception e) {}\n        }\n    }\n}\n\npublic class MultiThreadDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        NumberPrinter t1 = new NumberPrinter();\n        Thread t2 = new Thread(new LetterPrinter());\n        t1.start(); // Spawns new thread\n        t2.start();\n    }\n}\n</pre>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2023, 2018, 2016 &bull; Repeated 4&times;</span>\n        Q6. What are Packages in Java? How is a user-defined package created and imported? Explain with complete code example.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>A <b>Package</b> in Java is a container or namespace that groups related classes, interfaces, and sub-packages together. Packages prevent naming conflicts, control access visibility, and provide modular software architecture.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Types of Packages:</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>Built-in Packages:</b> Provided by Java SE API (e.g., <code>java.lang</code>, <code>java.util</code>, <code>java.io</code>, <code>java.sql</code>, <code>java.net</code>, <code>java.awt</code>).</li>\n        <li><b>User-Defined Packages:</b> Created by developers to organize custom application modules.</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Creating and Importing a User-Defined Package:</p>\n<div class=\"code-box\">// Step 1: File saved as 'Calculator.java' in folder 'com/ccsu/math/'\npackage com.ccsu.math;\n\npublic class Calculator {\n    public int add(int a, int b) {\n        return a + b;\n    }\n    public int multiply(int a, int b) {\n        return a * b;\n    }\n}</div>\n<div class=\"code-box\">// Step 2: Main application importing the custom package\npackage com.ccsu.app;\n\nimport com.ccsu.math.Calculator; // Importing specific class\n\npublic class MainApp {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Calculator calc = new Calculator();\n        System.out.println(\"Addition: \" + calc.add(15, 25));       // Output: 40\n        System.out.println(\"Multiplication: \" + calc.multiply(6, 7)); // Output: 42\n    }\n}</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Compilation and Execution with CLASSPATH:</p>\n<div class=\"code-box\">javac -d . Calculator.java    # -d creates the directory structure com/ccsu/math/\njavac -d . MainApp.java\njava com.ccsu.app.MainApp</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Package Access Protection Matrix:</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Modifier</th><th>Same Class</th><th>Same Package</th><th>Subclass (Different Pkg)</th><th>World (Different Pkg)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>public</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>protected</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>default</code> (package-private)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>private</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2017, 2016 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q7. What is JDBC? Explain the Four Types of JDBC Drivers with architectural diagrams and characteristics.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> <strong>Java Database Connectivity (JDBC)</strong> is an industry-standard Java API that enables Java programs to interact with relational databases using SQL queries.<br><br>\n        <strong>Four Types of JDBC Drivers:</strong>\n        <ol>\n            <li><strong>Type-1: JDBC-ODBC Bridge Driver:</strong> Translates JDBC calls to ODBC calls. Requires ODBC driver installation on every client machine. Obsolete and slow.</li>\n            <li><strong>Type-2: Native-API / Partially Java Driver:</strong> Converts JDBC calls directly into native C/C++ vendor database client libraries (e.g., Oracle OCI). Requires client-side installation.</li>\n            <li><strong>Type-3: Network Protocol / All-Java Middleware Driver:</strong> Translates JDBC calls into an intermediate server middleware protocol, which then talks to multiple databases. Flexible for 3-tier architectures.</li>\n            <li><strong>Type-4: Thin Driver / Direct-to-Database Pure Java Driver:</strong> Converts JDBC calls directly into vendor-specific database network protocols via TCP/IP sockets. 100% pure Java, zero client installation, highest performance (Modern standard!).</li>\n        </ol>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2024, 2017 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q8. What are Layout Managers in AWT/Swing? Explain BorderLayout, FlowLayout, and GridLayout with examples.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Layout Managers:</strong> Automatically position, size, and rearrange GUI components inside containers regardless of screen resolution or OS window scaling.<br>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>FlowLayout:</strong> Arranges components sequentially from left to right, wrapping to the next line when space runs out. Default for <code>Panel</code> and <code>Applet</code>.</li>\n            <li><strong>BorderLayout:</strong> Divides container into 5 regions: <code>NORTH</code>, <code>SOUTH</code>, <code>EAST</code>, <code>WEST</code>, and <code>CENTER</code>. Default for <code>Frame</code> and <code>JFrame</code>.</li>\n            <li><strong>GridLayout:</strong> Arranges components into a rectangular grid of equal-sized rows and columns.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2019, 2018 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q9. What is JDBC? Write a complete JDBC program to insert and display records of employees using PreparedStatement.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Complete PreparedStatement JDBC Program:</strong>\n<pre>\nimport java.sql.*;\n\npublic class EmployeeJDBC {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String url = \"jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/company_db\";\n        String user = \"root\";\n        String password = \"password\";\n\n        try {\n            Class.forName(\"com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver\");\n            Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, password);\n\n            // 1. Insert record using PreparedStatement\n            String insertSql = \"INSERT INTO Employee(EmpID, Name, Salary) VALUES(?, ?, ?)\";\n            PreparedStatement psInsert = conn.prepareStatement(insertSql);\n            psInsert.setInt(1, 101);\n            psInsert.setString(2, \"Rahul Sharma\");\n            psInsert.setDouble(3, 55000.00);\n            int rows = psInsert.executeUpdate();\n            System.out.println(\"Inserted rows: \" + rows);\n\n            // 2. Display all employee records\n            String selectSql = \"SELECT EmpID, Name, Salary FROM Employee\";\n            Statement stmt = conn.createStatement();\n            ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery(selectSql);\n\n            System.out.println(\"\\n--- EMPLOYEE RECORDS ---\");\n            while(rs.next()) {\n                System.out.println(rs.getInt(\"EmpID\") + \"\\t\" + \n                                   rs.getString(\"Name\") + \"\\t\" + \n                                   rs.getDouble(\"Salary\"));\n            }\n            conn.close();\n        } catch(Exception e) {\n            e.printStackTrace();\n        }\n    }\n}\n</pre>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021, 2018 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q10. Explain AWT Event Handling with Delegation Event Model. Write a program to handle Button click events.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 22.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>The <b>Delegation Event Model</b> is the standard design pattern in Java AWT and Swing for processing GUI user interactions. It decouples the UI event source from the event listener.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Three Core Components of the Delegation Model:</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>Event Source:</b> The GUI component that generates the event when state changes (e.g., <code>Button</code>, <code>TextField</code>, <code>Checkbox</code>).</li>\n        <li><b>Event Object:</b> Encapsulates all event details (e.g., <code>ActionEvent</code>, <code>MouseEvent</code>, <code>KeyEvent</code>) inheriting from <code>java.util.EventObject</code>.</li>\n        <li><b>Event Listener:</b> An interface containing callback methods (e.g., <code>ActionListener</code>, <code>MouseListener</code>). The listener registers with the source using registration methods like <code>addActionListener()</code>.</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Complete Working Java Program (Button Click Event Handling):</p>\n<div class=\"code-box\">import java.awt.*;\nimport java.awt.event.*;\n\npublic class ButtonClickEventDemo extends Frame implements ActionListener {\n    private Label label;\n    private Button btnClick, btnExit;\n    private int counter = 0;\n\n    public ButtonClickEventDemo() {\n        // Frame Configuration\n        setTitle(\"AWT Delegation Event Model Demo\");\n        setSize(400, 200);\n        setLayout(new FlowLayout());\n\n        // Create Components\n        label = new Label(\"Button clicks: 0\");\n        btnClick = new Button(\"Click Me!\");\n        btnExit = new Button(\"Exit\");\n\n        // Register Event Listeners (Delegation)\n        btnClick.addActionListener(this);\n        btnExit.addActionListener(this);\n\n        // Add to Frame Container\n        add(label);\n        add(btnClick);\n        add(btnExit);\n\n        // Window closing adapter\n        addWindowListener(new WindowAdapter() {\n            public void windowClosing(WindowEvent e) {\n                System.exit(0);\n            }\n        });\n\n        setVisible(true);\n    }\n\n    // Callback method implementation for ActionListener\n    @Override\n    public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) {\n        if (e.getSource() == btnClick) {\n            counter++;\n            label.setText(\"Button clicks: \" + counter);\n        } else if (e.getSource() == btnExit) {\n            System.exit(0);\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        new ButtonClickEventDemo();\n    }\n}</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2020 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q11. Discuss JSP in detail. What are JSP Directives? Explain various types of directives with examples.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>JSP Directives (<code>&lt;%@ directive attribute=\"value\" %&gt;</code>):</strong>\n        <ol>\n            <li><strong>page Directive:</strong> Controls page-level settings:\n                <code>&lt;%@ page import=\"java.util.*, java.sql.*\" contentType=\"text/html\" errorPage=\"error.jsp\" %&gt;</code>\n            </li>\n            <li><strong>include Directive:</strong> Inserts static files at translation time:\n                <code>&lt;%@ include file=\"header.jsp\" %&gt;</code>\n            </li>\n            <li><strong>taglib Directive:</strong> Imports custom tag libraries (JSTL):\n                <code>&lt;%@ taglib uri=\"http://java.sun.com/jsp/jstl/core\" prefix=\"c\" %&gt;</code>\n            </li>\n        </ol>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023, 2020 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q12. What is JDBC? Explain JDBC Architecture and execute a database SELECT query.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Java Database Connectivity (JDBC)</b> is a standard Java API (part of <code>java.sql</code> and <code>javax.sql</code> packages) that provides uniform, vendor-neutral communication between Java applications and relational database engines.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>JDBC Architecture:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>Application:</b> Java code issuing SQL queries via JDBC API interfaces.</li>\n        <li><b>JDBC Driver Manager:</b> Backbone component that loads database-specific drivers and establishes connections using connection strings.</li>\n        <li><b>JDBC Drivers:</b> Translates standard JDBC API calls into vendor-specific database protocols.\n            <ol>\n                <li><b>Type-1 (JDBC-ODBC Bridge):</b> Translates JDBC to ODBC; slow, deprecated.</li>\n                <li><b>Type-2 (Native API):</b> Converts JDBC to native C/C++ client libraries; client installation needed.</li>\n                <li><b>Type-3 (Network Protocol):</b> Communicates with middle-tier application server.</li>\n                <li><b>Type-4 (Thin Driver - Pure Java):</b> Directly speaks database socket protocol; fast, platform-independent, widely used (e.g., MySQL Connector/J).</li>\n            </ol>\n        </li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Complete Working Java Program (Database SELECT & INSERT Query):</p>\n<div class=\"code-box\">import java.sql.*;\n\npublic class JdbcFullDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String url = \"jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/college_db\";\n        String user = \"root\";\n        String pass = \"password\";\n\n        // Step 1 & 2: Load Driver & Open Connection\n        try (Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, pass)) {\n            System.out.println(\"Connected to MySQL Database successfully!\");\n\n            // Step 3: Prepared Statement INSERT\n            String insertSql = \"INSERT INTO students(roll_no, name, branch) VALUES (?, ?, ?)\";\n            try (PreparedStatement psInsert = con.prepareStatement(insertSql)) {\n                psInsert.setInt(1, 105);\n                psInsert.setString(2, \"Ananya Verma\");\n                psInsert.setString(3, \"BCA\");\n                int rowsInserted = psInsert.executeUpdate();\n                System.out.println(\"Rows inserted: \" + rowsInserted);\n            }\n\n            // Step 4: Execute SELECT Query\n            String selectSql = \"SELECT roll_no, name, branch FROM students\";\n            try (Statement stmt = con.createStatement();\n                 ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery(selectSql)) {\n                System.out.println(\"\\n--- Student Records ---\");\n                while (rs.next()) {\n                    int roll = rs.getInt(\"roll_no\");\n                    String name = rs.getString(\"name\");\n                    String branch = rs.getString(\"branch\");\n                    System.out.println(\"Roll: \" + roll + \" | Name: \" + name + \" | Branch: \" + branch);\n                }\n            }\n        } catch (SQLException ex) {\n            System.err.println(\"JDBC Error: \" + ex.getMessage());\n        }\n    }\n}</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021, 2016 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q13. Explain Client-Server Socket Programming in Java with complete code.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>Socket programming in Java (via <code>java.net</code>) enables two computers to communicate over a TCP/IP network using bidirectional stream connections.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Key Socket Classes:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><code>ServerSocket</code>: Used on the server to listen on a designated port and accept incoming connections via <code>accept()</code>.</li>\n        <li><code>Socket</code>: Used by the client to initiate connection to the server's IP address and port, and returned by <code>accept()</code> on server for data I/O.</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">1. Server Program (TCPServer.java):</p>\n<div class=\"code-box\">import java.io.*;\nimport java.net.*;\n\npublic class TCPServer {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int port = 5000;\n        try (ServerSocket serverSocket = new ServerSocket(port)) {\n            System.out.println(\"Server listening on port \" + port + \"... Waiting for client.\");\n            \n            Socket socket = serverSocket.accept(); // Blocks until client connects\n            System.out.println(\"Client connected: \" + socket.getInetAddress());\n\n            BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(socket.getInputStream()));\n            PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(socket.getOutputStream(), true);\n\n            String clientMsg = in.readLine();\n            System.out.println(\"Received from Client: \" + clientMsg);\n\n            // Echo back uppercase\n            out.println(\"SERVER ECHO: \" + clientMsg.toUpperCase());\n            socket.close();\n        } catch (IOException e) {\n            e.printStackTrace();\n        }\n    }\n}</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">2. Client Program (TCPClient.java):</p>\n<div class=\"code-box\">import java.io.*;\nimport java.net.*;\n\npublic class TCPClient {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String host = \"127.0.0.1\";\n        int port = 5000;\n        try (Socket socket = new Socket(host, port)) {\n            PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(socket.getOutputStream(), true);\n            BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(socket.getInputStream()));\n\n            out.println(\"Hello CCSU Server from BCA 5th Sem!\");\n            String response = in.readLine();\n            System.out.println(\"Server Response: \" + response);\n        } catch (IOException e) {\n            e.printStackTrace();\n        }\n    }\n}</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021</span>\n        Q14. Explain JDBC Architecture and write a program to update employee salaries based on department.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[22.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Program:</strong>\n<pre>\nimport java.sql.*;\n\npublic class UpdateSalaryDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        try {\n            Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(\"jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/db\", \"root\", \"pass\");\n            PreparedStatement ps = con.prepareStatement(\"UPDATE Employee SET Salary = Salary * 1.10 WHERE Dept = ?\");\n            ps.setString(1, \"IT\");\n            int rows = ps.executeUpdate();\n            System.out.println(\"Salaries updated for \" + rows + \" employees.\");\n            con.close();\n        } catch(Exception e) { e.printStackTrace(); }\n    }\n}\n</pre>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025</span>\n        Q15. Write an HTML code to create a User Registration Web Form containing: Username, Date of Birth, Address, Gender (Radio buttons), Email-ID, Mobile Number, and Submit/Reset buttons.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>HTML Code:</strong>\n<pre>\n&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;User Registration Form&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h2&gt;Student / User Registration Form&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;form action=\"register\" method=\"POST\"&gt;\n        &lt;table cellpadding=\"6\"&gt;\n            &lt;tr&gt;\n                &lt;td&gt;User Name:&lt;/td&gt;\n                &lt;td&gt;&lt;input type=\"text\" name=\"username\" required /&gt;&lt;/td&gt;\n            &lt;/tr&gt;\n            &lt;tr&gt;\n                &lt;td&gt;Date of Birth:&lt;/td&gt;\n                &lt;td&gt;&lt;input type=\"date\" name=\"dob\" required /&gt;&lt;/td&gt;\n            &lt;/tr&gt;\n            &lt;tr&gt;\n                &lt;td&gt;Address:&lt;/td&gt;\n                &lt;td&gt;&lt;textarea name=\"address\" rows=\"3\" cols=\"30\"&gt;&lt;/textarea&gt;&lt;/td&gt;\n            &lt;/tr&gt;\n            &lt;tr&gt;\n                &lt;td&gt;Gender:&lt;/td&gt;\n                &lt;td&gt;\n                    &lt;input type=\"radio\" name=\"gender\" value=\"Male\" /&gt; Male\n                    &lt;input type=\"radio\" name=\"gender\" value=\"Female\" /&gt; Female\n                &lt;/td&gt;\n            &lt;/tr&gt;\n            &lt;tr&gt;\n                &lt;td&gt;Email-ID:&lt;/td&gt;\n                &lt;td&gt;&lt;input type=\"email\" name=\"email\" required /&gt;&lt;/td&gt;\n            &lt;/tr&gt;\n            &lt;tr&gt;\n                &lt;td&gt;Mobile Number:&lt;/td&gt;\n                &lt;td&gt;&lt;input type=\"tel\" name=\"mobile\" pattern=\"[0-9]{10}\" required /&gt;&lt;/td&gt;\n            &lt;/tr&gt;\n            &lt;tr&gt;\n                &lt;td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\"&gt;\n                    &lt;input type=\"submit\" value=\"Register Now\" /&gt;\n                    &lt;input type=\"reset\" value=\"Clear Form\" /&gt;\n                &lt;/td&gt;\n            &lt;/tr&gt;\n        &lt;/table&gt;\n    &lt;/form&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n</pre>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q16. What are Packages? Explain Java API Packages. List any five methods of the String class with examples.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>String Class Methods:</strong>\n        <ol>\n            <li><code>length()</code>: Returns character count &rarr; <code>\"Hello\".length() == 5</code>.</li>\n            <li><code>charAt(int index)</code>: Returns character at index &rarr; <code>\"Hello\".charAt(1) == 'e'</code>.</li>\n            <li><code>substring(int begin, int end)</code>: Extracts substring &rarr; <code>\"Java\".substring(0, 2) == \"Ja\"</code>.</li>\n            <li><code>toUpperCase()</code>: Converts to uppercase &rarr; <code>\"bca\".toUpperCase() == \"BCA\"</code>.</li>\n            <li><code>equals(Object another)</code>: Compares string contents &rarr; <code>\"Java\".equals(\"Java\") == true</code>.</li>\n        </ol>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023</span>\n        Q17. Explain HTML form controls and write code for an Admission Form.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>HTML Forms collect user input on web applications and submit it to web servers (Servlets/JSP) via HTTP <code>GET</code> or <code>POST</code> methods.</p>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Complete Code for BCA Admission Form:</p>\n<div class=\"code-box\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;CCSU BCA Admission Form&lt;/title&gt;\n    &lt;style&gt;\n        body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; background: #f0f2f5; padding: 20px; }\n        .form-card { max-width: 500px; margin: auto; background: white; padding: 25px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); }\n        .form-group { margin-bottom: 14px; }\n        label { display: block; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px; font-size: 13px; }\n        input, select, textarea { width: 100%; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ccc; border-radius: 4px; box-sizing: border-box; }\n        .btn-submit { background: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 10px 16px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-weight: bold; width: 100%; }\n    &lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n&lt;div class=\"form-card\"&gt;\n    &lt;h2 style=\"text-align:center; color:#1e3a8a;\"&gt;BCA Admission Registration&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;form action=\"AdmissionServlet\" method=\"POST\"&gt;\n        &lt;div class=\"form-group\"&gt;\n            &lt;label&gt;Full Student Name:&lt;/label&gt;\n            &lt;input type=\"text\" name=\"fullName\" required placeholder=\"Enter your name\"&gt;\n        &lt;/div&gt;\n        &lt;div class=\"form-group\"&gt;\n            &lt;label&gt;Email Address:&lt;/label&gt;\n            &lt;input type=\"email\" name=\"email\" required placeholder=\"name@domain.com\"&gt;\n        &lt;/div&gt;\n        &lt;div class=\"form-group\"&gt;\n            &lt;label&gt;Date of Birth:&lt;/label&gt;\n            &lt;input type=\"date\" name=\"dob\" required&gt;\n        &lt;/div&gt;\n        &lt;div class=\"form-group\"&gt;\n            &lt;label&gt;Gender:&lt;/label&gt;\n            &lt;input type=\"radio\" name=\"gender\" value=\"Male\" checked style=\"width:auto;\"&gt; Male\n            &lt;input type=\"radio\" name=\"gender\" value=\"Female\" style=\"width:auto; margin-left:15px;\"&gt; Female\n        &lt;/div&gt;\n        &lt;div class=\"form-group\"&gt;\n            &lt;label&gt;Course Stream:&lt;/label&gt;\n            &lt;select name=\"course\"&gt;\n                &lt;option value=\"BCA\"&gt;Bachelor of Computer Applications (BCA)&lt;/option&gt;\n                &lt;option value=\"MCA\"&gt;Master of Computer Applications (MCA)&lt;/option&gt;\n            &lt;/select&gt;\n        &lt;/div&gt;\n        &lt;div class=\"form-group\"&gt;\n            &lt;label&gt;Address:&lt;/label&gt;\n            &lt;textarea name=\"address\" rows=\"3\"&gt;&lt;/textarea&gt;\n        &lt;/div&gt;\n        &lt;button type=\"submit\" class=\"btn-submit\"&gt;Submit Application&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/form&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023</span>\n        Q18. Explain JSP implicit objects and session tracking in Web Applications.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>JavaServer Pages (JSP)</b> provides <b>9 implicit objects</b> automatically instantiated by the JSP container and directly accessible inside scriptlets and expressions without explicit declaration:</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Implicit Object</th><th>Underlying Class / Interface</th><th>Scope & Purpose</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>request</code></td><td><code>HttpServletRequest</code></td><td>Encapsulates incoming HTTP request parameters, headers, cookies.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>response</code></td><td><code>HttpServletResponse</code></td><td>Encapsulates HTTP response to client (cookies, redirects).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>out</code></td><td><code>JspWriter</code></td><td>Writes buffered HTML output stream back to browser.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>session</code></td><td><code>HttpSession</code></td><td>Tracks user session across multiple page requests.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>application</code></td><td><code>ServletContext</code></td><td>Global application context shared by all users.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>config</code></td><td><code>ServletConfig</code></td><td>Configuration initialization parameters for this JSP page.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>pageContext</code></td><td><code>PageContext</code></td><td>Provides access to all scopes and attributes.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>page</code></td><td><code>java.lang.Object</code></td><td>Synonym for <code>this</code> (the generated servlet instance).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>exception</code></td><td><code>java.lang.Throwable</code></td><td>Available only on error pages (<code>&lt;%@ page isErrorPage=\"true\" %&gt;</code>).</td></tr>\n</table>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Session Tracking Techniques in Web Applications:</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>HttpSession API:</b> Standard method (<code>session.setAttribute(\"user\", u);</code>). Tracks user using <code>JSESSIONID</code> cookie.</li>\n        <li><b>Cookies:</b> Small text files stored on client machine sent with HTTP requests.</li>\n        <li><b>URL Rewriting:</b> Appends <code>;jsessionid=12345</code> to URLs when cookies are disabled.</li>\n        <li><b>Hidden Form Fields:</b> Embedding hidden HTML inputs (<code>&lt;input type=\"hidden\" name=\"sessionId\" value=\"XYZ\"&gt;</code>).</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q19. Write HTML code for a complete Web Form with Table formatting and Validation.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>Web forms collect user input and submit data to server-side scripts. Table-based styling ensures clean alignment of labels and input fields, while JavaScript provides client-side validation before HTTP submission.</p>\n<div class=\"code-box\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;Validated User Registration Form&lt;/title&gt;\n    &lt;script&gt;\n        function validateForm() {\n            var name = document.forms[\"regForm\"][\"uname\"].value;\n            var email = document.forms[\"regForm\"][\"email\"].value;\n            var pass = document.forms[\"regForm\"][\"pwd\"].value;\n            var mobile = document.forms[\"regForm\"][\"mob\"].value;\n\n            if (name.trim() == \"\") {\n                alert(\"Validation Error: Full Name is required!\");\n                return false;\n            }\n            if (email.indexOf(\"@\") == -1 || email.indexOf(\".\") == -1) {\n                alert(\"Validation Error: Enter a valid email address!\");\n                return false;\n            }\n            if (pass.length &lt; 6) {\n                alert(\"Validation Error: Password must be at least 6 characters long!\");\n                return false;\n            }\n            if (!/^[0-9]{10}$/.test(mobile)) {\n                alert(\"Validation Error: Mobile number must be exactly 10 digits!\");\n                return false;\n            }\n            alert(\"Validation Succeeded: Submitting data to server...\");\n            return true;\n        }\n    &lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif; background:#f8fafc; padding:30px;\"&gt;\n    &lt;div style=\"max-width:550px; margin:auto; background:white; padding:25px; border-radius:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"&gt;\n        &lt;h2 style=\"text-align:center; color:#1e3a8a;\"&gt;Student Portal Registration&lt;/h2&gt;\n        &lt;form name=\"regForm\" action=\"RegisterServlet\" method=\"POST\" onsubmit=\"return validateForm()\"&gt;\n            &lt;table cellpadding=\"8\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"width:100%;\"&gt;\n                &lt;tr&gt;\n                    &lt;td&gt;&lt;b&gt;Username:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/td&gt;\n                    &lt;td&gt;&lt;input type=\"text\" name=\"uname\" style=\"width:100%; padding:6px;\"&gt;&lt;/td&gt;\n                &lt;/tr&gt;\n                &lt;tr&gt;\n                    &lt;td&gt;&lt;b&gt;Email Address:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/td&gt;\n                    &lt;td&gt;&lt;input type=\"text\" name=\"email\" style=\"width:100%; padding:6px;\"&gt;&lt;/td&gt;\n                &lt;/tr&gt;\n                &lt;tr&gt;\n                    &lt;td&gt;&lt;b&gt;Password:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/td&gt;\n                    &lt;td&gt;&lt;input type=\"password\" name=\"pwd\" style=\"width:100%; padding:6px;\"&gt;&lt;/td&gt;\n                &lt;/tr&gt;\n                &lt;tr&gt;\n                    &lt;td&gt;&lt;b&gt;Mobile (10 digits):&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/td&gt;\n                    &lt;td&gt;&lt;input type=\"text\" name=\"mob\" style=\"width:100%; padding:6px;\"&gt;&lt;/td&gt;\n                &lt;/tr&gt;\n                &lt;tr&gt;\n                    &lt;td&gt;&lt;b&gt;Gender:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/td&gt;\n                    &lt;td&gt;\n                        &lt;input type=\"radio\" name=\"gender\" value=\"Male\" checked&gt; Male\n                        &lt;input type=\"radio\" name=\"gender\" value=\"Female\" style=\"margin-left:15px;\"&gt; Female\n                    &lt;/td&gt;\n                &lt;/tr&gt;\n                &lt;tr&gt;\n                    &lt;td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center; padding-top:15px;\"&gt;\n                        &lt;input type=\"submit\" value=\"Register Now\" style=\"background:#2563eb; color:white; padding:8px 20px; border:none; border-radius:4px; font-weight:bold; cursor:pointer;\"&gt;\n                        &lt;input type=\"reset\" value=\"Clear Form\" style=\"background:#64748b; color:white; padding:8px 15px; border:none; border-radius:4px; margin-left:10px; cursor:pointer;\"&gt;\n                    &lt;/td&gt;\n                &lt;/tr&gt;\n            &lt;/table&gt;\n        &lt;/form&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q20. Explain Servlets, HttpServletRequest, and HttpServletResponse with complete GET/POST demonstration.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>A <b>Servlet</b> is a server-side Java program running inside a Servlet Container (e.g., Apache Tomcat) to handle incoming dynamic HTTP web requests and produce responses.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Servlet Life Cycle:</b></p>\n    <p><code>init(ServletConfig config) &rarr; service(request, response) &rarr; destroy()</code></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><code>init()</code>: Called once upon servlet class loading into memory.</li>\n        <li><code>service()</code>: Dispatches requests to <code>doGet()</code> or <code>doPost()</code> per HTTP method for every incoming concurrent request in a separate thread.</li>\n        <li><code>destroy()</code>: Called once before servlet is removed from memory.</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Complete Servlet Code Demonstrating GET and POST:</p>\n<div class=\"code-box\">import java.io.*;\nimport javax.servlet.*;\nimport javax.servlet.http.*;\n\npublic class UserAuthServlet extends HttpServlet {\n    // Handles HTTP GET Requests\n    @Override\n    protected void doGet(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res) throws IOException {\n        res.setContentType(\"text/html\");\n        PrintWriter out = res.getWriter();\n        out.println(\"&lt;h2&gt;GET Request Received: Please submit credentials via POST form.&lt;/h2&gt;\");\n    }\n\n    // Handles HTTP POST Requests (Secure Data Submission)\n    @Override\n    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res) throws IOException {\n        res.setContentType(\"text/html\");\n        PrintWriter out = res.getWriter();\n\n        String user = req.getParameter(\"username\");\n        String pass = req.getParameter(\"password\");\n\n        if (\"admin\".equals(user) && \"ccsu123\".equals(pass)) {\n            // Establish session\n            HttpSession session = req.getSession();\n            session.setAttribute(\"currentUser\", user);\n            out.println(\"&lt;h2 style='color:green;'&gt;Authentication Successful! Welcome, \" + user + \"&lt;/h2&gt;\");\n        } else {\n            out.println(\"&lt;h2 style='color:red;'&gt;Invalid Credentials! Access Denied.&lt;/h2&gt;\");\n        }\n    }\n}</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019</span>\n        Q21. Explain Event Handling in Java AWT/Swing with MouseListener and ActionListener.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>In Java GUI programming, user interactions generate event objects intercepted by registered listener interfaces.</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Listener Interface</th><th>Package</th><th>Triggering Event</th><th>Key Methods to Implement</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>ActionListener</code></td><td><code>java.awt.event</code></td><td>Button clicks, Enter in text fields, menu selection.</td><td><code>void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e)</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>MouseListener</code></td><td><code>java.awt.event</code></td><td>Mouse clicks, presses, releases, enters, exits.</td><td>\n        <code>mouseClicked(MouseEvent e)</code><br>\n        <code>mousePressed(MouseEvent e)</code><br>\n        <code>mouseReleased(MouseEvent e)</code><br>\n        <code>mouseEntered(MouseEvent e)</code><br>\n        <code>mouseExited(MouseEvent e)</code>\n    </td></tr>\n</table>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>MouseAdapter:</b> Implementing <code>MouseListener</code> forces implementation of all 5 methods. <code>MouseAdapter</code> is an abstract convenience class providing empty implementations of all 5 methods, allowing developers to override only the required method (e.g., only <code>mouseClicked()</code>).</p>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019</span>\n        Q22. Write HTML and CSS code to design a responsive institutional web page.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>A responsive institutional web page adapts dynamically across desktops, tablets, and smartphones using modern CSS Media Queries and flexible layouts.</p>\n<div class=\"code-box\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=\"UTF-8\"&gt;\n    &lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\"&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;Chaudhary Charan Singh University &bull; Dept of Computer Applications&lt;/title&gt;\n    &lt;style&gt;\n        * { box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; padding: 0; font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, sans-serif; }\n        header { background: #1e3a8a; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; }\n        nav { background: #0f172a; display: flex; justify-content: center; flex-wrap: wrap; }\n        nav a { color: #f8fafc; padding: 12px 20px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; }\n        nav a:hover { background: #2563eb; }\n        .hero { background: linear-gradient(rgba(0,0,0,0.6), rgba(0,0,0,0.6)), url('campus.jpg') center/cover; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 60px 20px; }\n        .container { max-width: 1100px; margin: 30px auto; padding: 0 15px; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 2fr 1fr; gap: 25px; }\n        .card { background: white; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 2px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.08); }\n        footer { background: #0f172a; color: #94a3b8; text-align: center; padding: 20px; margin-top: 40px; font-size: 13px; }\n        /* Media Query for Mobile Responsive Screen */\n        @media (max-width: 768px) {\n            .container { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }\n            nav { flex-direction: column; text-align: center; }\n        }\n    &lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;header&gt;\n        &lt;h1&gt;Chaudhary Charan Singh University, Meerut&lt;/h1&gt;\n        &lt;p&gt;Faculty of Engineering &amp; Technology &bull; BCA Department&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/header&gt;\n    &lt;nav&gt;\n        &lt;a href=\"#\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;\n        &lt;a href=\"#\"&gt;Academics&lt;/a&gt;\n        &lt;a href=\"#\"&gt;Examinations&lt;/a&gt;\n        &lt;a href=\"#\"&gt;PYQ Bank&lt;/a&gt;\n        &lt;a href=\"#\"&gt;Contact Us&lt;/a&gt;\n    &lt;/nav&gt;\n    &lt;div class=\"hero\"&gt;\n        &lt;h2&gt;Excellence in Computer Science &amp; Applications&lt;/h2&gt;\n        &lt;p&gt;Empowering students with industry-standard programming and analytical skills.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;div class=\"container\"&gt;\n        &lt;main class=\"card\"&gt;\n            &lt;h3 style=\"color:#1e3a8a; margin-bottom:10px;\"&gt;About BCA Programme&lt;/h3&gt;\n            &lt;p&gt;The 3-Year Bachelor of Computer Applications curriculum encompasses Data Structures, DBMS, Java, Computer Networks, and Numerical Analysis to build cutting-edge software engineering capabilities.&lt;/p&gt;\n        &lt;/main&gt;\n        &lt;aside class=\"card\"&gt;\n            &lt;h3 style=\"color:#dc2626; margin-bottom:10px;\"&gt;Notice Board&lt;/h3&gt;\n            &lt;ul style=\"padding-left:18px; line-height:1.6; font-size:13px;\"&gt;\n                &lt;li&gt;5th Sem Examination Schedule Released.&lt;/li&gt;\n                &lt;li&gt;Solved PYQs uploaded to student portal.&lt;/li&gt;\n                &lt;li&gt;Project viva submissions due next week.&lt;/li&gt;\n            &lt;/ul&gt;\n        &lt;/aside&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;footer&gt;\n        &lt;p&gt;&amp;copy; CCS University BCA Portal. All Rights Reserved.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/footer&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"sec-title\" data-sec=\"sec-b\">Section – B (Short Answer Questions &bull; 7.5 Marks &bull; 7 Questions)</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2023, 2019 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q1. Explain the concepts of Inheritance in Java and its various types.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Inheritance:</strong> The mechanism where a subclass derives attributes and behaviors from a superclass using <code>extends</code>.<br>\n        <strong>Types in Java:</strong>\n        <ol>\n            <li><strong>Single Inheritance:</strong> Class B extends Class A.</li>\n            <li><strong>Multilevel Inheritance:</strong> Class C extends Class B, which extends Class A.</li>\n            <li><strong>Hierarchical Inheritance:</strong> Class B and Class C both extend Class A.</li>\n            <li><strong>Multiple Inheritance:</strong> Achieved through <strong>Interfaces</strong> (Java does not support multiple class inheritance to avoid the Diamond Problem).</li>\n        </ol>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q2. Explain TCP/IP Client Socket. Write constructors and methods used to create client sockets.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Client Socket (java.net.Socket):</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Constructors:</strong>\n                <ul>\n                    <li><code>Socket(String host, int port)</code> &mdash; Establishes TCP stream connection.</li>\n                    <li><code>Socket(InetAddress address, int port)</code></li>\n                </ul>\n            </li>\n            <li><strong>Methods:</strong>\n                <ul>\n                    <li><code>getInputStream()</code> &mdash; Returns InputStream for reading server responses.</li>\n                    <li><code>getOutputStream()</code> &mdash; Returns OutputStream for sending data.</li>\n                    <li><code>close()</code> &mdash; Gracefully closes the TCP connection.</li>\n                </ul>\n            </li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021</span>\n        Q3. What do you mean by a Package in Java? State the different Access Specifiers available in Java.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Access Specifiers:</strong>\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Access Specifier</th><th>Same Class</th><th>Same Package</th><th>Subclass (Diff Pkg)</th><th>World (Everywhere)</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n                <tr><td><code>private</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>\n                <tr><td><code>default</code> (package-private)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>\n                <tr><td><code>protected</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes (via inheritance)</td><td>No</td></tr>\n                <tr><td><code>public</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n        </table>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021</span>\n        Q4. What is Exception? Give different types of exceptions that occur during runtime. Why handle exceptions?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>An <b>Exception</b> in Java is an abnormal event interrupting the normal instruction flow during program execution.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Classification of Java Exceptions:</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>Checked Exceptions (Compile-Time):</b> Subclasses of <code>Exception</code> (excluding <code>RuntimeException</code>). Must be either caught in a <code>try-catch</code> or declared with <code>throws</code>; otherwise code will not compile.\n            <ul>\n                <li><code>IOException</code>: Attempting to read a non-existent file.</li>\n                <li><code>SQLException</code>: Database syntax error or lost network connection.</li>\n                <li><code>ClassNotFoundException</code>: Attempting to load an unavailable class via reflection.</li>\n            </ul>\n        </li>\n        <li><b>Unchecked Exceptions (Runtime):</b> Subclasses of <code>RuntimeException</code>. Usually caused by logic flaws:\n            <ul>\n                <li><code>ArithmeticException</code>: Division by zero (<code>10 / 0</code>).</li>\n                <li><code>NullPointerException (NPE)</code>: Accessing a method on a <code>null</code> reference.</li>\n                <li><code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>: Accessing array index outside <code>[0, length-1]</code>.</li>\n                <li><code>NumberFormatException</code>: Parsing <code>\"ABC\"</code> with <code>Integer.parseInt()</code>.</li>\n            </ul>\n        </li>\n    </ol>\n    <p><b>Why Handle Exceptions:</b> Without handling, an exception crashes the active thread, drops open file/database handles, leaves data in corrupt uncommitted states, and displays ugly stack traces to end users.</p>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021</span>\n        Q5. Explain Thread Priority. What are default values? Write a program to define two threads where one prints 1 to 10 and the other prints 10 to 1.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Thread Priorities:</strong> Integer scale from 1 (<code>MIN_PRIORITY</code>) to 10 (<code>MAX_PRIORITY</code>), with default 5 (<code>NORM_PRIORITY</code>).<br>\n        <strong>Program:</strong>\n<pre>\nclass ForwardPrinter extends Thread {\n    public void run() {\n        for(int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {\n            System.out.println(\"Forward: \" + i);\n            try { Thread.sleep(200); } catch(Exception e) {}\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nclass BackwardPrinter extends Thread {\n    public void run() {\n        for(int j = 10; j >= 1; j--) {\n            System.out.println(\"Backward: \" + j);\n            try { Thread.sleep(200); } catch(Exception e) {}\n        }\n    }\n}\n\npublic class PriorityThreadDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        ForwardPrinter t1 = new ForwardPrinter();\n        BackwardPrinter t2 = new BackwardPrinter();\n        t1.setPriority(Thread.MAX_PRIORITY);\n        t2.setPriority(Thread.MIN_PRIORITY);\n        t1.start();\n        t2.start();\n    }\n}\n</pre>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2017</span>\n        Q6. Explain Packages and Access Protection in Java.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>Java packages provide modular namespaces. Access protection controls which classes, methods, and variables can be seen across package boundaries using four access specifiers:</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Access Level</th><th>Keyword</th><th>Accessibility Scope</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Public</b></td><td><code>public</code></td><td>Accessible from <b>any class</b> in any package throughout the entire application.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Protected</b></td><td><code>protected</code></td><td>Accessible within the <b>same package</b>, and by <b>subclasses in other packages</b> via inheritance.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Default (Package-Private)</b></td><td><i>(No keyword)</i></td><td>Accessible <b>only within the same package</b>. Inaccessible to subclasses outside the package.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Private</b></td><td><code>private</code></td><td>Accessible <b>strictly within the declaring class</b>. Conceals internal state (Encapsulation).</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2016</span>\n        Q7. What do you mean by Constructor? Explain Constructor Overloading.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>A <b>Constructor</b> is a special method invoked automatically upon object instantiation using <code>new</code> to allocate heap memory and initialize state variables.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Characteristics of Constructors:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Name must <b>exactly match</b> the class name.</li>\n        <li>Must <b>not have any return type</b> (not even <code>void</code>).</li>\n        <li>If no constructor is defined, JVM inserts a default no-argument constructor.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p><b>Constructor Overloading:</b> Defining multiple constructors with different argument counts or data types within the same class.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Code Example:</p>\n<div class=\"code-box\">class Student {\n    int roll;\n    String name;\n    double marks;\n\n    // Default Constructor\n    Student() {\n        this.roll = 0;\n        this.name = \"Unknown\";\n        this.marks = 0.0;\n    }\n\n    // Overloaded Constructor (2 arguments)\n    Student(int roll, String name) {\n        this.roll = roll;\n        this.name = name;\n        this.marks = 0.0;\n    }\n\n    // Overloaded Constructor (3 arguments)\n    Student(int roll, String name, double marks) {\n        this.roll = roll;\n        this.name = name;\n        this.marks = marks;\n    }\n}</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"sec-title\" data-sec=\"sec-a\">Section – A (Very Short Answer Questions &bull; 3 Marks &bull; 21 Questions)</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2019, 2018, 2016 &bull; Repeated 4&times;</span>\n        Q1. Explain Garbage Collection in Java.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Garbage Collection (GC)</b> is Java's automatic memory management process that identifies and deletes unreferenced objects from the Heap memory, preventing memory leaks and dangling pointer bugs.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Key Mechanism:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>Daemon Thread:</b> A low-priority background thread run by the JVM continuously tracks heap allocations.</li>\n        <li><b>Eligibility for GC:</b> An object becomes eligible for GC when it has zero active live references. This occurs when:\n            <ol>\n                <li>Reference is set to <code>null</code>: <code>emp = null;</code></li>\n                <li>Reference is reassigned: <code>e1 = e2;</code></li>\n                <li>Object created inside a method goes out of scope.</li>\n                <li>Island of Isolation (objects reference each other but are unreachable from GC root).</li>\n            </ol>\n        </li>\n        <li><b>Requesting GC:</b> <code>System.gc()</code> or <code>Runtime.getRuntime().gc()</code> suggests garbage collection, though execution is subject to JVM discretion.</li>\n        <li><b>finalize() Method:</b> Declared in <code>Object</code> class, called right before an object is destroyed for final cleanup (deprecated in Java 9+ in favor of Cleaners/PhantomReferences).</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2019, 2017 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q2. What is Bytecode in Java? Why does it enable platform independence?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Bytecode</b> is the intermediate, non-executable binary machine code generated by the Java compiler (<code>javac</code>) stored in <code>.class</code> files.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Why Bytecode Enables Platform Independence:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Unlike C/C++ which compiles source directly into CPU-specific native machine code (x86, ARM), Java compiles into highly optimized bytecode instructions designed for an abstract machine: the <b>JVM</b>.</li>\n        <li>The compiled <code>.class</code> file is 100% platform-independent. It can run without recompilation on Windows, Linux, macOS, or Solaris.</li>\n        <li>Each platform has its own native JVM implementation that interprets or JIT-compiles this uniform bytecode into local CPU instructions.</li>\n        <li>This delivers Java's core slogan: <b>\"Write Once, Run Anywhere\" (WORA)</b>.</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2018 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q3. Explain the 'super' keyword in Java with usages.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>The <code>super</code> keyword in Java is a reference variable used by a subclass to explicitly refer to its immediate parent (superclass) object.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Three Distinct Usages of 'super':</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>Access Hidden Superclass Variables:</b> Resolves name collisions when a child class declares a field with the same name as a parent class field: <code>super.variableName;</code></li>\n        <li><b>Invoke Overridden Superclass Methods:</b> Calls the parent method implementation from inside the overriding subclass method: <code>super.methodName();</code></li>\n        <li><b>Invoke Superclass Constructors:</b> Explicitly calls the parent class constructor: <code>super();</code> or <code>super(arg1, arg2);</code>. <i>Rule: Must be the very first statement inside the subclass constructor.</i></li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Code Example:</p>\n<div class=\"code-box\">class Animal {\n    String color = \"White\";\n    Animal(String species) { System.out.println(\"Animal created: \" + species); }\n    void makeSound() { System.out.println(\"Generic animal sound\"); }\n}\n\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    String color = \"Black\";\n    Dog() {\n        super(\"Canine\"); // Calls Animal constructor (Usage 3)\n    }\n    void display() {\n        System.out.println(\"Subclass color: \" + color);        // Black\n        System.out.println(\"Superclass color: \" + super.color); // White (Usage 1)\n        super.makeSound(); // Calls Animal's makeSound() (Usage 2)\n    }\n}</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2020 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q4. Discuss Primitive Data Types in Java.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>Java supports <b>8 primitive data types</b> that represent fundamental values directly stored in stack memory, rather than heap objects:</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Data Type</th><th>Category</th><th>Size (Bits / Bytes)</th><th>Default Value</th><th>Range</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>byte</code></td><td>Integer</td><td>8 bits (1 byte)</td><td>0</td><td>-128 to 127</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>short</code></td><td>Integer</td><td>16 bits (2 bytes)</td><td>0</td><td>-32,768 to 32,767</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>int</code></td><td>Integer</td><td>32 bits (4 bytes)</td><td>0</td><td>-2<sup>31</sup> to 2<sup>31</sup> - 1</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>long</code></td><td>Integer</td><td>64 bits (8 bytes)</td><td>0L</td><td>-2<sup>63</sup> to 2<sup>63</sup> - 1</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>float</code></td><td>Floating Point</td><td>32 bits (4 bytes)</td><td>0.0f</td><td>~±3.40282347E+38F (6-7 digits)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>double</code></td><td>Floating Point</td><td>64 bits (8 bytes)</td><td>0.0d</td><td>~±1.79769313486231570E+308 (15 digits)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>char</code></td><td>Character</td><td>16 bits (2 bytes)</td><td>'\\u0000'</td><td>0 to 65,535 (Unicode characters)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>boolean</code></td><td>Logical</td><td>1 bit (JVM dependent)</td><td>false</td><td><code>true</code> or <code>false</code></td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023, 2017 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q5. What is Method Overloading vs Method Overriding?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Method Overloading (Compile-Time)</th><th>Method Overriding (Runtime)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Scope</b></td><td>Occurs within the <b>same class</b>.</td><td>Occurs between <b>Superclass and Subclass</b> (Inheritance).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Method Signature</b></td><td>Method name must be same, but <b>parameters must differ</b> (number, types, or order).</td><td>Method name, return type, and parameters must be <b>identical</b>.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Polymorphism Type</b></td><td>Static / Compile-time polymorphism.</td><td>Dynamic / Runtime polymorphism.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Return Type</b></td><td>Can have different return types as long as parameters differ.</td><td>Must be same or covariant.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Keywords / Annotations</b></td><td>No specific keyword needed.</td><td>Annotated with <code>@Override</code>.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Private / Static Methods</b></td><td>Can overload private and static methods.</td><td><b>Cannot</b> override private or static methods.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020, 2017 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q6. What is the purpose of the 'static' keyword in Java?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>The <code>static</code> keyword in Java denotes members that belong to the <b>class itself</b> rather than to individual object instances. Only a single copy is shared across all instances.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Four Applications of 'static':</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>Static Variables:</b> Stored in Method Area / Metaspace once when class is loaded. Used for global counters or shared company constants (e.g., <code>static int count = 0;</code>).</li>\n        <li><b>Static Methods:</b> Invoked directly using class name without creating an object (e.g., <code>Math.sqrt(16)</code>). <i>Rule: Cannot access instance (non-static) variables or use <code>this</code> / <code>super</code>.</i></li>\n        <li><b>Static Block:</b> Executes automatically once when class is loaded by JVM, before constructor or <code>main()</code>. Used for static resource initialization.</li>\n        <li><b>Static Nested Classes:</b> Inner class that does not require an outer class instance reference.</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019, 2018 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q7. What is a Final Variable? Give an example.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>In Java, the <code>final</code> keyword is a non-access modifier used to apply restrictions:</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>Final Variable:</b> Its value becomes constant and cannot be reassigned once initialized:\n            <div class=\"code-box\">final double PI = 3.14159265;\n// PI = 3.14; // Compilation error: cannot assign a value to final variable</div>\n        </li>\n        <li><b>Blank Final Variable:</b> An uninitialized final variable that must be initialized inside the constructor.</li>\n        <li><b>Final Method:</b> A method that <b>cannot be overridden</b> by any subclass, preventing altered behavior.</li>\n        <li><b>Final Class:</b> A class that <b>cannot be extended (inherited)</b> (e.g., <code>java.lang.String</code>, <code>java.lang.Math</code>).</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q8. Explain the Anchor and Table tags in HTML.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n    <li>\n        <b>Anchor Tag (<code>&lt;a&gt;</code>):</b> Defines hyperlinks to navigate across web pages, documents, or anchor bookmarks.\n        <div class=\"code-box\">&lt;a href=\"https://www.google.com\" target=\"_blank\"&gt;Visit Google&lt;/a&gt;</div>\n        <i>Key Attributes:</i> <code>href</code> (target URL), <code>target=\"_blank\"</code> (opens in new tab), <code>download</code> (downloads file).\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <b>Table Tag (<code>&lt;table&gt;</code>):</b> Arranges data into a two-dimensional grid of rows and columns.\n        <div class=\"code-box\">&lt;table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"5\" cellspacing=\"0\"&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Roll&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Name&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;101&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;Rahul&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n&lt;/table&gt;</div>\n        <i>Key Tags:</i> <code>&lt;tr&gt;</code> (Row), <code>&lt;th&gt;</code> (Header Cell, bold/centered), <code>&lt;td&gt;</code> (Data Cell), <code>colspan</code> / <code>rowspan</code> (cell merging).\n    </li>\n</ul>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q9. Briefly explain Internet Concepts: URL, HTTP, and Web Server.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>URL (Uniform Resource Locator):</b> The unique global web address used to locate a specific resource on the Internet.<br>\n        <i>Structure:</i> <code>https://www.example.com:443/bca/notes.html?id=10#sec2</code><br>\n        (Protocol &bull; Domain &bull; Port &bull; Path &bull; Query String &bull; Fragment).</li>\n        <li><b>HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol):</b> The stateless application-layer protocol governing communication between web browsers and servers. Operates over TCP port 80 (HTTP) or port 443 (HTTPS with TLS encryption). Methods include <code>GET</code>, <code>POST</code>, <code>PUT</code>, <code>DELETE</code>.</li>\n        <li><b>Web Server:</b> Software/hardware system that stores website files (HTML, CSS, JS, images) and delivers them to client browsers over HTTP (e.g., Apache HTTP Server, Nginx, Microsoft IIS).</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023</span>\n        Q10. How is an Object created in Java?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>An <b>Object</b> is a runtime instance of a class occupying heap memory. In Java, objects can be created through 4 distinct mechanisms:</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>Using the <code>new</code> Keyword (Most Common):</b> Invokes constructor directly: <code>Student s1 = new Student();</code></li>\n        <li><b>Using Reflection (<code>Class.forName().newInstance()</code>):</b> Dynamically loads class and instantiates object at runtime.</li>\n        <li><b>Using <code>clone()</code> Method:</b> Creates an exact field-by-field copy of an existing object implementing <code>Cloneable</code>: <code>Student s2 = (Student) s1.clone();</code></li>\n        <li><b>Using Deserialization:</b> Reconstructs an object from a saved byte stream using <code>ObjectInputStream.readObject()</code>.</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023</span>\n        Q11. Compare JDK, JRE, and JVM.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>JVM (Java Virtual Machine)</th><th>JRE (Java Runtime Environment)</th><th>JDK (Java Development Kit)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Definition</b></td><td>Abstract engine that executes Java bytecode (.class).</td><td>Software bundle required strictly to <b>run</b> compiled Java programs.</td><td>Complete software kit required to <b>develop</b>, compile, and run Java programs.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Includes</b></td><td>Class Loader, Execution Engine (Interpreter + JIT), Garbage Collector.</td><td>JVM + Standard Core Libraries (rt.jar, util, lang).</td><td>JRE + Development Tools (<code>javac</code>, <code>javadoc</code>, <code>jdb</code>, <code>jar</code>).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Target Audience</b></td><td>Internal runtime execution platform.</td><td>End users who only run Java applications.</td><td>Software developers writing and compiling code.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Formula</b></td><td>Core execution component.</td><td><b>JRE = JVM + Core Class Libraries</b></td><td><b>JDK = JRE + Development Tools</b></td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023</span>\n        Q12. Difference between JDBC and ODBC.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Criteria</th><th>JDBC (Java Database Connectivity)</th><th>ODBC (Open Database Connectivity)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Developer & Language</b></td><td>Developed by Sun Microsystems; written specifically in <b>pure Java</b>.</td><td>Developed by Microsoft; written in <b>C/C++</b>.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Platform Independence</b></td><td><b>Platform independent</b>; can run on any OS with JVM.</td><td>Platform dependent; primarily tied to Windows and C-runtimes.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Type Safety & OOP</b></td><td>Strongly typed, fully Object-Oriented Java API.</td><td>Procedural C function calls, pointer-based.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Performance with Java</b></td><td>High efficiency with Type-4 pure Java drivers.</td><td>Slower due to JNI / bridging translation layers.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q13. Explain why Java is considered the best language for Internet Applications.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>Java became the dominant language for Internet and enterprise web applications due to:</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>Platform Independence (WORA):</b> Bytecode runs identically on any client OS and server architecture.</li>\n        <li><b>Security:</b> Java provides a sandbox execution model, bytecode verifier, and eliminates direct pointer access, preventing memory corruption and unauthorized file access.</li>\n        <li><b>Built-in Multithreading & Networking:</b> Native support for TCP/IP sockets, URLs, concurrent threads, and high-throughput thread pooling.</li>\n        <li><b>Robust Server-Side Ecosystem:</b> Servlets, JSP, Spring Boot, JDBC, and Enterprise JavaBeans (EJB) power global web banking, e-commerce, and cloud platforms.</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q14. What are Wrapper Classes in Java?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Wrapper Classes</b> provide an object representation for Java's 8 primitive data types (e.g., <code>int</code> &rarr; <code>Integer</code>, <code>char</code> &rarr; <code>Character</code>). They reside in <code>java.lang</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Why Wrapper Classes are Required:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Java Collections (e.g., <code>ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;</code>, <code>HashMap</code>) can only store objects, not raw primitives.</li>\n        <li>Utility methods like <code>Integer.parseInt(\"123\")</code> and <code>Double.valueOf()</code> for type conversion.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p><b>Autoboxing & Unboxing:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>Autoboxing:</b> Automatic primitive-to-wrapper conversion: <code>Integer x = 50;</code></li>\n        <li><b>Unboxing:</b> Automatic wrapper-to-primitive conversion: <code>int y = x;</code></li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019</span>\n        Q15. List the four fundamental principles of Object-Oriented Programming.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Pillar</th><th>Core Concept & Definition</th><th>Java Implementation</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>1. Encapsulation</b></td><td>Binding data variables and methods together into a single unit (class), while hiding internal data using <code>private</code> variables with public getters/setters.</td><td><code>private int id; public int getId() { return id; }</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>2. Abstraction</b></td><td>Hiding internal implementation details and displaying only essential functional features to the user.</td><td><code>abstract class</code> and <code>interface</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>3. Inheritance</b></td><td>Mechanism where a child class acquires properties and behaviors of a parent class to foster code reuse.</td><td><code>class Dog extends Animal</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>4. Polymorphism</b></td><td>Ability of an entity or method to take many forms. Subdivided into Compile-time (Method Overloading) and Runtime (Method Overriding).</td><td>Overloading and <code>@Override</code></td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019</span>\n        Q16. Define Constructor in Java.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>A <b>Constructor</b> in Java is a special member method invoked automatically by the runtime when an object is instantiated using the <code>new</code> operator. Its primary role is to initialize instance variables and allocate required memory resources.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Core Characteristics of Constructors:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>Same Name as Class:</b> Must bear the exact identifier of the enclosing class.</li>\n        <li><b>No Return Type:</b> Does not return any value; must never declare a return type (not even <code>void</code>).</li>\n        <li><b>Automatic Invocation:</b> Triggered implicitly upon object creation: <code>Student s = new Student();</code></li>\n        <li><b>Default Constructor:</b> If no constructor is defined by the developer, the Java compiler automatically synthesizes a default no-argument constructor.</li>\n        <li><b>Cannot be static, abstract, or final:</b> Constructors belong to object instantiation and cannot be inherited or made static.</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2018</span>\n        Q17. What is an Abstract Method?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>An <b>Abstract Method</b> is a method declared without any implementation (no body), followed by a semicolon:</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <div class=\"code-box\">abstract void draw(); // Abstract method declaration</div>\n    <p><b>Rules Governing Abstract Methods:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Must be declared with the <code>abstract</code> keyword.</li>\n        <li>Can only exist inside an <code>abstract class</code> or <code>interface</code>.</li>\n        <li>Any non-abstract (concrete) subclass extending the abstract class <b>MUST override and implement</b> all inherited abstract methods.</li>\n        <li>Cannot be declared as <code>static</code>, <code>final</code>, or <code>private</code> because those cannot be overridden.</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2018</span>\n        Q18. What is a Layout Manager in Java AWT?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>A <b>Layout Manager</b> is an interface in <code>java.awt</code> responsible for automatically calculating the position, size, and alignment of GUI components within a container window.</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Layout Manager</th><th>Arrangement Mechanism</th><th>Default For</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>FlowLayout</code></td><td>Arranges components sequentially in a row from left to right; wraps to next line when row fills up.</td><td><code>Panel</code>, <code>Applet</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>BorderLayout</code></td><td>Divides container into 5 distinct regions: <code>NORTH</code>, <code>SOUTH</code>, <code>EAST</code>, <code>WEST</code>, <code>CENTER</code>.</td><td><code>Frame</code>, <code>Dialog</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>GridLayout</code></td><td>Arranges components into a strict 2D rectangular grid of equal-sized rows and columns: <code>new GridLayout(rows, cols)</code>.</td><td>Custom Grids</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>GridBagLayout</code></td><td>Most flexible manager; aligns components dynamically with varying cell spans and constraints.</td><td>Complex Forms</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>CardLayout</code></td><td>Stacks components like a deck of cards; only one card visible at a time.</td><td>Tabbed Wizards</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2017</span>\n        Q19. How will you create hyperlinks in a web document? Write the code also.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>Hyperlinks in HTML are created using the <b>Anchor tag</b> (<code>&lt;a&gt;</code>) with the mandatory <code>href</code> attribute:</p>\n<div class=\"code-box\">&lt;!-- 1. External Hyperlink to website --&gt;\n&lt;a href=\"https://www.google.com\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Search Web\"&gt;Open Google in New Tab&lt;/a&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- 2. Relative Link to Local Page --&gt;\n&lt;a href=\"syllabus.html\"&gt;View BCA Syllabus&lt;/a&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- 3. Email Link --&gt;\n&lt;a href=\"mailto:admissions@ccsu.ac.in\"&gt;Contact Admissions&lt;/a&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- 4. Internal Bookmark / Fragment Link --&gt;\n&lt;a href=\"#section3\"&gt;Jump to Section 3&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;h3 id=\"section3\"&gt;Section 3 Content&lt;/h3&gt;</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2017</span>\n        Q20. What is a Constructor? List its characteristics.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Characteristics:</strong> Same name as class, no return type (not even void), automatically called on <code>new</code>, can be overloaded.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2016</span>\n        Q21. What is Byte-code in JAVA? Explain.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> The intermediate machine instruction set generated by <code>javac</code>, executed by the JVM to provide platform independence.\n    </div>\n</div>\n", "counts": {"total": 50, "sec_c": 22, "sec_b": 7, "sec_a": 21}}, "networks": {"code": "BCA-503", "paperCode": "18023", "title": "Computer Networks", "desc": "Complete Deduplicated Question Bank covering OSI 7 Layers, TCP/IP, Sliding Window Protocols, CRC Error Control, Dijkstra, & Switching.", "icon": "fa-network-wired", "color": "emerald", "pdfBank": "pdfs/BCA503_Networks.pdf", "years": [{"year": "2025", "pdf": "pdfs/networks_2025.pdf"}, {"year": "2024", "pdf": "pdfs/networks_2024.pdf"}, {"year": "2023", "pdf": "pdfs/networks_2023.pdf"}, {"year": "2021", "pdf": "pdfs/networks_2021.pdf"}, {"year": "2020", "pdf": "pdfs/networks_2020.pdf"}, {"year": "2019", "pdf": "pdfs/networks_2019.pdf"}, {"year": "2018", "pdf": "pdfs/networks_2018.pdf"}, {"year": "2017", "pdf": "pdfs/networks_2017.pdf"}, {"year": "2016", "pdf": "pdfs/networks_2016.pdf"}], "contentHtml": "<div class=\"sec-title\" data-sec=\"sec-c\">Section – C (Detailed / Long Answer Questions &bull; 15 Marks &bull; 13 Questions)</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2024, 2023, 2021, 2020, 2019, 2018, 2017, 2016 &bull; Repeated 9&times;</span>\n        Q1. Explain the ISO/OSI Reference Model in detail. Describe the primary functions of each of the seven layers.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Seven Layers of OSI Model:</strong>\n        <ol>\n            <li><strong>Physical Layer (Layer 1):</strong> Transmission of raw unstructured bit streams over physical media (voltages, connectors, cables).</li>\n            <li><strong>Data Link Layer (Layer 2):</strong> Framing, physical addressing (MAC), flow control, error control (CRC), and media access control.</li>\n            <li><strong>Network Layer (Layer 3):</strong> Logical addressing (IP), routing (Dijkstra, distance vector), packet forwarding, and fragmentation.</li>\n            <li><strong>Transport Layer (Layer 4):</strong> End-to-end process-to-process delivery (ports), segmentation and reassembly, connection control (TCP handshake), reliable flow control.</li>\n            <li><strong>Session Layer (Layer 5):</strong> Dialogue control (simplex, half/full duplex), session checkpointing, synchronization tokens.</li>\n            <li><strong>Presentation Layer (Layer 6):</strong> Data translation, character code conversion (ASCII/EBCDIC), compression, encryption/decryption (SSL/TLS).</li>\n            <li><strong>Application Layer (Layer 7):</strong> Network virtual terminals, file transfer (FTP), web browsing (HTTP), email services (SMTP).</li>\n        </ol>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2024, 2023, 2021, 2020, 2019, 2017, 2016 &bull; Repeated 8&times;</span>\n        Q2. Explain Congestion Control Mechanisms in Computer Networks: Leaky Bucket and Token Bucket Algorithms with comparative analysis.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Network Congestion</b> occurs when the load on network nodes (routers/switches) exceeds their buffer capacity or link bandwidth, resulting in queue buildup, packet loss, and throughput collapse.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Two Fundamental Traffic Shaping Algorithms:</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>Leaky Bucket Algorithm:</b> Shapes bursty traffic into a strictly constant, uniform output rate regardless of packet burst size. Packets enter a bucket of capacity <i>C</i> and leak out at a constant rate <i>r</i>. If bucket overflows, subsequent incoming packets are discarded.</li>\n        <li><b>Token Bucket Algorithm:</b> Allows bursty traffic up to a configurable burst size. Tokens are generated and accumulated in the bucket at a constant rate <i>r</i> up to capacity <i>C</i>. A packet can transmit only if enough tokens are available to cover its byte size; transmitting consumes tokens.</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Comparative Analysis Table:</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Leaky Bucket Algorithm</th><th>Token Bucket Algorithm</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Output Rate</b></td><td>Strictly constant and uniform. Zero bursts permitted.</td><td>Allows controlled bursty transmission up to token capacity.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Token Mechanism</b></td><td>No tokens; drops excess packets when bucket is full.</td><td>Accumulates tokens; drops excess tokens if bucket is full.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Traffic Suitability</b></td><td>Ideal for constant bit rate (CBR) audio/video traffic.</td><td>Ideal for bursty data traffic (web browsing, database queries).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Packet Loss</b></td><td>Drops packets immediately when buffer fills up.</td><td>Buffers packets until enough tokens arrive.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2021, 2020, 2019, 2018, 2017, 2016 &bull; Repeated 7&times;</span>\n        Q3. Explain Sliding Window Protocols: Stop-and-Wait ARQ, Go-Back-N ARQ, and Selective Repeat ARQ with efficiency analysis.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 22.5M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Stop-and-Wait ARQ (Automatic Repeat reQuest)</b> is a Data Link Layer flow and error control protocol designed for noisy channels.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Working Mechanism:</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li>Sender transmits a single frame with a 1-bit sequence number (Frame 0 or Frame 1) and starts a retransmission timer.</li>\n        <li>Sender stops and waits; no new frame is sent until an ACK arrives.</li>\n        <li><b>Normal Case:</b> Receiver verifies CRC. If correct, sends ACK with sequence number of next expected frame (e.g., ACK 1). Sender stops timer and transmits Frame 1.</li>\n        <li><b>Lost Frame / Damaged Frame:</b> Receiver discards damaged frame silently. Sender timer expires (timeout), triggering automatic retransmission of the unacknowledged frame.</li>\n        <li><b>Lost ACK:</b> Sender retransmits frame after timeout. Receiver detects duplicate sequence number, discards duplicate frame, and resends ACK.</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2024, 2020, 2019, 2018, 2017, 2016 &bull; Repeated 7&times;</span>\n        Q4. Compare Circuit Switching, Packet Switching, and Message Switching.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Comparison:</strong>\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Circuit Switching</th><th>Packet Switching</th><th>Message Switching</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n                <tr><td>Dedicated Path</td><td>Yes (setup before transfer).</td><td>No (packets routed dynamically).</td><td>No (store-and-forward per hop).</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Bandwidth</td><td>Reserved/wasted if idle.</td><td>Dynamically shared on-demand.</td><td>Dynamically shared.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Delay</td><td>Setup delay high; transmission delay low.</td><td>Jitter/variable queuing delay.</td><td>High storage delay at each switch.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Modern Use</td><td>PSTN landline telephone.</td><td>Internet (IP networks).</td><td>Early telegraph, postal email.</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n        </table>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2021, 2020, 2019, 2018, 2016 &bull; Repeated 6&times;</span>\n        Q5. Explain Routing Algorithms: (a) Distance Vector Routing (Bellman-Ford) and (b) Link State Routing (Dijkstra). Discuss Count-to-Infinity problem.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 22.5M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Routing Algorithms:</strong>\n        <ol>\n            <li><strong>Distance Vector Routing:</strong> Each router maintains a routing table containing distance to each destination and next hop. Routers periodically share complete tables with immediate neighbors. Uses Bellman-Ford algorithm. Vulnerable to <em>Count-to-Infinity problem</em> when links fail (mitigated by Split Horizon and Poison Reverse).</li>\n            <li><strong>Link State Routing:</strong> Each router floods Link State Packets (LSP) containing status of its local links to all routers in the entire autonomous system. Every router constructs a global topology map and computes shortest paths using Dijkstra's algorithm (used in OSPF). Fast convergence and no routing loops.</li>\n        </ol>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2020, 2019, 2018, 2017, 2016 &bull; Repeated 6&times;</span>\n        Q6. Explain Error Detection Techniques: Parity Check, Checksum, and CRC (Cyclic Redundancy Check).\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Error Detection:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Parity Check:</strong> Appends single parity bit (even or odd parity). Detects only odd numbers of bit errors.</li>\n            <li><strong>Checksum:</strong> Divides data into 16-bit words, adds them using 1's complement arithmetic, and appends inverted sum (used in TCP/IP).</li>\n            <li><strong>Cyclic Redundancy Check (CRC):</strong> Treats bit stream as polynomial coefficients and performs modulo-2 binary division by generator polynomial <span class=\"math-expr\">G(x)</span>. Appends remainder (FCS). Detects all single, double, odd, and burst errors up to generator length!</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023, 2020, 2019, 2018, 2017, 2016 &bull; Repeated 6&times;</span>\n        Q7. Explain Guided Media: Twisted Pair Cable, Coaxial Cable, and Fiber Optic Cable.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Guided (Bounded) Media</b> refers to physical, wired transmission media that direct data signals along a solid conductor or optical waveguide.</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Twisted Pair Cable</th><th>Coaxial Cable</th><th>Optical Fiber Cable</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Signal Type</b></td><td>Electrical electrical signals (copper wires).</td><td>Electrical signals (copper core).</td><td>Light pulses (photons) through silica glass core.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Subtypes</b></td><td>UTP (Unshielded) & STP (Shielded, Cat5e/6/7).</td><td>Thicknet (10Base5) & Thinnet (10Base2, 50/75 &Omega;).</td><td>Single-Mode (long-haul) & Multi-Mode (LAN/campus).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Bandwidth & Speed</b></td><td>Moderate (10 Mbps to 10 Gbps).</td><td>Moderate-High (10 Mbps to 100 Mbps).</td><td><b>Extremely high (10 Gbps to 100+ Gbps)</b>.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Attenuation & Repeater Distance</b></td><td>High attenuation; repeater every 100 meters.</td><td>Moderate attenuation; repeater every 500 meters.</td><td><b>Lowest attenuation; repeater every 50+ km</b>.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>EMI Immunity</b></td><td>Prone to electromagnetic interference (crosstalk).</td><td>Good immunity (outer braided copper shield).</td><td><b>100% immune to EMI, lightning, and radio noise</b>.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Cost & Installation</b></td><td>Cheapest, flexible, very easy to terminate.</td><td>Moderate cost, bulky, medium difficulty.</td><td>High cost, fragile, requires skilled fusion splicing.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2023, 2016 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q8. What is Subnetting? Give an example.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> <strong>Subnetting</strong> is the process of partitioning a single physical network into two or more smaller logical subnets by borrowing host bits to create subnet bits, reducing broadcast traffic and optimizing address allocation.<br>\n        <strong>Example:</strong> Given Class C network <code>192.168.1.0/24</code> with default mask <code>255.255.255.0</code>. Borrowing 2 host bits gives subnet mask <code>255.255.255.192</code> (/26), creating <span class=\"math-expr\">2<sup>2</sup> = 4</span> subnets with 62 usable hosts each:\n        <ul>\n            <li>Subnet 1: <code>192.168.1.0</code> to <code>192.168.1.63</code></li>\n            <li>Subnet 2: <code>192.168.1.64</code> to <code>192.168.1.127</code></li>\n            <li>Subnet 3: <code>192.168.1.128</code> to <code>192.168.1.191</code></li>\n            <li>Subnet 4: <code>192.168.1.192</code> to <code>192.168.1.255</code></li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2023 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q9. Write detailed notes on: (a) DNS (Domain Name System), (b) Firewalls and Network Security, (c) SMTP and Email Architecture.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Detailed Notes:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>DNS:</strong> Hierarchical distributed database mapping hostnames (<code>www.google.com</code>) to IP addresses (<code>142.250.190.46</code>) over UDP/TCP port 53.</li>\n            <li><strong>Firewalls:</strong> Security barrier controlling inbound/outbound network traffic based on rules. Types: Packet Filtering, Stateful Inspection, Proxy/Application-level gateways.</li>\n            <li><strong>Email Architecture:</strong> User Agent (UA) composes email, Mail Transfer Agent (MTA) transmits using SMTP (port 25/587), and Mail Access Agent (MAA) retrieves email to client via POP3 (port 110) or IMAP (port 143).</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2023 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q10. Explain Error Control in Data Link Layer using CRC with a complete numerical example.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Cyclic Redundancy Check (CRC)</b> is an error-detecting polynomial code based on binary modulo-2 arithmetic (XOR operations without carry or borrow).</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Step-by-Step CRC Algorithm:</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li>Let Dataword be <i>D</i> of length <i>k</i>, and Generator Divisor be <i>G</i> of length <i>(n + 1)</i> bits.</li>\n        <li>Append <i>n</i> zeros to the right of Dataword: <code>D' = D &bull; 0<sup>n</sup></code>.</li>\n        <li>Divide <i>D'</i> by <i>G</i> using <b>Modulo-2 Division (XOR)</b>.</li>\n        <li>The resulting <i>n</i>-bit remainder is the <b>CRC Checksum</b> (FCS).</li>\n        <li>Codeword transmitted: <code>T = D &bull; CRC</code>.</li>\n        <li>At Receiver: Divide received Codeword <i>T</i> by <i>G</i>. If remainder == 0, accept frame; otherwise reject (error detected).</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Complete Numerical Example:</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p>Given Dataword = <code>100100</code> (6 bits), Divisor = <code>1101</code> (4 bits, degree n = 3).</p>\n    <p>Append 3 zeros: Dividend = <code>100100000</code>.</p>\n    <div class=\"code-box\">           111101 (Quotient)\n     -------------------\n1101 | 100100000\n       1101\n       -----\n       01000\n        1101\n       -----\n        01010\n         1101\n        -----\n         01110\n          1101\n         -----\n          00110\n           0000\n          -----\n           01100\n            1101\n           -----\n            0001 (Remainder / CRC = 001)</div>\n    <p>Transmitted Codeword = Dataword + Remainder = <b>100100001</b> &check;</p>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023, 2017 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q11. Explain Dijkstra's Shortest Path Routing Algorithm with a network graph example.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Dijkstra's Algorithm</b> is a Link-State routing algorithm (used in OSPF) that computes the shortest path tree from a single source node to all destination nodes in a weighted network graph.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Algorithm Steps:</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li>Initialize distance to source node as 0: <code>Dist[Source] = 0</code>. Set distance to all other nodes as &infin;.</li>\n        <li>Mark all nodes as unvisited. Create visited set <code>S = {}</code>.</li>\n        <li>For currently unvisited node <code>u</code> with minimum distance:\n            <ul>\n                <li>Add <code>u</code> to visited set <code>S</code>.</li>\n                <li>For each unvisited neighbor <code>v</code> of <code>u</code>:\n                    <br><code>If Dist[u] + Cost(u, v) &lt; Dist[v]: Dist[v] = Dist[u] + Cost(u, v)</code> (Relaxation step).</li>\n            </ul>\n        </li>\n        <li>Repeat until all nodes are included in set <code>S</code>. Time complexity: <i>O(|V|<sup>2</sup>)</i> or <i>O(|E| log |V|)</i> using min-heap.</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Numerical Graph Example:</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p>Graph with nodes A, B, C, D, E. Source: Node A.</p>\n    <p>Edges: (A-B)=4, (A-C)=2, (B-C)=1, (B-D)=5, (C-D)=8, (C-E)=10, (D-E)=2.</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Start at A: Dist = {A:0, B:4, C:2, D:&infin;, E:&infin;}. Pick C (min dist = 2).</li>\n        <li>From C: Update B: min(4, 2+1) = 3; Update D: min(&infin;, 2+8) = 10; Update E: min(&infin;, 2+10) = 12. Dist = {A:0, B:3, C:2, D:10, E:12}. Pick B (dist = 3).</li>\n        <li>From B: Update D: min(10, 3+5) = 8. Dist = {A:0, B:3, C:2, D:8, E:12}. Pick D (dist = 8).</li>\n        <li>From D: Update E: min(12, 8+2) = 10. Pick E (dist = 10).</li>\n        <li><b>Final Shortest Distances from A:</b> A=0, B=3 (A&rarr;C&rarr;B), C=2 (A&rarr;C), D=8 (A&rarr;C&rarr;B&rarr;D), E=10 (A&rarr;C&rarr;B&rarr;D&rarr;E).</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q12. (a) Explain Shannon Capacity with examples. (b) What is CSMA? List protocols used with CSMA.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>(a) Shannon Capacity Theorem:</strong> Gives theoretical maximum data rate <span class=\"math-expr\">C</span> of a channel with bandwidth <span class=\"math-expr\">B</span> and Signal-to-Noise Ratio <span class=\"math-expr\">SNR</span>:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">C = B log<sub>2</sub>(1 + SNR)  bps</div>\n        <em>Example:</em> For telephone line with <span class=\"math-expr\">B = 3000</span> Hz and <span class=\"math-expr\">SNR = 3162</span> (35 dB):\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">C = 3000 log<sub>2</sub>(1 + 3162) &approx; 3000 &times; 11.62 = 34,860  bps &approx; 34.8  kbps</span>.<br><br>\n        <strong>(b) CSMA Protocols:</strong> 1-Persistent CSMA, Non-Persistent CSMA, p-Persistent CSMA, and CSMA/CD / CSMA/CA.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q13. Describe any three: (a) SSL, (b) Modems, (c) Line Configuration, (d) Distortion vs Noise.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Short Notes:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>SSL (Secure Sockets Layer):</strong> Cryptographic protocol providing end-to-end encryption, authentication, and integrity over TCP.</li>\n            <li><strong>Modems (Modulator-Demodulator):</strong> Converts digital computer signals to analog audio frequencies for transmission over analog lines and vice versa.</li>\n            <li><strong>Line Configuration:</strong> Point-to-Point (dedicated link between two devices) vs Multipoint (multidrop link where multiple devices share a single link).</li>\n            <li><strong>Distortion vs Noise:</strong> Distortion is alteration of signal shape caused by different frequency phase delays in transmission media. Noise is unwanted external thermal/electromagnetic energy added into the signal.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"sec-title\" data-sec=\"sec-b\">Section – B (Short Answer Questions &bull; 7.5 Marks &bull; 10 Questions)</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2021 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q1. Write short notes on: (a) Throughput & Wavelength, (b) Synchronous Protocols, (c) Routers.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Notes:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>(a) Throughput & Wavelength:</strong> Throughput is the actual measurement of data transferred across a network per second. Wavelength (<span class=\"math-expr\">&lambda; = v / f</span>) is the physical distance between consecutive signal wave peaks in optical communication.</li>\n            <li><strong>(b) Synchronous Protocols:</strong> Continuous transmission of data blocks synchronized to a shared clock, using sync bytes/flags (e.g. HDLC).</li>\n            <li><strong>(c) Routers:</strong> Network Layer (Layer 3) internetworking devices that inspect destination IP addresses and make packet forwarding decisions based on routing tables.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025, 2017 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q2. Describe Multiplexing. Explain FDM, TDM, and WDM with diagrams.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> Combining multiple separate data streams into a single composite signal transmitted over a shared transmission medium.<br>\n        <strong>Techniques:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>FDM (Frequency Division Multiplexing):</strong> Divides channel bandwidth into separate non-overlapping frequency bands with guard bands (Analog signals, radio, cable TV).</li>\n            <li><strong>TDM (Time Division Multiplexing):</strong> Interleaves time slots from multiple sources onto a high-speed digital carrier (Synchronous vs Statistical TDM).</li>\n            <li><strong>WDM (Wavelength Division Multiplexing):</strong> Multiplexes optical signals of differing wavelengths (colors) along a single optical fiber.</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2016 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q3. Explain the switching methods in Computer Networks.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Switching</b> is the technique by which nodes route and forward data across interconnected communication paths between sender and receiver.</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Circuit Switching</th><th>Packet Switching (Datagram / Virtual)</th><th>Message Switching</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Connection Setup</b></td><td>Mandatory dedicated physical circuit setup phase before data transfer.</td><td>No setup in Datagram; short virtual circuit ID setup in Virtual Circuit.</td><td>No setup needed; store-and-forward at each intermediate node.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Dedicated Bandwidth</b></td><td>Yes, entire bandwidth is reserved for the session duration.</td><td>No, dynamic statistical multiplexing shares channel bandwidth.</td><td>No dedicated path; entire message stored and queued at each router.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Call Termination</b></td><td>Explicit teardown phase required.</td><td>Automatic when last packet arrives.</td><td>Automatic.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Delay</b></td><td>Initial connection delay, but zero transmission delay once established.</td><td>Packet queuing and processing delay at every router hop.</td><td>High queuing delay due to large file buffering on intermediate disks.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Example Systems</b></td><td>Traditional Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN), ISDN.</td><td><b>Internet (IP)</b>, ATM, Frame Relay, modern 4G/5G networks.</td><td>Telegraph systems, legacy postal mail routing.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2023 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q4. Explain in brief: (a) Guided Media, (b) Transmission Modes.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>(a) Guided Media:</strong> Physical physical conductors that guide signals along a specific path: Twisted Pair (UTP/STP), Coaxial Cable, and Optical Fiber.<br>\n        <strong>(b) Transmission Modes:</strong>\n        <ul>\n            <li><em>Simplex:</em> Unidirectional transmission (e.g., keyboard to CPU, TV broadcast).</li>\n            <li><em>Half-Duplex:</em> Bidirectional, but only one direction at a time (e.g., Walkie-Talkie).</li>\n            <li><em>Full-Duplex:</em> Simultaneous bidirectional transmission (e.g., telephone call).</li>\n        </ul>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023</span>\n        Q5. Describe the functions & responsibilities of the Session Layer.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>The <b>Session Layer (Layer 5)</b> of the OSI Model is responsible for establishing, managing, synchronizing, and terminating communication dialogs between presentation entities.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Core Responsibilities:</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>Dialog Control:</b> Manages dialog discipline: determines whether the session operates in <b>Half-Duplex</b> (one side talks at a time using data tokens) or <b>Full-Duplex</b> (both sides talk simultaneously).</li>\n        <li><b>Synchronization & Checkpointing:</b> Inserts checkpoints into long data streams. If a connection failure occurs during a 100-page document transfer at page 65, only pages 66-100 are retransmitted instead of restarting from page 1.</li>\n        <li><b>Session Disconnection (Graceful Close):</b> Ensures all pending data has been transmitted and acknowledged before tearing down the session.</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021</span>\n        Q6. Discuss ISDN, its services, and layer structure.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>ISDN (Integrated Services Digital Network)</b> is a set of international CCITT/ITU-T standards for end-to-end digital transmission of voice, data, video, and fax over standard copper telephone lines.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>ISDN Channels:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>B-Channel (Bearer):</b> 64 kbps digital channel carrying user voice or payload data.</li>\n        <li><b>D-Channel (Delta):</b> 16 or 64 kbps channel for signaling, control, call setup, and packet telemetry.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p><b>Standard ISDN Service Interfaces:</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>BRI (Basic Rate Interface):</b> Designed for residential homes and small offices.<br>\n        <i>Structure:</i> <b>2B + D</b> = 2 &times; 64 kbps + 16 kbps = <b>144 kbps</b> (total 192 kbps with framing).</li>\n        <li><b>PRI (Primary Rate Interface):</b> Designed for corporate PBXs and enterprises.<br>\n        <i>Structure (North America / Japan):</i> <b>23B + D</b> = 1.544 Mbps (T1 line).<br>\n        <i>Structure (Europe / International):</i> <b>30B + D</b> = 2.048 Mbps (E1 line).</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021</span>\n        Q7. Explain TCP Packet Format (Header Structure) in detail.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>The <b>TCP Header</b> is a 20-to-60 byte segment header that encapsulates data to provide reliable, connection-oriented, stream transmission:</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Header Field</th><th>Bit Length</th><th>Function and Description</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Source Port</b></td><td>16 bits</td><td>Port number of the sending application (e.g., dynamic port 50231).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Destination Port</b></td><td>16 bits</td><td>Port number of receiving application service (e.g., port 80 for HTTP, 443 for HTTPS).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Sequence Number</b></td><td>32 bits</td><td>Tracks byte-stream sequence position of the first data byte in this segment.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Acknowledgment Number</b></td><td>32 bits</td><td>Next byte number expected by receiver when ACK flag is set (cumulative ACK).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Data Offset (HLEN)</b></td><td>4 bits</td><td>Specifies TCP header length in 32-bit (4-byte) words (5 words = 20 bytes min).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Reserved</b></td><td>6 bits</td><td>Reserved for future expansion (must be 0).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Control Flags</b></td><td>6 bits</td><td>\n        &bull; <code>URG</code>: Urgent pointer valid.<br>\n        &bull; <code>ACK</code>: Acknowledgment field valid.<br>\n        &bull; <code>PSH</code>: Push data directly to application.<br>\n        &bull; <code>RST</code>: Reset connection abruptly.<br>\n        &bull; <code>SYN</code>: Synchronize sequence numbers (Connection setup).<br>\n        &bull; <code>FIN</code>: Terminate sender connection.\n    </td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Window Size</b></td><td>16 bits</td><td>Flow control buffer size advertised by receiver (Sliding Window size in bytes).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Checksum</b></td><td>16 bits</td><td>Mandatory error detection covering header, payload, and IP pseudo-header.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Urgent Pointer</b></td><td>16 bits</td><td>Points to the last urgent data byte when URG flag is set.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Options & Padding</b></td><td>0-40 bytes</td><td>Optional fields like MSS (Maximum Segment Size), Timestamps, Window Scaling.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q8. How does DTE-DCE interface work? Discuss the essence of Modems.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>DTE vs DCE Architecture:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>DTE (Data Terminal Equipment):</b> The endpoint device that creates or consumes data (e.g., Computer, Server, Terminal).</li>\n        <li><b>DCE (Data Circuit-terminating Equipment):</b> The communication device that interfaces the DTE to the physical transmission medium (e.g., Modem, CSU/DSU, Network Interface Card).</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p><b>Working Principle & Standards:</b> Connected via physical interface standards such as <b>RS-232 / EIA-232</b>. DTE sends digital serial data (TxD, pin 2) to DCE, which converts it (modulation) for the analog or digital line. Control signals like RTS (Request to Send) and CTS (Clear to Send) synchronize transmission.</p>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2018</span>\n        Q9. Explain any one protocol used for flow control in noisy channel: Stop-and-Wait ARQ.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Stop-and-Wait ARQ (Automatic Repeat reQuest)</b> is a Data Link Layer flow and error control protocol designed for noisy channels.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Working Mechanism:</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li>Sender transmits a single frame with a 1-bit sequence number (Frame 0 or Frame 1) and starts a retransmission timer.</li>\n        <li>Sender stops and waits; no new frame is sent until an ACK arrives.</li>\n        <li><b>Normal Case:</b> Receiver verifies CRC. If correct, sends ACK with sequence number of next expected frame (e.g., ACK 1). Sender stops timer and transmits Frame 1.</li>\n        <li><b>Lost Frame / Damaged Frame:</b> Receiver discards damaged frame silently. Sender timer expires (timeout), triggering automatic retransmission of the unacknowledged frame.</li>\n        <li><b>Lost ACK:</b> Sender retransmits frame after timeout. Receiver detects duplicate sequence number, discards duplicate frame, and resends ACK.</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2018</span>\n        Q10. Explain Fiber Distributed Data Interface (FDDI) operation.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>FDDI (Fiber Distributed Data Interface)</b> is an ANSI and ISO standard for a 100 Mbps token-passing local/metropolitan area network (LAN/MAN) utilizing optical fiber cables across distances up to 100 km.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Dual Counter-Rotating Ring Architecture:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>Primary Ring:</b> Transmits token and data packets in one direction at 100 Mbps.</li>\n        <li><b>Secondary Ring:</b> Standby ring rotating in the opposite direction.</li>\n        <li><b>Self-Healing Ring Wrap:</b> If a cable break or node failure occurs, the stations adjacent to the fault automatically wrap the primary ring onto the secondary ring, forming a single closed continuous ring. This provides extreme fault tolerance.</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"sec-title\" data-sec=\"sec-a\">Section – A (Very Short Answer Questions &bull; 3 Marks &bull; 25 Questions)</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023, 2019, 2018, 2016 &bull; Repeated 4&times;</span>\n        Q1. What is Network Topology? List advantages and disadvantages of Mesh Topology.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Network Topology</b> describes the geometric and logical arrangement of nodes (computers, routers) and connecting communication links in a computer network.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Mesh Topology:</b> Every device is connected to every other device via dedicated point-to-point links. For <i>n</i> nodes, total physical links required: <code>N = n(n - 1) / 2</code>.</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>Advantages of Mesh Topology:</b>\n            <ul>\n                <li><i>Extreme Reliability & Fault Tolerance:</i> If one link breaks, traffic instantly reroutes through alternative paths.</li>\n                <li><i>Zero Traffic Congestion:</i> Dedicated links carry only traffic between their two endpoints.</li>\n                <li><i>High Privacy & Security:</i> Only sender and recipient receive data.</li>\n            </ul>\n        </li>\n        <li><b>Disadvantages of Mesh Topology:</b>\n            <ul>\n                <li><i>Prohibitive Cost:</i> Huge cabling expense (<i>O(n<sup>2</sup>)</i> cables).</li>\n                <li><i>Hardware Complexity:</i> Every computer requires <code>(n - 1)</code> I/O network ports.</li>\n                <li><i>Difficult Installation & Maintenance:</i> Dense cabling makes expansion challenging.</li>\n            </ul>\n        </li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019, 2018, 2016 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q2. Which device is used for Modulation and Demodulation? Explain its function.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>A <b>MODEM (Modulator-Demodulator)</b> is the hardware device used to convert between digital signals produced by computers and analog signals transmitted over physical communication channels (like telephone lines or coaxial cables).</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>Modulation (Transmitter Side):</b> Converts computer digital binary square waves (0s and 1s) into continuous analog sine waves by varying amplitude (ASK), frequency (FSK), or phase (PSK) to traverse long-distance analog media.</li>\n        <li><b>Demodulation (Receiver Side):</b> Reverses the process, extracting the original discrete binary bits from incoming analog waveforms to feed the computer's CPU.</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2018, 2016 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q3. Explain Transmission Modes: Simplex, Half-Duplex, and Full-Duplex.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Transmission Mode</th><th>Directionality</th><th>Channel Utilization</th><th>Real-World Examples</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Simplex</b></td><td><b>Strictly Unidirectional</b>. One station only sends, other only receives.</td><td>Entire channel bandwidth reserved for one direction.</td><td>Keyboard to CPU, Monitor, Traditional Radio/TV broadcast.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Half-Duplex</b></td><td><b>Bidirectional, but one direction at a time</b>. Cannot transmit and receive simultaneously.</td><td>Shared directionality; stations take turns.</td><td>Walkie-Talkie systems (saying \"Over\"), CSMA/CD Ethernet.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Full-Duplex</b></td><td><b>Simultaneous Bidirectional</b>. Both stations can transmit and receive at the same time.</td><td>Channel capacity divided between two directions.</td><td>Modern Telephone conversation, Full-Duplex Switched Ethernet.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025</span>\n        Q4. What is a MAC address? How is it different from an IP Address?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Comparison:</strong>\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>MAC Address (Physical Address)</th><th>IP Address (Logical Address)</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n                <tr><td>Layer</td><td>Data Link Layer (Layer 2).</td><td>Network Layer (Layer 3).</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Size & Format</td><td>48 bits (6 bytes), hex (e.g., <code>00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E</code>).</td><td>32 bits (IPv4, e.g., <code>192.168.1.1</code>) or 128 bits (IPv6).</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Nature</td><td>Hardcoded by manufacturer into NIC; globally fixed.</td><td>Dynamically/statically assigned based on network location.</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n        </table>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025</span>\n        Q5. Differentiate between a Hub and a Switch.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Hub (Multi-port Repeater)</th><th>Switch (Intelligent Bridge)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>OSI Layer</b></td><td>Physical Layer (Layer 1).</td><td>Data Link Layer (Layer 2) / Layer 3 (Multilayer).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Data Transmission</b></td><td><b>Broadcast:</b> Repeats every incoming electrical signal to all connected ports.</td><td><b>Unicast:</b> Looks up destination MAC address in forwarding table to send frame directly to designated port.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Collision Domain</b></td><td>All ports share a <b>single collision domain</b>. Collisions frequent.</td><td>Each individual port has its own <b>dedicated collision domain</b>. Zero collisions.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Duplex Mode</b></td><td>Half-duplex only (cannot send and receive simultaneously).</td><td>Full-duplex (simultaneous send/receive at wire speed).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Intelligence & Memory</b></td><td>Zero memory; purely passive electrical signal repeater.</td><td>Maintains dynamic MAC Address Table (CAM table) in RAM.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2025</span>\n        Q6. What is CSMA/CD? How does it operate in Ethernet?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Detection:</strong> A media access protocol used in shared half-duplex Ethernet:\n        <ol>\n            <li><strong>Carrier Sense:</strong> Station listens before transmitting (\"listen before talk\"). If channel is idle, it begins transmitting.</li>\n            <li><strong>Collision Detection:</strong> While transmitting, station monitors signal level. If collision occurs, it transmits a <em>jam signal</em> to notify all stations.</li>\n            <li><strong>Backoff:</strong> Stations wait a random backoff period using the Binary Exponential Backoff algorithm before retransmitting.</li>\n        </ol>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q7. What is the need for a Computer Network?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>A <b>Computer Network</b> is an interconnected collection of autonomous computers and peripherals capable of exchanging information and sharing resources.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Five Fundamental Needs & Advantages:</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>Hardware Resource Sharing:</b> High-cost hardware peripherals such as network printers, scanners, and multi-terabyte NAS storage servers are shared across hundreds of workstations.</li>\n        <li><b>Data & Software Centralization:</b> Centralized database servers (e.g., banking cores) ensure data consistency, centralized backups, and eliminate data silos.</li>\n        <li><b>Fast & Global Communication:</b> Facilitates instant real-time collaboration through E-Mail, VoIP, video conferencing, and messaging protocols.</li>\n        <li><b>High Reliability & Redundancy:</b> Alternative routing paths ensure that if one server fails, backup servers maintain business continuity.</li>\n        <li><b>Cost Reduction:</b> Eliminates redundant hardware purchases and software licenses through multi-user enterprise servers.</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q8. Define HDLC (High-Level Data Link Control).\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>HDLC (High-Level Data Link Control)</b> is a bit-oriented synchronous Data Link Layer protocol developed by ISO (ISO 3309) providing reliable point-to-point and multipoint communication.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Standard HDLC Frame Structure:</b></p>\n    <table class=\"data-table\">\n        <tr><th>Flag (8 bits)</th><th>Address (8 bits)</th><th>Control (8/16 bits)</th><th>Information (Variable)</th><th>FCS / CRC (16/32 bits)</th><th>Flag (8 bits)</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><code>01111110</code></td><td>Station Address</td><td>Frame type &amp; Sequence</td><td>Network Layer Payload</td><td>CRC Error Checksum</td><td><code>01111110</code></td></tr>\n    </table>\n    <p><b>Three Frame Types:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>I-Frame (Information):</b> Carries user payload data and piggybacked ACKs.</li>\n        <li><b>S-Frame (Supervisory):</b> Carries flow/error control signals (RR, RNR, REJ).</li>\n        <li><b>U-Frame (Unnumbered):</b> Carries link setup, disconnection, and mode setting commands (SABM, DISC).</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p><b>Bit Stuffing:</b> To prevent premature flag detection within data, HDLC inserts a '0' bit whenever five consecutive '1' bits appear in the payload.</p>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q9. Explain the concept of Piggybacking.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Piggybacking</b> is an optimization technique used in bidirectional sliding window protocols where positive acknowledgment (ACK) bits for received frames are attached onto an outgoing outgoing data frame rather than transmitting a dedicated ACK frame.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Operational Mechanism & Benefits:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li>When Station A receives a data frame from Station B, instead of immediately sending a separate small ACK packet (which incurs full 20-byte IP header + 14-byte Ethernet header overhead), it waits briefly until its own application layer generates an outgoing data packet for Station B.</li>\n        <li>The ACK sequence number is then placed into the header of that outgoing data frame.</li>\n        <li><b>Significantly Reduces Bandwidth Consumption:</b> Saves link channel capacity by cutting packet framing overhead in half.</li>\n        <li><i>Timer Precaution:</i> If no outgoing data frame is generated within a predetermined timeout period (ACK delay timer), a standalone ACK frame is dispatched to prevent retransmission timeouts at the sender.</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q10. Describe how SMTP protocol is used in E-Mail applications.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP)</b> is an application-layer, push-based protocol operating over TCP port 25 (or port 587 with TLS) used to deliver email messages across the Internet.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>SMTP Roles & Architecture:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>Push Protocol:</b> SMTP is strictly used by the sending Mail User Agent (MUA) to push email to the sender's Mail Transfer Agent (MTA) server, and from the sender MTA to the receiver's MTA server.</li>\n        <li><i>Pull Limitation:</i> SMTP <b>cannot pull</b> email messages from a mailbox down to a client device; retrieval requires pull protocols such as <b>POP3 (Port 110)</b> or <b>IMAP (Port 143)</b>.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p><b>Key SMTP Commands:</b> <code>HELO / EHLO</code> (handshake), <code>MAIL FROM:</code> (sender identity), <code>RCPT TO:</code> (recipient address), <code>DATA</code> (message body and headers), <code>QUIT</code> (session termination).</p>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023</span>\n        Q11. Explain the use of Bridges in Computer Networks.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>A <b>Bridge</b> is an internetworking device operating at the <b>Data Link Layer (Layer 2)</b> of the OSI model that connects and filters traffic between two separate LAN segments sharing identical protocols.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Core Functions & Mechanism:</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>Collision Domain Segmentation:</b> Splits a large collision domain into two smaller, isolated collision domains, dramatically reducing packet collisions.</li>\n        <li><b>MAC Address Filtering:</b> Inspects the destination MAC address of incoming frames using an internal forwarding table. If sender and receiver reside on the same segment, the frame is filtered (blocked) from crossing over.</li>\n        <li><b>Transparent Learning:</b> Dynamically inspects source MAC addresses of passing frames to build and update its bridge forwarding table.</li>\n        <li><b>Spanning Tree Protocol (STP):</b> Eliminates broadcast storms and packet loops in redundant multi-bridge topologies.</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023</span>\n        Q12. Write a short note on E-Mail and its core protocols.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Electronic Mail (E-Mail)</b> is a store-and-forward transmission system allowing digital messages and attachments to be exchanged globally between networked hosts.</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Protocol</th><th>Port</th><th>Role / Nature</th><th>Key Distinction</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol)</b></td><td>25 / 587</td><td><b>Push Protocol:</b> Sends email from sender client to sender server, and relays between intermediate mail servers.</td><td>Outbound delivery only; cannot retrieve from server mailbox.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>POP3 (Post Office Protocol v3)</b></td><td>110 / 995</td><td><b>Pull Protocol:</b> Downloads email messages from server to local client disk and deletes from server by default.</td><td>Offline access; changes on local device not synchronized to server.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>IMAP (Internet Message Access Protocol)</b></td><td>143 / 993</td><td><b>Pull & Sync Protocol:</b> Manages messages directly on the central mail server. Folders, read status, and drafts sync across all devices.</td><td>Real-time two-way synchronization for multiple client devices (phones, laptops).</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023</span>\n        Q13. Define Bandwidth and Throughput.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Bandwidth</th><th>Throughput</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Definition</b></td><td>The theoretical maximum theoretical data carrying capacity of a transmission medium.</td><td>The actual rate of successful data delivery achieved across the link under real network conditions.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Nature</b></td><td>Theoretical peak ceiling (potential).</td><td>Practical measured performance (reality).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Unit</b></td><td>Bits per second (bps, Mbps, Gbps) or Hertz (Hz).</td><td>Bits per second (bps, Mbps) or Packets per sec.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Factors Influencing</b></td><td>Physical cable characteristics, channel frequency spectrum.</td><td>Network congestion, packet collisions, processing overhead, protocol headers, retransmissions.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Analogy</b></td><td>The width of a highway (e.g., 6 lanes).</td><td>The actual number of cars passing per minute during rush hour.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q14. Four channels, each with 100-KHz bandwidth, are to be multiplexed together. What is the minimum bandwidth of the link if there is a need for a guard band of 10 KHz between channels?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Given Problem:</b> Four channels, each with 100-kHz bandwidth, are multiplexed together using Frequency Division Multiplexing (FDM). Guard bands of 10 kHz are required between adjacent channels. Find the minimum link bandwidth.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Step-by-Step Calculation:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Number of channels: <i>N = 4</i></li>\n        <li>Bandwidth per channel: <i>B = 100 kHz</i></li>\n        <li>Total bandwidth for 4 channels: <code>4 &times; 100 kHz = 400 kHz</code></li>\n        <li>Number of guard bands between <i>N</i> channels: <code>N - 1 = 4 - 1 = 3 guard bands</code></li>\n        <li>Bandwidth per guard band: <i>10 kHz</i></li>\n        <li>Total guard band bandwidth: <code>3 &times; 10 kHz = 30 kHz</code></li>\n    </ul>\n    <p><b>Minimum Link Bandwidth Required:</b></p>\n    <p><code>Total Bandwidth = (N &times; B) + [(N - 1) &times; Guard Band] = 400 kHz + 30 kHz = <b>430 kHz</b></code> &check;</p>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q15. Discuss Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP).\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>PPP (Point-to-Point Protocol)</b> is a standard Data Link Layer protocol (RFC 1661) used to establish a direct connection between two networking nodes over dial-up, serial, or broadband links.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Three Primary Components of PPP:</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>Framing Method:</b> HDLC-like framing mechanism providing unambiguous frame delimitation and 16/32-bit CRC error detection.</li>\n        <li><b>LCP (Link Control Protocol):</b> Establishes, configures, tests data link quality, negotiates options (packet sizes), and gracefully terminates the point-to-point connection.</li>\n        <li><b>NCP (Network Control Protocols):</b> A family of protocols that negotiates network layer protocols (e.g., IPCP negotiates dynamic IP addresses for IPv4).</li>\n    </ol>\n    <p><b>Authentication Support:</b> PPP supports robust authentication via <b>PAP (Password Authentication Protocol)</b> and <b>CHAP (Challenge Handshake Authentication Protocol)</b>.</p>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q16. Differentiate between LAN, MAN, and WAN.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>LAN (Local Area Network)</th><th>MAN (Metropolitan Area Network)</th><th>WAN (Wide Area Network)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Geographical Span</b></td><td>Confined to small area (single room, office, building, campus: &le; 1 km).</td><td>Spans an entire city or metropolitan town (&le; 50 km).</td><td>Spans entire countries, continents, or the globe (&gt; 100 km).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Ownership</b></td><td>Privately owned by single organization/individual.</td><td>Public or private enterprise (Cable TV provider, Telecom).</td><td>Distributed, collective ownership (Telecom consortiums, ISPs).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Data Transfer Rate</b></td><td><b>Highest:</b> 1 Gbps to 10 Gbps.</td><td>Moderate: 100 Mbps to 1 Gbps.</td><td>Lowest: 10 Mbps to 1 Gbps across long-haul undersea fibers.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Error Rate & Delay</b></td><td>Lowest error rates; sub-millisecond delay.</td><td>Moderate error rates and propagation delay.</td><td>Highest error rates; significant propagation delay (50-200 ms).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Technology Example</b></td><td>Ethernet (IEEE 802.3), Wi-Fi (802.11).</td><td>FDDI, Cable TV Networks, Metro Ethernet.</td><td><b>The Global Internet</b>, ATM, Satellite Links.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q17. What is a Collision Domain vs Broadcast Domain?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Collision Domain</th><th>Broadcast Domain</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Definition</b></td><td>A network segment where simultaneous transmissions from two stations result in an electrical signal packet collision.</td><td>A network segment where a broadcast packet sent by one station is received by all other stations in that segment.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Delimited By</b></td><td>Separated and broken up by <b>Switches, Bridges, and Routers</b> (Hubs do not break collision domains).</td><td>Separated and broken up <b>ONLY by Routers and VLANs</b> (Hubs and Switches forward broadcasts).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>OSI Layer</b></td><td>Data Link Layer (Layer 2) / Physical Layer (Layer 1).</td><td>Network Layer (Layer 3).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Switch Behavior</b></td><td>Each individual switch port forms an isolated collision domain.</td><td>All ports on a switch belong to a single default broadcast domain.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019</span>\n        Q18. Differentiate between Bridges and Repeaters.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Repeater</th><th>Bridge</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>OSI Layer</b></td><td>Operates strictly at the <b>Physical Layer (Layer 1)</b>.</td><td>Operates at the <b>Data Link Layer (Layer 2)</b>.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Functionality</b></td><td>Amplifies, regenerates, and retimes electrical/optical signals to overcome cable attenuation.</td><td>Inspects MAC addresses, filters frames, and forwards traffic across network segments.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Intelligence</b></td><td>Zero intelligence; cannot inspect packet headers or MAC addresses.</td><td>Intelligent; maintains dynamic MAC Address Table (CAM) in memory.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Collision Domain</b></td><td>Extends the single collision domain (does not break it).</td><td><b>Divides collision domains</b> into separate segments.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Data Unit Handled</b></td><td>Individual electrical bits (0s and 1s).</td><td>Frames (packets with MAC addresses and CRC).</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019</span>\n        Q19. What are the services provided by the Transport Layer?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>The <b>Transport Layer (Layer 4)</b> is the core of the OSI architecture providing transparent, logical, end-to-end communication between application processes running on distinct hosts.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Five Core Services:</b></p>\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>Port Addressing (Service Point Addressing):</b> Identifies specific communicating software processes using 16-bit Port Numbers (e.g., Port 80 for HTTP, Port 22 for SSH).</li>\n        <li><b>Segmentation and Reassembly:</b> Divides large application messages into smaller numbered segments at sender, and accurately reconstructs them at receiver.</li>\n        <li><b>Connection Management:</b> Provides either reliable connection-oriented transport (TCP 3-Way Handshake) or fast connectionless transport (UDP).</li>\n        <li><b>Flow Control:</b> Regulates data transmission speed using Sliding Window buffers to prevent fast senders from overwhelming slow receivers.</li>\n        <li><b>Error Control & Recovery:</b> Verifies end-to-end data integrity via Checksums and initiates retransmissions for lost or damaged segments.</li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019</span>\n        Q20. What is Shannon Capacity Theorem?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Shannon's Channel Capacity Theorem</b> establishes the absolute theoretical upper limit on the maximum data rate (in bits per second) that can be transmitted over a noisy analog communication channel without uncorrectable errors.</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Shannon's Formula:</b></p>\n    <p style=\"font-size:1.1rem; font-weight:bold; color:#1e40af; text-align:center;\">C = B &times; log<sub>2</sub>(1 + SNR)</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><b>C:</b> Channel Capacity in bits per second (bps).</li>\n        <li><b>B:</b> Channel Bandwidth in Hertz (Hz).</li>\n        <li><b>SNR:</b> Signal-to-Noise Ratio (linear power ratio: <i>P<sub>signal</sub> / P<sub>noise</sub></i>).</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p><i>Note on SNR:</i> If SNR is given in decibels (dB): <code>SNR<sub>dB</sub> = 10 &times; log<sub>10</sub>(SNR)</code>. For example, a 30 dB SNR corresponds to linear SNR = 1000.</p>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2018</span>\n        Q21. What is Bit Rate vs Baud Rate?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Bit Rate</th><th>Baud Rate (Modulation Rate)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Definition</b></td><td>The total number of binary data bits (0s and 1s) transmitted per second.</td><td>The total number of signal units or voltage state transitions (symbols) per second.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Unit</b></td><td>Bits per second (bps, kbps, Mbps).</td><td>Baud (or symbols/second).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Relationship Formula</b></td><td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b>Bit Rate = Baud Rate &times; N</b> &nbsp; (where <i>N = log<sub>2</sub> M</i> is bits encoded per signal element)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Example</b></td><td colspan=\"2\">In 16-QAM modulation, each signal symbol represents <i>log<sub>2</sub>(16) = 4 bits</i>. If Baud Rate is 1000 Baud, the Bit Rate is: <code>1000 &times; 4 = 4000 bps</code>.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2017</span>\n        Q22. A local telephone network is an example of which switching technology?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Circuit Switching</strong>. A dedicated physical communication circuit is established between the calling and called parties through telephone exchanges for the entire duration of the call.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2017</span>\n        Q23. What is Guided Media? List examples.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> Physical solid transmission media that provide a physical conduit for electromagnetic signals: Twisted Pair Cable, Coaxial Cable, and Fiber Optic Cable.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2017</span>\n        Q24. Compare TCP and UDP at the Transport Layer.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>Criteria</th><th>TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)</th><th>UDP (User Datagram Protocol)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Connection Type</b></td><td><b>Connection-Oriented:</b> Requires 3-Way Handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) before data transfer.</td><td><b>Connectionless:</b> Sends datagrams immediately without handshake setup.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Reliability</b></td><td><b>100% Reliable:</b> Guarantees delivery via ACKs, sequence numbers, and retransmissions.</td><td><b>Unreliable (Best-effort):</b> No ACKs; packets may arrive out of order, duplicate, or be lost.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Flow & Congestion Control</b></td><td>Yes, uses Sliding Window and algorithms (Slow Start, Congestion Avoidance).</td><td>None; transmits as fast as the application supplies data.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Header Size</b></td><td><b>20 to 60 bytes</b> (High overhead).</td><td><b>8 bytes fixed</b> (Very lightweight).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Applications</b></td><td>HTTP/HTTPS (Web), SMTP (Email), FTP (File Transfer), SSH.</td><td>DNS queries, VoIP calls, Video Streaming (YouTube/Zoom), Online Gaming.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2016</span>\n        Q25. What is Bandwidth of a channel?\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> The range of frequencies contained in a signal or passed by a transmission channel: <span class=\"math-expr\">B = f<sub>max</sub> - f<sub>min</sub></span> (measured in Hertz, or bps in digital communication).\n    </div>\n</div>\n", "counts": {"total": 48, "sec_c": 13, "sec_b": 10, "sec_a": 25}}, "numerical": {"code": "BCA-504", "paperCode": "18024", "title": "Numerical Methods", "desc": "Complete Deduplicated Question Bank covering Bisection, Newton-Raphson, Gauss Elimination, Interpolation (Newton/Lagrange) & RK4.", "icon": "fa-calculator", "color": "purple", "pdfBank": "pdfs/BCA504_Numerical.pdf", "years": [{"year": "2024", "pdf": "pdfs/nm_2024.pdf"}, {"year": "2023", "pdf": "pdfs/nm_2023.pdf"}, {"year": "2022", "pdf": "pdfs/nm_2022.pdf"}, {"year": "2021", "pdf": "pdfs/nm_2021.pdf"}, {"year": "2020", "pdf": "pdfs/nm_2020.pdf"}, {"year": "2019", "pdf": "pdfs/nm_2019.pdf"}, {"year": "2018", "pdf": "pdfs/nm_2018.pdf"}], "contentHtml": "<div class=\"sec-title\" data-sec=\"sec-c\">Section – C (Detailed / Long Answer Questions &bull; 15 Marks &bull; 16 Questions)</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2023, 2022, 2021, 2020, 2019, 2018 &bull; Repeated 7&times;</span>\n        Q1. Using Runge-Kutta 4th order method, find y(0.2) if dy/dx = x + y², given y(0) = 1 and step size h = 0.1.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 22.5M / 3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> We need 2 steps of <span class=\"math-expr\">h = 0.1</span> to reach <span class=\"math-expr\">x = 0.2</span>.<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">f(x, y) = x + y²</span>.<br>\n        <strong>STEP 1: From x₀ = 0, y₀ = 1 to x₁ = 0.1:</strong><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">k<sub>1</sub> = h f(0, 1) = 0.1(0 + 1) = 0.1000</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">k<sub>2</sub> = h f(0 + 0.05, 1 + 0.05) = 0.1(0.05 + 1.05<sup>2</sup>) = 0.1(0.05 + 1.1025) = 0.11525</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">k<sub>3</sub> = h f(0 + 0.05, 1 + 0.057625) = 0.1(0.05 + 1.057625<sup>2</sup>) = 0.1(0.05 + 1.11857) = 0.11686</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">k<sub>4</sub> = h f(0 + 0.1, 1 + 0.11686) = 0.1(0.1 + 1.11686<sup>2</sup>) = 0.1(0.1 + 1.24738) = 0.13474</span><br>\n        <div class=\"math-block\">&Delta; y<sub>0</sub> = (1)/(6)(k<sub>1</sub> + 2k<sub>2</sub> + 2k<sub>3</sub> + k<sub>4</sub>) = (1)/(6)(0.1 + 0.2305 + 0.23372 + 0.13474) = (0.69896)/(6) = 0.11649</div>\n        Thus, <span class=\"math-expr\">y(0.1) = 1 + 0.11649 = <b>1.11649</b></span>.<br><br>\n\n        <strong>STEP 2: From x₁ = 0.1, y₁ = 1.11649 to x₂ = 0.2:</strong><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">k<sub>1</sub> = 0.1 [0.1 + (1.11649)<sup>2</sup>] = 0.1 [0.1 + 1.24655] = 0.13466</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">k<sub>2</sub> = 0.1 [0.15 + (1.11649 + 0.06733)<sup>2</sup>] = 0.1 [0.15 + (1.18382)<sup>2</sup>] = 0.1 [0.15 + 1.40143] = 0.15514</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">k<sub>3</sub> = 0.1 [0.15 + (1.11649 + 0.07757)<sup>2</sup>] = 0.1 [0.15 + (1.19406)<sup>2</sup>] = 0.1 [0.15 + 1.42578] = 0.15758</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">k<sub>4</sub> = 0.1 [0.2 + (1.11649 + 0.15758)<sup>2</sup>] = 0.1 [0.2 + (1.27407)<sup>2</sup>] = 0.1 [0.2 + 1.62325] = 0.18233</span><br>\n        <div class=\"math-block\">&Delta; y<sub>1</sub> = (1)/(6)(0.13466 + 2(0.15514) + 2(0.15758) + 0.18233) = (0.94243)/(6) = 0.15707</div>\n        Thus, <span class=\"math-expr\">y(0.2) = 1.11649 + 0.15707 = <b>1.27356</b></span>.<br>\n        <strong>Final Answer:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">y(0.2) &approx; <b>1.2736</b></span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2023, 2022, 2021, 2020, 2019, 2018 &bull; Repeated 7&times;</span>\n        Q2. Obtain a real root of the equation f(x) = x³ + x² - 1 = 0 using Bisection method correct to three decimal places.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Given <span class=\"math-expr\">f(x) = x<sup>3</sup> + x<sup>2</sup> - 1 = 0</span>.<br>\n        Evaluating at test points:\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">f(0) = -1 < 0</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">f(1) = 1 + 1 - 1 = +1 > 0</span>.<br>\n        Since <span class=\"math-expr\">f(0) &bull; f(1) < 0</span>, a real root lies in <span class=\"math-expr\">[0, 1]</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Iteration 1:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>1</sub> = (0 + 1)/(2) = 0.5</span> &rArr; <span class=\"math-expr\">f(0.5) = 0.125 + 0.25 - 1 = -0.625 < 0</span> &rArr; root &isin; <span class=\"math-expr\">[0.5, 1.0]</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Iteration 2:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>2</sub> = (0.5 + 1.0)/(2) = 0.75</span> &rArr; <span class=\"math-expr\">f(0.75) = 0.421875 + 0.5625 - 1 = -0.015625 < 0</span> &rArr; root &isin; <span class=\"math-expr\">[0.75, 1.0]</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Iteration 3:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>3</sub> = (0.75 + 1.0)/(2) = 0.875</span> &rArr; <span class=\"math-expr\">f(0.875) = 0.6699 + 0.7656 - 1 = +0.4355 > 0</span> &rArr; root &isin; <span class=\"math-expr\">[0.75, 0.875]</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Iteration 4:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>4</sub> = (0.75 + 0.875)/(2) = 0.8125</span> &rArr; <span class=\"math-expr\">f(0.8125) = +0.1963 > 0</span> &rArr; root &isin; <span class=\"math-expr\">[0.75, 0.8125]</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Iteration 5:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>5</sub> = (0.75 + 0.8125)/(2) = 0.78125</span> &rArr; <span class=\"math-expr\">f(0.78125) = +0.0872 > 0</span> &rArr; root &isin; <span class=\"math-expr\">[0.75, 0.78125]</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Iteration 6:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>6</sub> = (0.75 + 0.78125)/(2) = 0.765625</span> &rArr; <span class=\"math-expr\">f(0.7656) = +0.0349 > 0</span> &rArr; root &isin; <span class=\"math-expr\">[0.75, 0.765625]</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Iteration 7:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>7</sub> = (0.75 + 0.765625)/(2) = 0.7578</span> &rArr; <span class=\"math-expr\">f(0.7578) = +0.0094 > 0</span> &rArr; root &isin; <span class=\"math-expr\">[0.75, 0.7578]</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Iteration 8:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>8</sub> = (0.75 + 0.7578)/(2) = 0.7539</span> &rArr; <span class=\"math-expr\">f(0.7539) = -0.0031 < 0</span> &rArr; root &isin; <span class=\"math-expr\">[0.7539, 0.7578]</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Iteration 9:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>9</sub> = 0.7558</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Root correct to 3 decimal places:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\"><b>x &approx; 0.755</b></span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2023, 2022, 2021, 2020, 2019, 2018 &bull; Repeated 7&times;</span>\n        Q3. Solve the differential equation dy/dx = x + y², y(0) = 1 using Picard's method (two approximations).\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Given <span class=\"math-expr\">y' = x + y<sup>2</sup></span> with initial condition <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>0</sub> = 0, y<sub>0</sub> = 1</span>.<br>\n        Picard's iterative formula: <span class=\"math-expr\">y<sup>(n)</sup>(x) = y<sub>0</sub> + &int;<sub>x<sub>0</sub></sub><sup>x</sup> f(t, y<sup>(n-1)</sup>(t))   dt</span>.<br>\n        <strong>First Approximation (n = 1):</strong>\n        <div class=\"math-block\">y<sup>(1)</sup>(x) = 1 + &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>x</sup> (t + 1<sup>2</sup>)   dt = 1 + [ (t<sup>2</sup>)/(2) + t ]<sub>0</sub><sup>x</sup> = <b>1 + x + (x<sup>2</sup>)/(2)</b></div>\n        <strong>Second Approximation (n = 2):</strong>\n        <div class=\"math-block\">y<sup>(2)</sup>(x) = 1 + &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>x</sup> [ t + (1 + t + (t<sup>2</sup>)/(2))<sup>2</sup> ] dt = 1 + &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>x</sup> [ t + 1 + 2t + 2t<sup>2</sup> + t<sup>3</sup> + (t<sup>4</sup>)/(4) ] dt</div>\n        <div class=\"math-block\">= 1 + &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>x</sup> ( 1 + 3t + 2t<sup>2</sup> + t<sup>3</sup> + (t<sup>4</sup>)/(4) ) dt = <b>1 + x + (3)/(2)x<sup>2</sup> + (2)/(3)x<sup>3</sup> + (x<sup>4</sup>)/(4) + (x<sup>5</sup>)/(20)</b>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2023, 2022, 2021, 2019, 2018 &bull; Repeated 6&times;</span>\n        Q4. Evaluate ∫₀¹ dx/(1 + x²) using Simpson's 3/8 rule. Hence obtain the approximate value of π.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 22.5M / 3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Let <span class=\"math-expr\">f(x) = (1)/(1 + x²)</span> over interval <span class=\"math-expr\">[0, 1]</span>.<br>\n        For Simpson's 3/8 rule, number of subintervals <span class=\"math-expr\">n</span> must be a multiple of 3. Choose <span class=\"math-expr\">n = 6</span>, so <span class=\"math-expr\">h = (1 - 0)/(6) = (1)/(6)</span>.\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <tr><th>i</th><th>x<sub>i</sub></th><th>y<sub>i</sub> = 1 / (1 + x_i²)</th><th>Decimal (5 places)</th></tr>\n            <tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1 / 1</td><td>1.00000</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>1</td><td>1/6</td><td>36 / 37</td><td>0.97297</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>2</td><td>2/6 = 1/3</td><td>9 / 10</td><td>0.90000</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>3</td><td>3/6 = 1/2</td><td>4 / 5</td><td>0.80000</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>4</td><td>4/6 = 2/3</td><td>9 / 13</td><td>0.69231</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>5</td><td>5/6</td><td>36 / 61</td><td>0.59016</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>6</td><td>1</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>0.50000</td></tr>\n        </table>\n        Simpson's 3/8 formula:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">I = (3h)/(8) [ (y<sub>0</sub> + y<sub>6</sub>) + 3(y<sub>1</sub> + y<sub>2</sub> + y<sub>4</sub> + y<sub>5</sub>) + 2(y<sub>3</sub>) ]</div>\n        Calculate components:\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">y<sub>0</sub> + y<sub>6</sub> = 1.00000 + 0.50000 = 1.50000</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">y<sub>1</sub> + y<sub>2</sub> + y<sub>4</sub> + y<sub>5</sub> = 0.97297 + 0.90000 + 0.69231 + 0.59016 = 3.15544</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">3 &times; 3.15544 = 9.46632</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">2 &times; y<sub>3</sub> = 2 &times; 0.80000 = 1.60000</span><br>\n        Sum inside brackets = <span class=\"math-expr\">1.50000 + 9.46632 + 1.60000 = 12.56632</span>.<br>\n        <div class=\"math-block\">I = (3 &times; (1/6))/(8) &times; 12.56632 = (1)/(16) &times; 12.56632 = <b>0.785395</b></div>\n        Since <span class=\"math-expr\">&int;<sub>0</sub><sup>1</sup> (dx)/(1 + x<sup>2</sup>) = [tan<sup>-1</sup>x]<sub>0</sub><sup>1</sup> = (&pi;)/(4)</span>:<br>\n        <div class=\"math-block\">&pi; &approx; 4 &times; I = 4 &times; 0.785395 = <b>3.14158</b></div>\n        <strong>Final Answer:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">I &approx; 0.78540</span> and <span class=\"math-expr\">&pi; &approx; 3.1416</span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2023, 2022, 2020, 2019, 2018 &bull; Repeated 6&times;</span>\n        Q5. Solve the system of linear equations by Gauss-Seidel Iteration method: 6x + y + z = 20, x + 4y - z = 6, x - y + 5z = 7.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> The system is diagonally dominant because <span class=\"math-expr\">|6| > |1|+|1|</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">|4| > |1|+|-1|</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">|5| > |1|+|-1|</span>.<br>\n        Rewrite in iterative form:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">x = (20 - y - z)/(6), &nbsp;&nbsp; y = (6 - x + z)/(4), &nbsp;&nbsp; z = (7 - x + y)/(5)</div>\n        Start with initial guess <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sup>(0)</sup> = 0, y<sup>(0)</sup> = 0, z<sup>(0)</sup> = 0</span>:<br><br>\n        <strong>Iteration 1:</strong>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sup>(1)</sup> = (20 - 0 - 0)/(6) = 3.3333</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">y<sup>(1)</sup> = (6 - 3.3333 + 0)/(4) = 0.6667</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">z<sup>(1)</sup> = (7 - 3.3333 + 0.6667)/(5) = 0.8667</span><br><br>\n        <strong>Iteration 2:</strong>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sup>(2)</sup> = (20 - 0.6667 - 0.8667)/(6) = (18.4666)/(6) = 3.0778</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">y<sup>(2)</sup> = (6 - 3.0778 + 0.8667)/(4) = (3.7889)/(4) = 0.9472</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">z<sup>(2)</sup> = (7 - 3.0778 + 0.9472)/(5) = (4.8694)/(5) = 0.9739</span><br><br>\n        <strong>Iteration 3:</strong>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sup>(3)</sup> = (20 - 0.9472 - 0.9739)/(6) = (18.0789)/(6) = 3.0132</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">y<sup>(3)</sup> = (6 - 3.0132 + 0.9739)/(4) = (3.9607)/(4) = 0.9902</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">z<sup>(3)</sup> = (7 - 3.0132 + 0.9902)/(5) = (4.9770)/(5) = 0.9954</span><br><br>\n        <strong>Iteration 4:</strong>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sup>(4)</sup> = (20 - 0.9902 - 0.9954)/(6) = 3.0024</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">y<sup>(4)</sup> = (6 - 3.0024 + 0.9954)/(4) = 0.9983</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">z<sup>(4)</sup> = (7 - 3.0024 + 0.9983)/(5) = 0.9992</span><br><br>\n        <strong>Final Converged Solution:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\"><b>x &approx; 3.000,   y &approx; 1.000,   z &approx; 1.000</b></span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2023, 2022, 2021, 2020 &bull; Repeated 5&times;</span>\n        Q6. Using method of False Position (Regula-Falsi), find a positive root of the equation x³ - 2x - 5 = 0.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[22.5M / 3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Let <span class=\"math-expr\">f(x) = x<sup>3</sup> - 2x - 5</span>.<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">f(2) = 8 - 4 - 5 = -1 < 0</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">f(3) = 27 - 6 - 5 = +16 > 0</span>.<br>\n        Root lies between <span class=\"math-expr\">a = 2</span> and <span class=\"math-expr\">b = 3</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Iter 1:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>1</sub> = (a f(b) - b f(a))/(f(b) - f(a)) = (2(16) - 3(-1))/(16 - (-1)) = (32 + 3)/(17) = (35)/(17) = 2.0588</span>.<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">f(2.0588) = (2.0588)<sup>3</sup> - 2(2.0588) - 5 = 8.7228 - 4.1176 - 5 = -0.3948 < 0</span>.<br>\n        Root lies between <span class=\"math-expr\">2.0588</span> and <span class=\"math-expr\">3</span>.<br><br>\n        <strong>Iter 2:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>2</sub> = (2.0588(16) - 3(-0.3948))/(16 - (-0.3948)) = (32.9408 + 1.1844)/(16.3948) = (34.1252)/(16.3948) = 2.0813</span>.<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">f(2.0813) = 9.0125 - 4.1626 - 5 = -0.1501 < 0</span>.<br><br>\n        <strong>Iter 3:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>3</sub> = (2.0813(16) - 3(-0.1501))/(16 + 0.1501) = (33.3008 + 0.4503)/(16.1501) = (33.7511)/(16.1501) = <b>2.0898</b></span>.<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">f(2.0898) &approx; -0.05</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Iter 4:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>4</sub> = <b>2.0945</b> &approx; <b>2.094</b></span>.<br>\n        <strong>Final Root:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\"><b>x &approx; 2.094</b></span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2022, 2021 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q7. Find the value of dy/dx at x = 1.2 for the following ordered pairs: (1.0, 2.7183), (1.2, 3.3201), (1.4, 4.0552), (1.6, 4.9530), (1.8, 6.0496), (2.0, 7.3891), (2.2, 9.0250).\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Here step size <span class=\"math-expr\">h = 0.2</span>. We evaluate the derivative at <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>0</sub> = 1.2</span> (using forward differences with base <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>0</sub> = 1.2</span>):<br>\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <thead><tr><th>x</th><th>y</th><th>&Delta;y</th><th>&Delta;&sup2;y</th><th>&Delta;&sup3;y</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n                <tr><td>1.2</td><td>3.3201</td><td>0.7351</td><td>0.1627</td><td>0.0339</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>1.4</td><td>4.0552</td><td>0.8978</td><td>0.1966</td><td>0.0429</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>1.6</td><td>4.9530</td><td>1.0966</td><td>0.2429</td><td>-</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>1.8</td><td>6.0496</td><td>1.3395</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>2.0</td><td>7.3891</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n        </table>\n        Using Newton's forward difference formula for derivative at the first point (<span class=\"math-expr\">u=0</span>):\n        <div class=\"math-block\">((dy)/(dx))<sub>x=1.2</sub> = (1)/(h) [ &Delta; y<sub>0</sub> - (1)/(2)&Delta;<sup>2</sup> y<sub>0</sub> + (1)/(3)&Delta;<sup>3</sup> y<sub>0</sub> ]</div>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">= (1)/(0.2) [ 0.7351 - (0.1627)/(2) + (0.0339)/(3) ] = 5 &times; [0.7351 - 0.08135 + 0.01130] = 5 &times; 0.66505 = <b>3.3253</b></span>.<br>\n        <em>Note:</em> Analytical derivative of <span class=\"math-expr\">y = e<sup>x</sup></span> at <span class=\"math-expr\">1.2</span> is <span class=\"math-expr\">e<sup>1.2</sup> = 3.3201</span>; numerical formula gives <span class=\"math-expr\"><b>3.325</b></span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023, 2022, 2019 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q8. Using Lagrange's interpolation formula, find the value of y corresponding to x = 2 from: (1, 1), (3, 27), (5, 125), (6, 216).\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Points: <span class=\"math-expr\">(x<sub>0</sub>, y<sub>0</sub>) = (1, 1)</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">(x<sub>1</sub>, y<sub>1</sub>) = (3, 27)</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">(x<sub>2</sub>, y<sub>2</sub>) = (5, 125)</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">(x<sub>3</sub>, y<sub>3</sub>) = (6, 216)</span> (Note: <span class=\"math-expr\">y = x<sup>3</sup></span>).<br>\n        Evaluating at <span class=\"math-expr\">x = 2</span> by Lagrange formula:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">y(2) = &Sigma;<sub>i=0</sub><sup>3</sup> L<sub>i</sub>(2) y<sub>i</sub></div>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">L<sub>0</sub>(2) = ((2-3)(2-5)(2-6))/((1-3)(1-5)(1-6)) = ((-1)(-3)(-4))/((-2)(-4)(-5)) = (-12)/(-40) = 0.300</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">L<sub>1</sub>(2) = ((2-1)(2-5)(2-6))/((3-1)(3-5)(3-6)) = ((1)(-3)(-4))/((2)(-2)(-3)) = (12)/(12) = 1.000</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">L<sub>2</sub>(2) = ((2-1)(2-3)(2-6))/((5-1)(5-3)(5-6)) = ((1)(-1)(-4))/((4)(2)(-1)) = (4)/(-8) = -0.500</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">L<sub>3</sub>(2) = ((2-1)(2-3)(2-5))/((6-1)(6-3)(6-5)) = ((1)(-1)(-3))/((5)(3)(1)) = (3)/(15) = 0.200</span><br><br>\n        Sum of weights <span class=\"math-expr\">= 0.3 + 1.0 - 0.5 + 0.2 = 1.0</span>.<br>\n        Compute <span class=\"math-expr\">y(2)</span>:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">y(2) = 0.3(1) + 1.0(27) - 0.5(125) + 0.2(216) = 0.3 + 27 - 62.5 + 43.2 = <b>8.0</b></div>\n        <strong>Answer:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\"><b>y(2) = 8</b></span> (Exact value of <span class=\"math-expr\">2<sup>3</sup></span>).\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021, 2020, 2019 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q9. Write the conditions for applicability of: (a) Trapezoidal rule, (b) Simpson's 1/3 rule. Evaluate &int;₀¹ dx/(1+x) using both rules with h = 0.25.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong><br>\n        <strong>Conditions:</strong><br>\n        (a) <em>Trapezoidal Rule:</em> The number of subintervals <span class=\"math-expr\">n</span> can be any positive integer (even or odd).<br>\n        (b) <em>Simpson's 1/3 Rule:</em> The number of subintervals <span class=\"math-expr\">n</span> must be even (<span class=\"math-expr\">n = 2m</span>).<br><br>\n        <strong>Evaluation for &int;₀¹ dx/(1+x), h = 0.25:</strong><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">y<sub>0</sub> = 1.0, y<sub>1</sub> = 0.8, y<sub>2</sub> = 0.6667, y<sub>3</sub> = 0.5714, y<sub>4</sub> = 0.5</span>.<br>\n        - <em>Trapezoidal:</em> <span class=\"math-expr\">I<sub>T</sub> = (0.25)/(2) [ 1.5 + 2(0.8 + 0.6667 + 0.5714) ] = 0.125 &times; 5.5762 = <b>0.6970</b></span>.<br>\n        - <em>Simpson's 1/3:</em> <span class=\"math-expr\">I<sub>S</sub> = (0.25)/(3) [ 1.5 + 4(0.8 + 0.5714) + 2(0.6667) ] = (0.25)/(3)[ 1.5 + 5.4856 + 1.3334 ] = (0.25)/(3)(8.319) = <b>0.6932</b></span>.<br>\n        <em>Exact value:</em> <span class=\"math-expr\">ln(2) &approx; 0.69315</span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2022, 2020 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q10. Solve the system by Gauss-Seidel iterative method: 20x + y - 2z = 17, 2x + 20y - 2z = -18, 2x - 3y + 20z = 25.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> The system is strictly diagonally dominant:<br>\n        <div class=\"math-block\">x = (17 - y + 2z)/(20), &nbsp;&nbsp; y = (-18 - 2x + 2z)/(20), &nbsp;&nbsp; z = (25 - 2x + 3y)/(20)</div>\n        Start with <span class=\"math-expr\">(x<sub>0</sub>, y<sub>0</sub>, z<sub>0</sub>) = (0, 0, 0)</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Iter 1:</strong>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>1</sub> = (17)/(20) = 0.8500</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">y<sub>1</sub> = (-18 - 2(0.85))/(20) = (-19.7)/(20) = -0.9850</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">z<sub>1</sub> = (25 - 2(0.85) + 3(-0.985))/(20) = (25 - 1.7 - 2.955)/(20) = 1.0173</span><br><br>\n        <strong>Iter 2:</strong>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>2</sub> = (17 - (-0.9850) + 2(1.0173))/(20) = (20.0196)/(20) = 1.0010</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">y<sub>2</sub> = (-18 - 2(1.0010) + 2(1.0173))/(20) = (-17.9674)/(20) = -0.9984</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">z<sub>2</sub> = (25 - 2(1.0010) + 3(-0.9984))/(20) = (20.0028)/(20) = 1.0001</span><br><br>\n        <strong>Iter 3:</strong>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>3</sub> = (17 - (-0.9984) + 2(1.0001))/(20) = 1.0000</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">y<sub>3</sub> = (-18 - 2(1) + 2(1))/(20) = -1.0000</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">z<sub>3</sub> = (25 - 2(1) + 3(-1))/(20) = 1.0000</span><br>\n        <strong>Final Answer:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\"><b>x = 1.000,   y = -1.000,   z = 1.000</b></span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021</span>\n        Q11. Interpolate by means of Gauss's formula the population for the year 1936, given: [1901: 12k, 1911: 15k, 1921: 20k, 1931: 27k, 1941: 39k, 1951: 52k].\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[22.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Origin at <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>0</sub> = 1931, h = 10 &rArr; u = (1936 - 1931)/(10) = 0.5</span>.<br>\n        Central difference table around 1931 (<span class=\"math-expr\">y<sub>0</sub> = 27</span>):<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">&Delta; y<sub>0</sub> = 39 - 27 = 12</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">&Delta; y<sub>-1</sub> = 27 - 20 = 7</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">&Delta;<sup>2</sup> y<sub>-1</sub> = 12 - 7 = 5</span>.<br>\n        Gauss Forward Interpolation formula:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">y = y<sub>0</sub> + u &Delta; y<sub>0</sub> + (u(u-1))/(2) &Delta;<sup>2</sup> y<sub>-1</sub> + ((u+1)u(u-1))/(6) &Delta;<sup>3</sup> y<sub>-1</sub></div>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">y(1936) = 27 + 0.5(12) + (0.5(-0.5))/(2)(5) = 27 + 6 - 0.625 = <b>32.375</b>  thousand</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Population in 1936:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\"><b>&approx; 32,375</b></span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021</span>\n        Q12. Find the solution of the system by Gauss-Seidel Method: 27x + 6y - z = 85, 6x + 15y + 2z = 72, x + y + 54z = 110.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[22.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Diagonally dominant formulas:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">x = (85 - 6y + z)/(27), &nbsp;&nbsp; y = (72 - 6x - 2z)/(15), &nbsp;&nbsp; z = (110 - x - y)/(54)</div>\n        Start <span class=\"math-expr\">(x<sub>0</sub>, y<sub>0</sub>, z<sub>0</sub>) = (0, 0, 0)</span>.<br>\n        Iter 1: <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>1</sub> = 3.1481, y<sub>1</sub> = 3.5407, z<sub>1</sub> = 1.9132</span>.<br>\n        Iter 2: <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>2</sub> = 2.4322, y<sub>2</sub> = 3.5720, z<sub>2</sub> = 1.9258</span>.<br>\n        Iter 3: <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>3</sub> = 2.4257, y<sub>3</sub> = 3.5730, z<sub>3</sub> = 1.9260</span>.<br>\n        Converged solution: <span class=\"math-expr\"><b>x &approx; 2.426,   y &approx; 3.573,   z &approx; 1.926</b></span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024</span>\n        Q13. Find the Lagrange interpolating polynomial of degree 2 approximating y = logₑ(x) defined by values: (2.0, 0.69315), (2.5, 0.91629), (3.0, 1.09861). Hence determine the value of logₑ(2.7).\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Data points: <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>0</sub> = 2.0, y<sub>0</sub> = 0.69315</span>; <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>1</sub> = 2.5, y<sub>1</sub> = 0.91629</span>; <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>2</sub> = 3.0, y<sub>2</sub> = 1.09861</span>.<br>\n        Lagrange interpolation formula for 3 points:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">P<sub>2</sub>(x) = ((x - x<sub>1</sub>)(x - x<sub>2</sub>))/((x<sub>0</sub> - x<sub>1</sub>)(x<sub>0</sub> - x<sub>2</sub>)) y<sub>0</sub> + ((x - x<sub>0</sub>)(x - x<sub>2</sub>))/((x<sub>1</sub> - x<sub>0</sub>)(x<sub>1</sub> - x<sub>2</sub>)) y<sub>1</sub> + ((x - x<sub>0</sub>)(x - x<sub>1</sub>))/((x<sub>2</sub> - x<sub>0</sub>)(x<sub>2</sub> - x<sub>1</sub>)) y<sub>2</sub></div>\n        For <span class=\"math-expr\">x = 2.7</span>:\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">L<sub>0</sub>(2.7) = ((2.7 - 2.5)(2.7 - 3.0))/((2.0 - 2.5)(2.0 - 3.0)) = ((0.2)(-0.3))/((-0.5)(-1.0)) = (-0.06)/(0.5) = -0.12</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">L<sub>1</sub>(2.7) = ((2.7 - 2.0)(2.7 - 3.0))/((2.5 - 2.0)(2.5 - 3.0)) = ((0.7)(-0.3))/((0.5)(-0.5)) = (-0.21)/(-0.25) = +0.84</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">L<sub>2</sub>(2.7) = ((2.7 - 2.0)(2.7 - 2.5))/((3.0 - 2.0)(3.0 - 2.5)) = ((0.7)(0.2))/((1.0)(0.5)) = (0.14)/(0.5) = +0.28</span><br><br>\n        Check sum of weights: <span class=\"math-expr\">-0.12 + 0.84 + 0.28 = 1.00</span> (exact).<br>\n        Now compute <span class=\"math-expr\">P<sub>2</sub>(2.7)</span>:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">P<sub>2</sub>(2.7) = -0.12(0.69315) + 0.84(0.91629) + 0.28(1.09861)</div>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">= -0.083178 + 0.769684 + 0.307611 = <b>0.993245</b> &approx; <b>0.9932</b></span>.<br>\n        <em>Verification:</em> Exact <span class=\"math-expr\">ln(2.7) &approx; 0.9932518</span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021</span>\n        Q14. By means of Newton's divided difference formula find f(8) and f(15) from: x = [4, 5, 7, 10, 11, 13], f(x) = [48, 100, 294, 900, 1210, 2028].\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Construct divided difference table:\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <thead><tr><th>x</th><th>f(x)</th><th>1st DD</th><th>2nd DD</th><th>3rd DD</th><th>4th DD</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n                <tr><td>4</td><td>48</td><td>52</td><td>15</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>5</td><td>100</td><td>97</td><td>21</td><td>1</td><td>-</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>7</td><td>294</td><td>202</td><td>27</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>10</td><td>900</td><td>310</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>11</td><td>1210</td><td>409</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>13</td><td>2028</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n        </table>\n        Newton's Divided Difference formula:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">f(x) = 48 + 52(x - 4) + 15(x - 4)(x - 5) + 1(x - 4)(x - 5)(x - 7)</div>\n        For <span class=\"math-expr\">x = 8</span>:\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">f(8) = 48 + 52(4) + 15(4)(3) + 1(4)(3)(1) = 48 + 208 + 180 + 12 = <b>448</b></span>.<br>\n        For <span class=\"math-expr\">x = 15</span>:\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">f(15) = 48 + 52(11) + 15(11)(10) + 1(11)(10)(8) = 48 + 572 + 1650 + 880 = <b>3150</b></span>.<br>\n        <strong>Answer:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\"><b>f(8) = 448</b></span>, <span class=\"math-expr\"><b>f(15) = 3150</b></span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q15. Apply Lagrange's formula to find the cubic polynomial which includes: (0, 2), (1, 3), (2, 12), (5, 147).\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Applying Lagrange interpolation across <span class=\"math-expr\">x = [0, 1, 2, 5]</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">y = [2, 3, 12, 147]</span>:<br>\n        Notice <span class=\"math-expr\">y = x<sup>3</sup> + 2</span>:<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">x=0 &rArr; y=2</span>;<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">x=1 &rArr; y=3</span>;<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">x=2 &rArr; y=8+2=10 &approx; 12</span>.<br>\n        Computing Lagrange polynomial:\n        <div class=\"math-block\"><b>y(x) = x<sup>3</sup> + x<sup>2</sup> - x + 2</b></div>\n        <em>Verification:</em> <span class=\"math-expr\">y(0)=2, y(1)=3, y(2)=8+4-2+2=12, y(5)=125+25-5+2=147</span>. (Exact!)\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-c\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2018</span>\n        Q16. Using Stirling formula, find f(28) from table: f(20)=49225, f(25)=48316, f(30)=47236, f(35)=45926, f(40)=44306.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[15M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Equidistant table with <span class=\"math-expr\">h = 5</span>. We need <span class=\"math-expr\">f(28)</span>.<br>\n        Choose origin <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>0</sub> = 30</span> (closest center point). Then <span class=\"math-expr\">u = (28 - 30)/(5) = -0.4</span>.<br>\n        Central difference table:\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <tr><th>u</th><th>x</th><th>y = f(x)</th><th>Δy</th><th>Δ²y</th><th>Δ³y</th><th>Δ⁴y</th></tr>\n            <tr><td>-2</td><td>20</td><td>49225</td><td>-909</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>-1</td><td>25</td><td>48316</td><td>-1080</td><td>-171</td><td>-59</td><td></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>0</td><td>30</td><td>47236</td><td>-1310</td><td>-230</td><td>-90</td><td>-31</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>1</td><td>35</td><td>45926</td><td>-1620</td><td>-310</td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>2</td><td>40</td><td>44306</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n        </table>\n        Stirling's Formula:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">f(x) = y<sub>0</sub> + u {&Delta; y<sub>-1</sub> + &Delta; y<sub>0</sub>}{2} + (u<sup>2</sup>)/(2)&Delta;<sup>2</sup> y<sub>-1</sub> + (u(u<sup>2</sup> - 1))/(6){&Delta;<sup>3</sup> y<sub>-2</sub> + &Delta;<sup>3</sup> y<sub>-1</sub>}{2} + (u<sup>2</sup>(u<sup>2</sup> - 1))/(24)&Delta;<sup>4</sup> y<sub>-2</sub></div>\n        Substituting values:\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">y<sub>0</sub> = 47236</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">{&Delta; y<sub>-1</sub> + &Delta; y<sub>0</sub>}{2} = (-1080 + (-1310))/(2) = -1195</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">u &times; (-1195) = (-0.4) &times; (-1195) = +478.0</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">(u<sup>2</sup>)/(2)&Delta;<sup>2</sup> y<sub>-1</sub> = (0.16)/(2) &times; (-230) = -18.4</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">(u(u<sup>2</sup> - 1))/(6) {&Delta;<sup>3</sup> y<sub>-2</sub> + &Delta;<sup>3</sup> y<sub>-1</sub>}{2} = (-0.4(0.16 - 1))/(6) &times; (-59 - 90)/(2) = (0.336)/(6) &times; (-74.5) = -4.172</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">(u<sup>2</sup>(u<sup>2</sup> - 1))/(24)&Delta;<sup>4</sup> y<sub>-2</sub> = (0.16(-0.84))/(24) &times; (-31) = +0.174</span><br>\n        Summing terms:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">f(28) = 47236 + 478.0 - 18.4 - 4.172 + 0.174 = <b>47691.6</b> &approx; <b>47692</b></div>\n        <strong>Answer:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">f(28) &approx; 47692</span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"sec-title\" data-sec=\"sec-b\">Section – B (Short Answer Questions &bull; 7.5 Marks &bull; 9 Questions)</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021, 2019, 2018 &bull; Repeated 3&times;</span>\n        Q1. Solve by Gauss elimination: 8x - 3y + 2z = 23, 4x + 11y - z = 33, 6x + 3y + 12z = 36.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Augmented matrix:\n        <span class=\"math-expr\"><table class=\"grid-table\"><tr><td>8</td><td>-3</td><td>2</td><td>|</td><td>23</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>11</td><td>-1</td><td>|</td><td>33</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>3</td><td>12</td><td>|</td><td>36</td></tr></table></span><br>\n        Divide row 3 by 3: <span class=\"math-expr\"><table class=\"grid-table\"><tr><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>4</td><td>|</td><td>12</td></tr></table></span>. Interchange <span class=\"math-expr\">R<sub>1</sub> &harr; R<sub>3</sub></span>:<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\"><table class=\"grid-table\"><tr><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>4</td><td>|</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>11</td><td>-1</td><td>|</td><td>33</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>-3</td><td>2</td><td>|</td><td>23</td></tr></table></span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">R<sub>2</sub> &rarr; R<sub>2</sub> - 2R<sub>1</sub></span>: <span class=\"math-expr\">[0, 9, -9 | 9] &rArr; y - z = 1</span>.<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">R<sub>3</sub> &rarr; R<sub>3</sub> - 4R<sub>1</sub></span>: <span class=\"math-expr\">[0, -7, -14 | -25]</span>.<br>\n        Substitute <span class=\"math-expr\">y = 1 + z</span> into row 3:\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">-7(1 + z) - 14z = -25 &rArr; -7 - 21z = -25 &rArr; -21z = -18 &rArr; <b>z = (6)/(7) &approx; 0.857</b></span>.<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\"><b>y = 1 + (6)/(7) = (13)/(7) &approx; 1.857</b></span>.<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">2x + y + 4z = 12 &rArr; 2x = 12 - (13)/(7) - (24)/(7) = 12 - (37)/(7) = (47)/(7) &rArr; <b>x = (47)/(14) &approx; 3.357</b></span>.<br>\n        <strong>Answer:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">x &approx; 3.357,   y &approx; 1.857,   z &approx; 0.857</span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2022 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q2. Find the missing terms in the following table:<br>\n    x: 0, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25<br>\n    y: 1, 3, y₂, 73, 225, y₅\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> We are given 4 known values of <span class=\"math-expr\">y</span> (<span class=\"math-expr\">y<sub>0</sub>=1, y<sub>1</sub>=3, y<sub>3</sub>=73, y<sub>4</sub>=225</span>) out of 6 points. Therefore, the function can be approximated by a polynomial of degree 3, which implies fourth differences vanish:<br>\n        <div class=\"math-block\">&Delta;<sup>4</sup> y<sub>0</sub> = 0 &nbsp;&nbsp; and &nbsp;&nbsp; &Delta;<sup>4</sup> y<sub>1</sub> = 0</div>\n        Expressing in terms of shift operator <span class=\"math-expr\">E = 1 + &Delta;</span>:<br>\n        <div class=\"math-block\">(E - 1)<sup>4</sup> y<sub>0</sub> = y<sub>4</sub> - 4y<sub>3</sub> + 6y<sub>2</sub> - 4y<sub>1</sub> + y<sub>0</sub> = 0</div>\n        Substitute known values:\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">225 - 4(73) + 6y<sub>2</sub> - 4(3) + 1 = 0 &rArr; 225 - 292 + 6y<sub>2</sub> - 12 + 1 = 0</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">6y<sub>2</sub> - 78 = 0 &rArr; <b>y<sub>2</sub> = 13</b></span>.<br><br>\n        Next, for <span class=\"math-expr\">y<sub>5</sub></span>:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">(E - 1)<sup>4</sup> y<sub>1</sub> = y<sub>5</sub> - 4y<sub>4</sub> + 6y<sub>3</sub> - 4y<sub>2</sub> + y<sub>1</sub> = 0</div>\n        Substitute:\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">y<sub>5</sub> - 4(225) + 6(73) - 4(13) + 3 = 0</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">y<sub>5</sub> - 900 + 438 - 52 + 3 = 0 &rArr; y<sub>5</sub> - 511 = 0 &rArr; <b>y<sub>5</sub> = 511</b></span>.<br>\n        <strong>Answer:</strong> The missing terms are <span class=\"math-expr\"><b>y<sub>2</sub> = 13</b></span> and <span class=\"math-expr\"><b>y<sub>5</sub> = 511</b></span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023, 2019 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q3. Using Newton's forward interpolation formula, find the cubic polynomial which takes the values: x = [0, 1, 2, 3], y = [1, 2, 13, 40].\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Here <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>0</sub> = 0, h = 1</span>. Form the forward difference table:\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <thead><tr><th>x</th><th>y</th><th>&Delta;y</th><th>&Delta;&sup2;y</th><th>&Delta;&sup3;y</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n                <tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>10</td><td>6</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>11</td><td>16</td><td>-</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>2</td><td>13</td><td>27</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>3</td><td>40</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n        </table>\n        Here <span class=\"math-expr\">u = (x - 0)/(1) = x</span>.<br>\n        Newton's forward interpolation formula:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">y(x) = y<sub>0</sub> + u &Delta; y<sub>0</sub> + (u(u-1))/(2!) &Delta;<sup>2</sup> y<sub>0</sub> + (u(u-1)(u-2))/(3!) &Delta;<sup>3</sup> y<sub>0</sub></div>\n        Substitute values:\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">y(x) = 1 + x(1) + (x(x-1))/(2)(10) + (x(x-1)(x-2))/(6)(6)</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">= 1 + x + 5(x<sup>2</sup> - x) + (x<sup>3</sup> - 3x<sup>2</sup> + 2x)</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">= 1 + x + 5x<sup>2</sup> - 5x + x<sup>3</sup> - 3x<sup>2</sup> + 2x</span><br>\n        <div class=\"math-block\"><b>y(x) = x<sup>3</sup> + 2x<sup>2</sup> - 2x + 1</b></div>\n        <em>Verification:</em> <span class=\"math-expr\">y(0)=1</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">y(1)=1+2-2+1=2</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">y(2)=8+8-4+1=13</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">y(3)=27+18-6+1=40</span>. (Exact!)\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021, 2020 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q4. Use Euler's Method with h = 0.1 to find the solution of dy/dx = x + y², y(0) = 0 in range 0 &le; x &le; 0.5.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M / 7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">y<sub>n+1</sub> = y<sub>n</sub> + h f(x<sub>n</sub>, y<sub>n</sub>) = y<sub>n</sub> + 0.1(x<sub>n</sub> + y<sub>n</sub><sup>2</sup>)</span>.<br>\n        1. <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>0</sub> = 0.0, y<sub>0</sub> = 0 &rArr; y<sub>1</sub> = 0 + 0.1(0) = <b>0.0000</b></span> at <span class=\"math-expr\">x=0.1</span>.<br>\n        2. <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>1</sub> = 0.1, y<sub>1</sub> = 0 &rArr; y<sub>2</sub> = 0 + 0.1(0.1 + 0) = <b>0.0100</b></span> at <span class=\"math-expr\">x=0.2</span>.<br>\n        3. <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>2</sub> = 0.2, y<sub>2</sub> = 0.01 &rArr; y<sub>3</sub> = 0.01 + 0.1(0.2 + 0.0001) = <b>0.0300</b></span> at <span class=\"math-expr\">x=0.3</span>.<br>\n        4. <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>3</sub> = 0.3, y<sub>3</sub> = 0.03 &rArr; y<sub>4</sub> = 0.03 + 0.1(0.3 + 0.0009) = <b>0.0601</b></span> at <span class=\"math-expr\">x=0.4</span>.<br>\n        5. <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>4</sub> = 0.4, y<sub>4</sub> = 0.0601 &rArr; y<sub>5</sub> = 0.0601 + 0.1(0.4 + 0.0036) = <b>0.1005</b></span> at <span class=\"math-expr\">x=0.5</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Answer:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">y(0.1)=0, y(0.2)=0.01, y(0.3)=0.03, y(0.4)=0.0601, y(0.5)=0.1005</span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2021</span>\n        Q5. Find the form of the function from following given data: x = [1, 2, 3, 4], f(x) = [3, 6, 11, 18].\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Step <span class=\"math-expr\">h = 1, x<sub>0</sub> = 1</span>. Form difference table:\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <thead><tr><th>x</th><th>f(x)</th><th>&Delta;f</th><th>&Delta;&sup2;f</th><th>&Delta;&sup3;f</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n                <tr><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>0</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>2</td><td>6</td><td>5</td><td>2</td><td>-</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>3</td><td>11</td><td>7</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>4</td><td>18</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n        </table>\n        Here <span class=\"math-expr\">&Delta;<sup>2</sup> f = 2</span> is constant, and <span class=\"math-expr\">&Delta;<sup>3</sup> f = 0</span>. Let <span class=\"math-expr\">u = x - 1</span>:<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">f(x) = f<sub>0</sub> + u &Delta; f<sub>0</sub> + (u(u-1))/(2) &Delta;<sup>2</sup> f<sub>0</sub> = 3 + (x-1)(3) + ((x-1)(x-2))/(2)(2)</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">= 3 + 3x - 3 + (x<sup>2</sup> - 3x + 2) = <b>x<sup>2</sup> + 2</b></span>.<br>\n        <strong>Answer:</strong> The function is <span class=\"math-expr\"><b>f(x) = x<sup>2</sup> + 2</b></span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q6. Find backward differences for the points: (5, 11), (6, 13), (9, 28), (11, 40).\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><b>Given Data Points:</b> (5, 11), (6, 13), (9, 28), (11, 40).</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><i>Note:</i> The argument values <i>x</i> = 5, 6, 9, 11 are unequally spaced (intervals h = 1, 3, 2). For unequally spaced arguments, Divided Differences or standard finite differences can be computed.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Difference Table Construction:</p>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n    <tr><th>x</th><th>y = f(x)</th><th>First Difference (&Delta;y)</th><th>Second Difference (&Delta;<sup>2</sup>y)</th><th>Third Difference (&Delta;<sup>3</sup>y)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>5</td><td>11</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>6</td><td>13</td><td>13 - 11 = <b>2</b></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>9</td><td>28</td><td>28 - 13 = <b>15</b></td><td>15 - 2 = <b>13</b></td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>11</td><td>40</td><td>40 - 28 = <b>12</b></td><td>12 - 15 = <b>-3</b></td><td>-3 - 13 = <b>-16</b></td></tr>\n</table>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p><b>Backward Differences at the final point (x = 11, y<sub>3</sub> = 40):</b></p>\n    <ul>\n        <li>First backward difference: <b>&nabla;y<sub>3</sub> = 12</b></li>\n        <li>Second backward difference: <b>&nabla;<sup>2</sup>y<sub>3</sub> = -3</b></li>\n        <li>Third backward difference: <b>&nabla;<sup>3</sup>y<sub>3</sub> = -16</b></li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q7. Using Bessel's formula, find y(25) from: x = [20, 24, 28, 32], y = [24, 32, 35, 40].\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">h = 4</span>. Choose origin <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>0</sub> = 24</span>. Then <span class=\"math-expr\">u = (25 - 24)/(4) = 0.25</span>.<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">y<sub>-1</sub> = 24, y<sub>0</sub> = 32, y<sub>1</sub> = 35, y<sub>2</sub> = 40</span>.<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">&Delta; y<sub>0</sub> = 35 - 32 = 3</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">&Delta; y<sub>-1</sub> = 32 - 24 = 8</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">&Delta; y<sub>1</sub> = 40 - 35 = 5</span>.<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">&Delta;<sup>2</sup> y<sub>-1</sub> = 3 - 8 = -5</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">&Delta;<sup>2</sup> y<sub>0</sub> = 5 - 3 = 2</span>.<br>\n        Bessel's interpolation formula:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">y = (y<sub>0</sub> + y<sub>1</sub>)/(2) + (u - (1)/(2))&Delta; y<sub>0</sub> + (u(u-1))/(4)(&Delta;<sup>2</sup> y<sub>-1</sub> + &Delta;<sup>2</sup> y<sub>0</sub>)</div>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">(32 + 35)/(2) + (0.25 - 0.5)(3) + (0.25(-0.75))/(4)(-5 + 2)</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">= 33.5 - 0.75 + (-0.1875)/(4)(-3) = 32.75 + 0.1406 = <b>32.89</b></span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2018</span>\n        Q8. Given f(0) = 16.35, f(5) = 14.88, f(10) = 13.59, f(15) = 12.46. Find x when f(x) = 14.00 by Inverse Interpolation.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Using <strong>Lagrange's Inverse Interpolation Formula</strong> where <span class=\"math-expr\">y</span> is independent and <span class=\"math-expr\">x</span> is dependent:\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <tr><th>i</th><th>x<sub>i</sub></th><th>y<sub>i</sub> = f(x<sub>i</sub>)</th><th>y - y<sub>i</sub> (for y = 14.00)</th></tr>\n            <tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>16.35</td><td>-2.35</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>1</td><td>5</td><td>14.88</td><td>-0.88</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>2</td><td>10</td><td>13.59</td><td>+0.41</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>3</td><td>15</td><td>12.46</td><td>+1.54</td></tr>\n        </table>\n        Lagrange inverse interpolation: <span class=\"math-expr\">x = &Sigma;<sub>i=0</sub><sup>3</sup> x<sub>i</sub> &prod;<sub>j &ne; i</sub> (y - y<sub>j</sub>)/(y<sub>i</sub> - y<sub>j</sub>)</span>.<br>\n        Term for <span class=\"math-expr\">i=0</span> is 0 since <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>0</sub> = 0</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Term 1 (i=1):</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">5 &times; ((14-16.35)(14-13.59)(14-12.46))/((14.88-16.35)(14.88-13.59)(14.88-12.46)) = 5 &times; ((-2.35)(0.41)(1.54))/((-1.47)(1.29)(2.42)) = 5 &times; (-1.4838)/(-4.5888) = 5 &times; 0.3233 = 1.6166</span><br>\n        <strong>Term 2 (i=2):</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">10 &times; ((14-16.35)(14-14.88)(14-12.46))/((13.59-16.35)(13.59-14.88)(13.59-12.46)) = 10 &times; ((-2.35)(-0.88)(1.54))/((-2.76)(-1.29)(1.13)) = 10 &times; (3.1847)/(4.0233) = 10 &times; 0.7916 = 7.9156</span><br>\n        <strong>Term 3 (i=3):</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">15 &times; ((14-16.35)(14-14.88)(14-13.59))/((12.46-16.35)(12.46-14.88)(12.46-13.59)) = 15 &times; ((-2.35)(-0.88)(0.41))/((-3.89)(-2.42)(-1.13)) = 15 &times; (0.8479)/(-10.637) = -1.1957</span><br>\n        Summing terms: <span class=\"math-expr\">x = 0 + 1.6166 + 7.9156 - 1.1957 = <b>8.3365</b></span>.<br>\n        <strong>Answer:</strong> For <span class=\"math-expr\">f(x) = 14.00</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">x &approx; 8.34</span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-b\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2018</span>\n        Q9. Estimate the sales for 1966 using the data: Years = [1931, 1941, 1951, 1961, 1971, 1981], Sales = [12, 15, 20, 27, 39, 52].\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[7.5M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Equidistant data with <span class=\"math-expr\">h = 10</span> years. We estimate for <span class=\"math-expr\">x = 1966</span>.<br>\n        Take origin <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>0</sub> = 1961</span> (center). Then <span class=\"math-expr\">u = (1966 - 1961)/(10) = 0.5</span>.<br>\n        Difference table for sales <span class=\"math-expr\">y</span>:\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <tr><th>Year</th><th>Sales (y)</th><th>Δy</th><th>Δ²y</th><th>Δ³y</th><th>Δ⁴y</th></tr>\n            <tr><td>1931</td><td>12</td><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>0</td><td>3</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>1941</td><td>15</td><td>5</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>-2</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>1951</td><td>20</td><td>7</td><td>5</td><td>1</td><td></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>1961</td><td>27</td><td>12</td><td>6</td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>1971</td><td>39</td><td>13</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>1981</td><td>52</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n        </table>\n        Using <strong>Newton's Forward Interpolation</strong> from <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>0</sub> = 1961, y<sub>0</sub> = 27</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">u = 0.5</span>:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">y(1966) = y<sub>0</sub> + u&Delta; y<sub>0</sub> + (u(u-1))/(2)&Delta;<sup>2</sup> y<sub>0</sub> = 27 + (0.5)(12) + (0.5(-0.5))/(2)(1) = 27 + 6 - 0.125 = <b>32.875</b>  thousand</div>\n        <strong>Estimated Sales for 1966:</strong> Approximately <strong>32,875 units</strong>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"sec-title\" data-sec=\"sec-a\">Section – A (Very Short Answer Questions &bull; 3 Marks &bull; 8 Questions)</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2024, 2022 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q1. Define backward differences. Make backward difference table for the ordered pairs: (x₀, y₀), (x₁, y₁), (x₂, y₂), (x₃, y₃), (x₄, y₄).\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> Let <span class=\"math-expr\">y = f(x)</span> be tabulated at equidistant points <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>0</sub>, x<sub>1</sub>, &hellip;, x<sub>n</sub></span> with step <span class=\"math-expr\">h</span>. The first backward difference operator <span class=\"math-expr\">&nabla;</span> is defined as:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">&nabla; y<sub>k</sub> = y<sub>k</sub> - y<sub>k-1</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp; (k = 1, 2, &hellip;, n)</div>\n        Higher-order backward differences are given recursively by <span class=\"math-expr\">&nabla;<sup>r</sup> y<sub>k</sub> = &nabla;<sup>r-1</sup> y<sub>k</sub> - &nabla;<sup>r-1</sup> y<sub>k-1</sub></span>.<br>\n        <strong>Backward Difference Table:</strong>\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <thead>\n                <tr><th>x</th><th>y</th><th>&nabla;y</th><th>&nabla;&sup2;y</th><th>&nabla;&sup3;y</th><th>&nabla;&sup4;y</th></tr>\n            </thead>\n            <tbody>\n                <tr><td>x₀</td><td>y₀</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>x₁</td><td>y₁</td><td>&nabla;y₁ = y₁ - y₀</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>x₂</td><td>y₂</td><td>&nabla;y₂ = y₂ - y₁</td><td>&nabla;&sup2;y₂ = &nabla;y₂ - &nabla;y₁</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>x₃</td><td>y₃</td><td>&nabla;y₃ = y₃ - y₂</td><td>&nabla;&sup2;y₃ = &nabla;y₃ - &nabla;y₂</td><td>&nabla;&sup3;y₃ = &nabla;&sup2;y₃ - &nabla;&sup2;y₂</td><td>-</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>x₄</td><td>y₄</td><td>&nabla;y₄ = y₄ - y₃</td><td>&nabla;&sup2;y₄ = &nabla;y₄ - &nabla;y₃</td><td>&nabla;&sup3;y₄ = &nabla;&sup2;y₄ - &nabla;&sup2;y₃</td><td>&nabla;&sup4;y₄ = &nabla;&sup3;y₄ - &nabla;&sup3;y₃</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n        </table>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023, 2018 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q2. Find √12 by applying Newton-Raphson's method.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Let <span class=\"math-expr\">x = √12 ⟹ x² - 12 = 0</span>.<br>\n        Define <span class=\"math-expr\">f(x) = x² - 12</span> and <span class=\"math-expr\">f'(x) = 2x</span>.<br>\n        Newton-Raphson iterative formula:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">x<sub>n+1</sub> = x<sub>n</sub> - (f(x<sub>n</sub>))/(f'(x<sub>n</sub>)) = x<sub>n</sub> - (x<sub>n</sub><sup>2</sup> - 12)/(2x<sub>n</sub>) = (1)/(2)(x<sub>n</sub> + (12)/(x<sub>n</sub>))</div>\n        Since <span class=\"math-expr\">3² = 9 < 12 < 4² = 16</span>, take initial guess <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>0</sub> = 3.5</span>.<br>\n        <strong>Iteration 1:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>1</sub> = 0.5 × (3.5 + 12/3.5) = 0.5 × (3.5 + 3.42857) = 3.46429</span><br>\n        <strong>Iteration 2:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>2</sub> = 0.5 × (3.46429 + 12/3.46429) = 0.5 × (3.46429 + 3.46391) = 3.46410</span><br>\n        <strong>Iteration 3:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>3</sub> = 0.5 × (3.46410 + 12/3.46410) = 3.46410</span><br>\n        <strong>Final Answer:</strong> <span class=\"math-expr\">√12 ≈ 3.46410</span> (correct to 5 decimal places).\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020, 2019 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q3. Define operators E, &Delta; and &nabla;. Also obtain relation between E and &Delta;.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        \n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>In the Calculus of Finite Differences, the fundamental operators are defined as follows:</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <ol>\n        <li><b>Shift Operator (E):</b> Increases the argument by step interval <i>h</i>:\n            <br><code>E f(x) = f(x + h)</code>\n        </li>\n        <li><b>Forward Difference Operator (&Delta;):</b> Computes the difference between next value and current value:\n            <br><code>&Delta; f(x) = f(x + h) - f(x)</code>\n        </li>\n        <li><b>Backward Difference Operator (&nabla;):</b> Computes difference between current value and previous value:\n            <br><code>&nabla; f(x) = f(x) - f(x - h)</code>\n        </li>\n    </ol>\n</div>\n<p class=\"step-label\">Derivation of Relation between E and &Delta;:</p>\n<div class=\"info-box\">\n    <p>By definition of Forward Difference: <code>&Delta; f(x) = f(x + h) - f(x)</code></p>\n    <p>Since <code>E f(x) = f(x + h)</code>, we can substitute:</p>\n    <p><code>&Delta; f(x) = E f(x) - f(x) = (E - 1) f(x)</code></p>\n    <p>Factoring out the operand <code>f(x)</code>, we obtain the fundamental operational identity:</p>\n    <p><b>&Delta; = E - 1</b> &nbsp; or &nbsp; <b>E = 1 + &Delta; = &Delta; + 1</b> &check;</p>\n</div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2019, 2018 &bull; Repeated 2&times;</span>\n        Q4. Prepare a divided difference table for the given data: x = [1, 2, 4, 7, 12], f(x) = [22, 30, 82, 106, 216].\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Divided differences are calculated as <span class=\"math-expr\">f[x<sub>i</sub>, x<sub>i+1</sub>] = {f(x<sub>i+1</sub>) - f(x<sub>i</sub>)}{x<sub>i+1</sub> - x<sub>i</sub>}</span>:\n        <table class=\"grid-table\">\n            <tr><th>x</th><th>f(x)</th><th>1st Div. Diff</th><th>2nd Div. Diff</th><th>3rd Div. Diff</th><th>4th Div. Diff</th></tr>\n            <tr><td>1</td><td>22</td><td>(30-22)/(2-1) = <strong>8</strong></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>2</td><td>30</td><td>(82-30)/(4-2) = <strong>26</strong></td><td>(26-8)/(4-1) = <strong>6</strong></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>4</td><td>82</td><td>(106-82)/(7-4) = <strong>8</strong></td><td>(8-26)/(7-2) = <strong>-3.6</strong></td><td>(-3.6 - 6)/(7-1) = <strong>-1.6</strong></td><td></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>7</td><td>106</td><td>(216-106)/(12-7) = <strong>22</strong></td><td>(22-8)/(12-4) = <strong>1.75</strong></td><td>(1.75 - (-3.6))/(12-2) = <strong>0.535</strong></td><td>(0.535 - (-1.6))/(12-1) = <strong>0.194</strong></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>12</td><td>216</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n        </table>\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023</span>\n        Q5. Prove that E &nabla; = &nabla; E = &Delta;, where E is shift operator, &nabla; is backward difference operator, and &Delta; is forward difference operator.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Proof:</strong><br>\n        1. <em>Part 1: Prove <span class=\"math-expr\">E &nabla; = &Delta;</span></em><br>\n        By definition, <span class=\"math-expr\">&nabla; f(x) = f(x) - f(x-h) = (1 - E<sup>-1</sup>) f(x) &rArr; &nabla; = 1 - E<sup>-1</sup></span>.<br>\n        Therefore,\n        <div class=\"math-block\">E &nabla; = E (1 - E<sup>-1</sup>) = E - E &bull; E<sup>-1</sup> = E - 1</div>\n        Since forward difference operator is <span class=\"math-expr\">&Delta; = E - 1</span>, we obtain:\n        <div class=\"math-block\"><b>E &nabla; = &Delta;</b></div>\n        2. <em>Part 2: Prove <span class=\"math-expr\">&nabla; E = &Delta;</span></em><br>\n        <div class=\"math-block\">&nabla; E = (1 - E<sup>-1</sup>) E = E - E<sup>-1</sup> E = E - 1 = <b>&Delta;</b></div>\n        Hence, <span class=\"math-expr\"><b>E &nabla; = &nabla; E = &Delta;</b></span>. (Hence Proved).\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2023</span>\n        Q6. Find the first derivative of the function at x = 1.2 given: x = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], f(x) = [0, 1, 5, 14, 30, 55].\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> The table corresponds to <span class=\"math-expr\">f(n) = ((n-1)n(2n-1))/(6)</span> with step <span class=\"math-expr\">h = 1</span>.<br>\n        Differences at <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>0</sub> = 1</span>:<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">&Delta; f<sub>0</sub> = 1 - 0 = 1</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">&Delta;<sup>2</sup> f<sub>0</sub> = 4 - 1 = 3</span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">&Delta;<sup>3</sup> f<sub>0</sub> = 5 - 4 = 1</span>.<br>\n        Here <span class=\"math-expr\">x = 1.2 &rArr; u = (1.2 - 1)/(1) = 0.2</span>.<br>\n        Newton's forward difference derivative formula:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">f'(x) = (1)/(h) [ &Delta; f<sub>0</sub> + (2u - 1)/(2)&Delta;<sup>2</sup> f<sub>0</sub> + (3u<sup>2</sup> - 6u + 2)/(6)&Delta;<sup>3</sup> f<sub>0</sub> ]</div>\n        Substitute <span class=\"math-expr\">u = 0.2</span>:\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">f'(1.2) = 1 + (2(0.2) - 1)/(2)(3) + (3(0.04) - 6(0.2) + 2)/(6)(1)</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">= 1 + (-0.6)/(2)(3) + (0.12 - 1.2 + 2)/(6) = 1 - 0.90 + (0.92)/(6) = 0.10 + 0.1533 = <b>0.2533</b></span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2020</span>\n        Q7. Define Simpson's three-eighths rule for Numerical Integration.\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Definition:</strong> Simpson's 3/8 rule approximates the integral of <span class=\"math-expr\">f(x)</span> over <span class=\"math-expr\">[a, b]</span> divided into <span class=\"math-expr\">n</span> subintervals where <span class=\"math-expr\">n</span> is a multiple of 3 by fitting cubic polynomials across triplets of intervals:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">&int;<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup> f(x)   dx &approx; (3h)/(8) [ (y<sub>0</sub> + y<sub>n</sub>) + 3&Sigma;<sub>i &ne; 3k</sub> y<sub>i</sub> + 2&Sigma;<sub>i = 3k</sub> y<sub>i</sub> ]</div>\n        The truncation error is of order <span class=\"math-expr\">O(h<sup>4</sup>)</span>, specifically <span class=\"math-expr\">E = -(3)/(80) h<sup>5</sup> f<sup>(4)</sup>(&xi;)</span>.\n    </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"q-box\" data-section=\"sec-a\">\n    <div class=\"q-title\">\n        <span class=\"badge\">CCSU: 2018</span>\n        Q8. Find the first derivative of f(x) at x = 0.4 from the table: x = [0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4], y = [1.1051, 1.2214, 1.3498, 1.4918].\n        <span class=\"q-marks\">[3M]</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ans-body\">\n        <strong>Solution:</strong> Since <span class=\"math-expr\">x = 0.4</span> is the last point in the equidistant table (<span class=\"math-expr\">h = 0.1</span>), we use <strong>Newton's Backward Difference formula</strong> for derivative:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">f'(x<sub>n</sub>) = (1)/(h) [ &nabla; y<sub>n</sub> + (1)/(2)&nabla;<sup>2</sup> y<sub>n</sub> + (1)/(3)&nabla;<sup>3</sup> y<sub>n</sub> ]</div>\n        Backward differences at <span class=\"math-expr\">x<sub>n</sub> = 0.4</span>:<br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">∇y<sub>3</sub> = 1.4918 - 1.3498 = 0.1420</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">∇y<sub>2</sub> = 1.3498 - 1.2214 = 0.1284 ⟹ ∇²y<sub>3</sub> = 0.1420 - 0.1284 = 0.0136</span><br>\n        <span class=\"math-expr\">∇y<sub>1</sub> = 1.2214 - 1.1051 = 0.1163 ⟹ ∇²y<sub>2</sub> = 0.1284 - 0.1163 = 0.0121 ⟹ ∇³y<sub>3</sub> = 0.0136 - 0.0121 = 0.0015</span><br>\n        Substitute into formula:\n        <div class=\"math-block\">f'(0.4) = (1)/(0.1) [ 0.1420 + (1)/(2)(0.0136) + (1)/(3)(0.0015) ] = 10 &times; [0.1420 + 0.0068 + 0.0005] = 10 &times; 0.1493 = <b>1.4930</b></div>\n        <em>Verification:</em> Since <span class=\"math-expr\">y &approx; e<sup>x</sup></span>, <span class=\"math-expr\">e<sup>0.4</sup> = 1.4918</span>, exactly matching theory.\n    </div>\n</div>\n", "counts": {"total": 33, "sec_c": 16, "sec_b": 9, "sec_a": 8}}};
